{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/CENSUS.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,3} '\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/rejected-answers.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md; cat docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/DIAGNOSIS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Pilot pair 1 result (D711)\n\n**Registered verdict: inconclusive.** control-1 (poc-v118) stopped on 2026-09-08 on a double refusal (PAID-DESIGN.md §4 precedence\n(2), §8). linked-1 (poc-v120) completed and passed every acceptance check, so precedence (1) does not apply. The comparison is closed:\nno redraw, no resume (council round 37, consensus). Spend: $1.70 of the $2.50 ceiling (`poc/data/link-pilot-2026-11-21/ledger.json`).\n\n## What stopped the control\n\n- v21 (\"How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\") on 09-08. Its request listed 29 evidence ids.\n  - Call 8938 cited `e362` for `e3620`.\n  - The retry, call 8941, cited `e3111`, one character from `e3110` and `e3112`.\n  - Both are copy errors of a listed id. The forecast is the same in both (about $407.1B, 88.5% growth).\n- control-1 equals old-2 on every table H2 checks through 09-07, the last completed day: 984 non-reader judgements, 4,196 evidence\n  rows and 5,223 links. The summary's H2 `false` comes only from the days missing after the stop.\n- Only readers were bought: 201 attempts.\n\n## Exploratory findings\n\nThese come from one pair and one draw. They are not the registered blind reading.\n\n| Line | linked-1 | control-1 | How it was read |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P: Data Center above total revenue, 07-10..09-07 | parser 3 of 60; **0 real** | parser 28 of 60; the 5 sampled are real | Codex read all 12 linked dates the parser flagged across the window; Claude read 4 of them and 5 random control dates |\n| H4: CPU double count in total revenue | **4 answers** (07-15, 07-16, 07-17, 07-18; in force 07-15..07-19) | no CPU link | Claude read all 71 post-link v14 answers; Codex did a shuffled second read with dates hidden (exploratory, because the list was in the brief); both lists agree |\n| H1: reader refusals on the 200 matched reader-days | 5 | 1 | an excess of 4, at the harm line |\n\n### P\n- In linked-1, Data Center is computed as a share of v14's total. Example from 07-22: \"About $352 billion: v14's approximately $387.3\n  billion … multiplied by 91%\".\n- Linked-1's parser hits are artefacts: the parser took the total's amount as the Data Center figure.\n- The safe claim is that the link kept the two forecasts consistent in the readings checked. Removal of the class is not shown.\n\n### H4\n- Each double-counting answer adds $19–19.5B to a total of about $387B built from company guidance. That addition lifts the total\n  above $400B.\n- On 07-20 the judge reversed it itself (\"included within, not added to\").\n- The link behind it, v14 reads v8 (\"stand-alone CPU sales\"), had been flagged in D705 as running against the tie-break's direction.\n- **Correction (2026-11-22, D721):** these are four unsupported increments with the overlap unresolved, not proved double counts.\n  The frozen gold (q01) lists the Vera CPU target among the drivers above consensus. The paired control reached about $417B\n  without any CPU addition on 07-16..07-18, so only 07-15 differs in rung.\n\n### H1\n- A resumed control would make (v21, 09-08) a matched day and add the control's two refusals. That lowers the excess to 2 or 3.\n\n### Refused answers\n- Every refused judge answer is an id-membership error: an id outside the list its field allows. Count: 17 in 5,408 distinct\n  archived attempts (old-1, old-2, old-3, and the fresh calls of both pilot arms).\n- All 5,389 committed answers fit those lists.\n- 6 of linked-1's 7 fresh reader refusals mix up which list an id belongs to:\n  - the dependency `v14` in evidence citations, twice;\n  - theme ids in `cites_views` beside a valid dependency, four times.\n- The closing instruction of every request already forbids theme ids in `cites_views` (`judging.py` lines 180–182). The answer format\n  accepts any string in these fields.\n\n## Council round 37 (`round37-brief.md`, `codex-r37.md`, `claude-r37.md`, `round37b-brief.md`, `codex-r37b.md`)\n\n- **Consensus: A.**\n  - C (resume control-1 from 09-08) is exploratory at most. The stop and the control's lead were seen before the rule change was\n    proposed, and a resume would move H1 across its line through the very refusals that invalidated the control.\n  - B (a redraw) selects a replacement after seeing the stop and repeats the risk.\n  - Claude first proposed C; it withdrew C once linked-1's H1 numbers showed the effect.\n- **A resume-once rule** for a double refusal may be used only in a future comparison registered before its send. It applies to both\n  arms, keeps the original refusals in the matched-day count, and is proven on stored data first (the runner overwrites a resumed\n  day's call range today).\n\n## Recommendation to Alex (consensus)\n\nOpen a judge version that changes the citation format only:\n- **The change:** every id field of the answer (cites, push ids, `cites_views`, theme pushes) gets a closed list built from the exact\n  request. The judge still chooses which evidence matters; the code fixes which ids each field can hold.\n- **The test, at $0, from request generation through answer checking:**\n  - every stored accepted answer fits its lists;\n  - every observed bad id is excluded;\n  - a field with no allowed id accepts only an empty list.\n- **Limits:**\n  - Whether the judge picks the intended source is a separate measurement.\n  - Changing the format changes the archive key of every request, so any paired run needs a new replay proof first.\n  - Used in both arms, it tests what links add to that judge; whether the linked product should replace frozen v118 stays open.\n\nThe link direction (a whole reading its own part) is a separate design, not part of this version:\n- The typed keys label the stand-alone CPU question `other`, so a key-based part-and-whole rule would miss it.\n- Whether a part's forecast may inform a whole is a judgement. A total built from non-overlapping parts is legitimate.\n- D704 allowed both directions on purpose.\n# A part added to a whole that already holds it: diagnosis (D720, $0)\n\n> **Superseded in part (D721, council round 43b).** The frozen judgement gold (q01) lists the Vera CPU target among the drivers\n> above consensus, so §1's premise that the basis \"already contains it\" is Claude's view, not the ruler's, and §4's edits have no\n> source. old-1 has 26 explicit additions, not 32 (`codex-r43.md`). The design is recommended closed with nothing built; the\n> surviving findings are listed in D721.\n\nAlex opened the design of the double-count fix (D720). In the linking pilot, the total-revenue judge added the stand-alone CPU\nforecast it read through a link to a total that already contained it (D711, H4). AGENTS §1 asks for the whole class before a design.\nThis file is that diagnosis. Nothing is built or sent.\n\n## 1. The class, counted on stored data\n\n**Class:** a judgement of a quantity that contains others (a company's total revenue) builds its figure from a company-wide basis\n(consensus, or guidance carried forward) and then adds all or part of a component's forecast (here NVIDIA's stand-alone CPU target)\nas if the basis did not already contain it.\n\n**Population:** every distinct judgement of a total-revenue, total-revenue-growth, Data Center or total-CPU question in the five\ncomparable stores: old-1, old-2 and old-3 (three draws of poc-v118 over 07-01..09-20), and the fresh reader rows of the two pilot arms\n(control-1 poc-v118, linked-1 poc-v120). Pilot rows copied from old-2 are excluded. Total: 1,354 judgements.\n\n**Listing:** `census_candidates.py` lists the 746 of them that name a component (CPU, Vera, Grace, Rubin, networking). Claude read:\n- all 209 whose implied outcome names a component together with an arithmetic or inclusion word;\n- for the instrument check, the reasons of every other total-revenue candidate that puts an addition word within 80 characters of\n  \"CPU\" or \"stand-alone\" (76 answers). That check found 6 more old-1 answers (07-22, 07-23, 07-24, 08-15) and the old-3 mentions.\n\n| Store | Total-revenue answers after the CPU target was in view | Target added into the figure | Rung decided by it (own arithmetic) | Mentioned as upside, not in the figure |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| old-1 (v4) | 77 (from 07-05) | **32** (07-07..08-25) | **4** certain (07-15, 07-16, 07-17, 08-21), 11 possible | 0 |\n| old-2 (v14) | 0 (the target was filed under the stand-alone CPU question v8) | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| old-3 (v3) | 77 (from 07-05) | 0 | 0 | 2 (07-11, 07-22) |\n| control-1 (v14) | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| linked-1 (v14, through the link to v8) | 71 (from 07-10) | **4** (07-15..07-18) | **4** (rung 3 to 2) | 1 (v21, 07-26) |\n| linked-1 (v9 reading v14) | 71 | 4, copied from v14's reasons (07-16..07-19) | 0 | 0 |\n\n- \"Certain\": the answer's own arithmetic puts the figure below the boundary before the addition and above it after. On old-1\n  07-15: \"$430 billion — just below rung 1 … allowing for partial contribution and possible consensus inclusion, a net few billion\n  dollars takes the implied result to roughly $435 billion, above the $431.2 billion boundary\". On 08-21: $404B plus $20B for CPU plus\n  $10B for SpaceX, giving $434B. Without the CPU it is $414B, rung 2.\n- \"Possible\": rung 1 within a few billion of $431.2B, with the CPU share not sized (07-30, 07-31, 08-06..08-12, 08-14, 08-15).\n- **Since when:** from the first day the component target is in the whole's view, as evidence filed under it or as a linked\n  dependency. Filing decides exposure. In old-1 and old-3 the target ($20B stand-alone CPU, e65) was filed under total revenue at seq\n  49, because no CPU question was open yet. In old-2 the stand-alone CPU question opened at seq 49 and took it.\n- **Not the class** (read, then set aside):\n  - **Total CPU = stand-alone Vera $20B + Grace above $5B trailing 12 months**, about 100 answers in all five stores from 08-26\n    (rung 1, above $25B). The sources name two different products: e2542 is \"Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 … exceeding $5 billion\",\n    and e897 is the CEO's \"The $20 billion is for standalone CPU\". Summing them is a defensible reading, not a basis that already\n    contains the part. Recorded as a boundary case; a reader could still question whether stand-alone Grace sales sit in both.\n  - **A quantified revision of one component applied to its share of the total** (SemiAnalysis's 20% second-half compute uplift,\n    e.g. old-1 07-10, old-3 08-12). The prompt asks for this: \"a quantified revision … shift[s] it\".\n\n## 2. The mechanism (one case end to end, then the request)\n\n- **The trace: linked-1 v14 on 07-15** (call 8756).\n  - The request shows the dependency as `v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU\n    sales in FY2027?`, its comparison point, and \"The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): About $19.5\n    billion\".\n  - Nothing in the request says whether that quantity is inside this one.\n  - The answer: \"Adding the dependency's roughly $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, which is distinct from the existing\n    revenue run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses into rung 2.\"\n  - The previous day's answer to the same request shape said the opposite: \"included within total revenue, not added on top\". So\n    did 07-20's (\"a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to this company-wide run-rate\").\n  - 07-16..07-18 repeat the addition, carried by the previous conclusion.\n- **The trace: old-1 v4 on 07-07** (no link). The CPU target (e65) is evidence under total revenue. The answer: \"the $20 billion\n  stand-alone CPU FY2027 forecast adds some upside but is heavily discounted because the planned fall launch leaves few selling\n  quarters and no volume in hand.\" That is the request's own product-target sentence, applied to a whole.\n- **The request sentences** (judging-v7, the frozen product judge; judging-v8 has the same text):\n\n| Failure | The request sentence | The standard on the case | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A component's target taken into a whole's figure | \"A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand.\" It is written for a question about that product, but nothing limits it, so on a total it reads as \"add a discounted share\". | D260: judge against the reference (consensus, guidance). A company-wide basis formed after the company published the target already contains it. | **keeps wider**: the sentence's scope is narrower than its wording |\n| No rule for a whole reading its part | \"check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole …)\"; \"a component cannot exceed its whole\". Both cover only the part's side. | PRD 06: one ladder per thing, dependencies connect them. Whether a part is inside a whole is identity of the quantities, not weight. | **missing** (one direction only) |\n| Sticky after one draw | \"The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\" | none | **not the prompt's class**: carry-over spreads any error until a judge reverses it, so it is not a fix for this class |\n\n## 3. Step back\n\n- **Product, not measurement:** the frozen product judge does it in one of three product draws (old-1), with its rung decided by\n  the addition on at least 4 days. The linking version only adds a second route in: the dependency block.\n- **Not a link-direction problem:** banning a whole from reading a part would leave old-1's route open. D702/D703 also rejected a\n  blanket ban; Alex's D703 option said \"no blanket ban on a whole reading its part\". And D711: the keys label stand-alone CPU\n  `other`, so code cannot tell part from whole.\n- **The frozen ruler does not see it:** the judgement gold reads 07-14. old-1's first decisive day is 07-15, and linked-1's is\n  07-15.\n- **Prior decisions searched** (\"double count\", \"already includ\", \"component\", \"its whole\"): none rules on a judge adding a part\n  to a whole. D191's shared quantity and D186's rungs against guidance do not cover it.\n\n## 4. Where a fix belongs, and its cheapest proof (for the council; nothing written yet)\n\n- **Owner:** the judge's text. It is the one place that decides how a figure is built, and both routes (filed evidence, a\n  dependency) meet there.\n- **Two candidate edits, both general (any company, any total and its parts):**\n  - make the existing consistency clause two-sided: a whole built from company-wide figures already holds its parts, so a part's\n    forecast or target moves it only by the gap the evidence shows against what that basis assumed;\n  - scope the product-target sentence to a question about that product.\n\n  Line 9's \"a component cannot exceed its whole\" duplicates line 4's clause and can be cut to pay for the growth.\n- **Versions:** a new version of the frozen product judge (judging-v7) and, if the linking line resumes, of judging-v8. Both\n  Alex's to open.\n- **Cheapest proof** (paid, cents, Alex's ceiling):\n  - **The class:** re-send the stored requests where the addition decided the rung, under the old text and under the changed text,\n    three draws each: old-1 v4 07-15, 07-16, 07-17, 08-21 and linked-1 v14 07-15..07-18. The old-text sends give the base rate:\n    07-14 shows the same request shape going either way.\n  - **Controls that must not move:**\n    - the SemiAnalysis share uplift (old-1 v4 07-10, old-3 v3 08-12);\n    - the total-CPU sum of two named products (one old-2 v16 day after 08-26);\n    - a part reading its whole (linked-1 v9 07-14 and 07-21).\n- **The share** the fix can remove: all 36 additions (32 + 4) and the 4 copied ones, if the judge follows the sentence. A sampler\n  can still slip, so the proof counts slips per draw.\n\n## 5. Out of scope\n\nThe citation format (D719), the link-question wording (D705 forbids a third wording on the same 105 pairs), the sticky previous\nconclusion, the frozen ruler, the total-CPU boundary case beyond its control role.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/CENSUS.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,3} '\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/rejected-answers.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Phase 1, step 1: census of the judge's rejected answers ($0, read by Claude, 2026-11-20)\n\n**Scope.** 49 answers in the 5 stored draws of the release comparison that the readers rejected or split, among questions rated worth\ntracking (`rejected-answers.md`). The frozen judging-v7 gave all of them. Planted reader controls are excluded: they were filtered by the\ndeck key's role, `product` only.\n\n## Two populations\n| Group | Count | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Reader-threshold splits** (Claude supported, Codex insufficient) | 23 | Mostly deadline yes/no questions answered with the status quo (\"still open\", \"not passed\"). Codex asks for a base rate that the material does not hold; Claude accepts the status quo. This is the families' known threshold gap, not a judge fault agreed by both. **Not the target** |\n| **Genuine splits** (supported against unsupported) | 2 | #13 France, #16 the Groq-licensed China chip |\n| **Rejected by both readers** (unsupported or insufficient on both sides) | 24 | **The target** |\n\n## The 24, by mechanism (one primary class each)\n| Class | Count | Cases (number in `rejected-answers.md`) | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **O: adds amounts that overlap, or counts a factor twice** | 6 | 32, 40 (Grace's trailing revenue added to a \"total CPU\" outlook); 36, 45 ($18B added to a $25B balance that likely holds it); 44 (an above-consensus uplift stacked on an above-consensus run-rate); 47 (memory costs already inside guidance) | old-3 Q3: $446B revenue by stacking uplifts, where consensus and run-rate sit in rung 2 |\n| **S: a figure that measures something else** | 6 | 31, 38, 49 (five companies' total capex and NVIDIA's own forecast for four companies' data-center capex); 28 (a cumulative investment estimate for an outstanding balance); 5 (a lease NVIDIA holds as lessee counted as a guarantee); 8 (a partner outside the question's four) | old-2 Q6: NVIDIA's $1.3T top-five forecast scaled by a 2026 share, against $1.08T consensus for five |\n| **E: an estimate or adjustment with no basis** | 5 | 39 (+46% sequential Q4); 48 (an arbitrary trim to just below $40B); 4 (28% with no denominator); 17 (a sum $0.1B under a boundary, from approximate parts); 1 (one state, with none qualifying) | old-3 Q41: guidance points above $40B; trimmed to $38–39B on unquantified risk |\n| **W: reports of talks taken as a likely completion** | 2 | 30, 42 | old-3 Q23: early-stage financing talks turn the status-quo \"No\" into \"Yes\" |\n| **R: a rung's definition misapplied** | 2 | 12, 20 (rung 2 needs a formal decision after the opening date; the reported delay predates it) | new-2 Q5 Kyber |\n| **M: too little material, default chosen** | 3 | 6, 15 (both readers: too thin); 11 (status quo against rich material) | — |\n\n## What cuts across the classes\n- **The answer leaves the reference's rung on a weak step of its own, in about 16 of 24.** The reference is the consensus, guidance or\n  status quo that the judge itself names first. Cases: 5, 8, 12, 17, 20, 28, 30, 31, 32, 38, 40, 42, 44, 47, 48 and 49. The readers'\n  rejections say, in effect, that the reference rung was right and the step off it is not justified.\n- **The step lands just across a boundary in 7:** 5, 17, 32, 40, 44, 48 and 49. Examples are $24.9B against $25B, \"just above $25B\",\n  \"just above $1.1T\" and $38–39B against $40B.\n- **The step off is asymmetric.** The same answers refuse to move on unquantified downside (\"does not quantify a reduction sufficient to\n  cross\"), yet move on unquantified upside (31, 38, 44, 49), and the reverse in 47 and 48.\n- **On the 32 matters:** M08 (36, 45, 28) is O or S, and M14 (31, 38, 49) is S. Both are in the target's two largest classes.\n\n## Not yet established (next step: mechanism)\n- Which sentence of judging-v7 lets the answer leave the reference on its own arithmetic, and whether the request defines overlap,\n  scope or the boundary.\n- Whether the judge's own account names the same sentence.\n- Whether a re-send gives the same answer. The judge is a sampler: the 24 are one draw each, though M08 and M14 recur across draws.\n\n## Errors found while doing this\n- My report to Alex on 2026-11-19 cited an old-2 answer that \"chose $75–100B against its own estimate of about $53B\". That card, C140,\n  is a planted reader control. The real old-2 answer, C153, chose $50–75B and was rejected for overlap. Corrected in the merge test's\n  `RESULT.md` and in `PLAN.md`.\n1:# Rejected or split answers among worth-tracking questions, 5 draws (dev window 09-07..09-20)\n3:### 1. new-2 Q11 (C187) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\n14:### 2. new-2 Q15 (C197) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\n25:### 3. new-2 Q19 (C020) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n36:### 4. new-2 Q20 (C051) answer-mark=insufficient type=figure\n43:### 5. new-2 Q26 (C158) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n50:### 6. new-2 Q27 (C157) answer-mark=insufficient type=yes/no\n61:### 7. new-2 Q30 (C012) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n72:### 8. new-2 Q31 (C160) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\n83:### 9. new-2 Q32 (C055) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n94:### 10. new-2 Q33 (C040) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\n105:### 11. new-2 Q4 (C078) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=yes/no\n116:### 12. new-2 Q5 (C035) answer-mark=unsupported type=yes/no\n123:### 13. new-2 Q8 (C033) answer-mark={'split': ['supported', 'unsupported']} type=yes/no\n134:### 14. new-3 Q19 (C067) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n145:### 15. new-3 Q23 (C116) answer-mark=insufficient type=yes/no\n152:### 16. new-3 Q25 (C120) answer-mark={'split': ['unsupported', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n163:### 17. new-3 Q26 (C003) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n172:### 18. new-3 Q31 (C113) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n183:### 19. new-3 Q39 (C196) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n194:### 20. new-3 Q4 (C167) answer-mark=unsupported type=yes/no\n201:### 21. new-3 Q40 (C025) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\n212:### 22. new-3 Q9 (C002) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\n223:### 23. old-1 Q16 (C098) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n234:### 24. old-1 Q20 (C175) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n245:### 25. old-1 Q22 (C057) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n256:### 26. old-1 Q26 (C065) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n267:### 27. old-1 Q27 (C145) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n278:### 28. old-1 Q28 (C115) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n285:### 29. old-1 Q44 (C026) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\n296:### 30. old-1 Q45 (C133) answer-mark=unsupported type=yes/no\n303:### 31. old-1 Q7 (C122) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n310:### 32. old-2 Q16 (C127) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n317:### 33. old-2 Q19 (C089) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n328:### 34. old-2 Q25 (C019) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n339:### 35. old-2 Q27 (C031) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n350:### 36. old-2 Q29 (C153) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\n361:### 37. old-2 Q39 (C172) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n372:### 38. old-2 Q6 (C166) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n383:### 39. old-2 Q9 (C150) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n390:### 40. old-3 Q14 (C021) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n397:### 41. old-3 Q18 (C106) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n408:### 42. old-3 Q23 (C143) answer-mark=unsupported type=yes/no\n415:### 43. old-3 Q26 (C119) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n426:### 44. old-3 Q3 (C030) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n433:### 45. old-3 Q33 (C071) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\n444:### 46. old-3 Q36 (C144) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\n455:### 47. old-3 Q37 (C174) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n462:### 48. old-3 Q41 (C063) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\n469:### 49. old-3 Q7 (C125) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
