{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '1,435p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"06-views.md step 7: one re-judging round. Open views whose reading changed are judged in dependency order, each\n     2\tonce; a view whose conclusion changed adds its open dependants. The model reads the evidence in force (with each\n     3\treplaced predecessor), the dependencies' current conclusions and its own last conclusion (for comparison only).\n     4\tCode checks every citation and that a rung move or a first judgement says changed; one retry, then the item fails.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe request is judging-v7 (D354, frozen with steps 3 and 4), judging-v8 (D694: v7's text with the fuller dependency block) or\n     7\tjudging-v12 (D713: v7's text and request; the answer's id fields take only the ids the request lists, and the check reads the same\n     8\tlists): no other version is built, and a configuration that names another is refused before any call. Every answer is stored in the\n     9\tcanonical vocabulary.\n    10\t\n    11\tThe theme block (D191, D195): an asset thesis is judged with every open, judged theme thesis in view and answers, in theme_pushes,\n    12\twhich way each theme it weighed pushes it; theme theses are judged before asset theses in a round, and a theme judgement that says\n    13\tchanged queues every open asset thesis with evidence in force, once. The round's queue is derived from the ledger (the day's changed\n    14\tviews, the dependants of views judged at this sequence number with a changed conclusion, and the asset theses a changed theme queues),\n    15\tso a resumed round recovers what an earlier commit had queued.\n    16\t\n    17\tThe evidence in view (D198): a judgement reads the arrivals and the basis among the evidence in force, not everything in force.\n    18\t\n    19\tThe reference (D260): the rung is the forecast the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects\n    20\t(consensus, guidance, the stated plan); the answer states the reference and the outcome the evidence implies, both stored beside the\n    21\trung, and an empty one is refused.\"\"\"\n    22\timport dataclasses\n    23\timport json\n    24\timport re\n    25\t\n    26\tfrom .. import config, model, vocabulary\n    27\tfrom . import store\n    28\t\n    29\tPUSHES = vocabulary.PUSHES\n    30\tSUPPORTED_VERSIONS = ('judging-v7', 'judging-v8', 'judging-v12')   # D354: the frozen judging prompt; D694: v8 = v7's text with the fuller dependency block; D712/D713: v12 = v7's text and request with closed id lists\n    31\tFULL_DEPENDENCY_BLOCK = ('judging-v8',)\n    32\tCLOSED_LISTS = ('judging-v12',)   # the answer's id fields take the ids the request lists (D713)\n    33\tREADINGS = 5                      # a piece is shown in the thesis's first five judgements after it arrives\n    34\tBASIS = 10                        # and while one of the last ten judgements cited it\n    35\tTHEME_REASONS_CHARS = 300\n    36\tUPDATED_MARK = 'updated since this thesis was last judged'\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\tdef require_supported(prompt_version):\n    40\t    \"\"\"Refuses a judging prompt version this code does not build, before any request is made.\"\"\"\n    41\t    if prompt_version not in SUPPORTED_VERSIONS:\n    42\t        raise ValueError(f'judging {prompt_version!r} is not built; this code builds only {\", \".join(SUPPORTED_VERSIONS)} (D354)')\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\tdef evidence_in_view(conn, view_id):\n    46\t    \"\"\"(the pieces a judgement of this thesis reads, how many pieces in force are not shown): the arrivals (linked after the\n    47\t    READINGS-th most recent judgement, or all when there are fewer) and the basis (cited by one of the last BASIS judgements),\n    48\t    both among the evidence in force.\"\"\"\n    49\t    in_force = store.evidence_in_force(conn, view_id)\n    50\t    recent = store.recent_judgements(conn, view_id, BASIS)\n    51\t    arrivals_after = recent[READINGS - 1]['seq'] if len(recent) >= READINGS else -1\n    52\t    basis = {evidence_id for j in recent for evidence_id in j['cites']}\n    53\t    linked_at = {r['evidence_id']: r['seq'] for r in conn.execute('select evidence_id, seq from evidence_links where view_id=?', (view_id,))}\n    54\t    shown = [p for p in in_force if linked_at.get(p['evidence_id'], -1) > arrivals_after or p['evidence_id'] in basis]\n    55\t    return shown, len(in_force) - len(shown)\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\tdef schema_for(prompt_version, lists=None):\n    59\t    \"\"\"The answer's format. Under judging-v12 (D713) its four id fields take the ids the request lists (`lists`, from\n    60\t    `request_parts`): one shared enum of the evidence ids for cites and push ids, the dependency ids for cites_views, the theme ids\n    61\t    for theme pushes; a field with no id allowed is an array with maxItems 0. Other versions ignore `lists`.\"\"\"\n    62\t    schema = _open_format(prompt_version)\n    63\t    if prompt_version not in CLOSED_LISTS:\n    64\t        return schema\n    65\t    if lists is None:\n    66\t        raise ValueError(f'{prompt_version} builds its answer format from the ids its request lists')\n    67\t    properties = schema['properties']\n    68\t    evidence, dependencies, themes = (sorted(set(lists[key])) for key in ('evidence', 'dependencies', 'themes'))\n    69\t    if evidence:\n    70\t        schema['$defs'] = {'evidence_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence}}\n    71\t        properties['cites'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'$ref': '#/$defs/evidence_id'}}\n    72\t        push_item = properties['pushes']['items']\n    73\t        properties['pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': dict(push_item, properties=dict(push_item['properties'], evidence_id={'$ref': '#/$defs/evidence_id'}))}\n    74\t    else:\n    75\t        properties['cites'] = dict(properties['cites'], maxItems=0)\n    76\t        properties['pushes'] = dict(properties['pushes'], maxItems=0)\n    77\t    if dependencies:\n    78\t        properties['cites_views'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': dependencies}}\n    79\t    else:\n    80\t        properties['cites_views'] = dict(properties['cites_views'], maxItems=0)\n    81\t    theme_item = properties['theme_pushes']['items']\n    82\t    if themes:\n    83\t        properties['theme_pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': dict(theme_item, properties=dict(theme_item['properties'], view_id={'type': 'string', 'enum': themes}))}\n    84\t    else:\n    85\t        properties['theme_pushes'] = dict(properties['theme_pushes'], maxItems=0)\n    86\t    return schema\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\tdef _open_format(prompt_version):\n    90\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n    91\t    pushes = PUSHES\n    92\t    schema = {\n    93\t        'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False,\n    94\t        'required': ['rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed'],\n    95\t        'properties': {\n    96\t            'rung': {'type': 'integer'}, 'reasons': {'type': 'string'},\n    97\t            'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}}, 'cites_views': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}},\n    98\t            'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['evidence_id', 'push'],\n    99\t                                                  'properties': {'evidence_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}},\n   100\t            'changed': {'type': 'boolean'}, 'why_changed': {'type': 'string'},\n   101\t        },\n   102\t    }\n   103\t    schema['required'].append('theme_pushes')\n   104\t    schema['properties']['theme_pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['view_id', 'push'],\n   105\t                                                                       'properties': {'view_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}}\n   106\t    schema['required'][1:1] = STANCE_FIELDS           # after rung: the reference and the implied outcome are written before the reasons\n   107\t    schema['properties'].update({field: {'type': 'string'} for field in STANCE_FIELDS})\n   108\t    return schema\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   111\tdef themes_in_force(conn, view_id):\n   112\t    \"\"\"The theme block of an asset thesis: every open theme thesis with a current state, with its rung label and whether a\n   113\t    changed conclusion was recorded after this thesis's last judgement. Empty for a theme thesis.\"\"\"\n   114\t    if store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme':\n   115\t        return []\n   116\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   117\t    out = []\n   118\t    for row in conn.execute(\"select view_id from views where object_kind='theme' order by view_id\"):\n   119\t        theme_id = row['view_id']\n   120\t        state = store.current_state(conn, theme_id)\n   121\t        if not state or not store.is_open(conn, theme_id):\n   122\t            continue\n   123\t        updated = bool(last) and bool(conn.execute('select 1 from judgements where view_id=? and changed=1 and judgement_id>? limit 1',\n   124\t                                                   (theme_id, last['judgement_id'])).fetchone())\n   125\t        rung = store.rungs_of(conn, theme_id)[state['rung'] - 1]\n   126\t        out.append({'view_id': theme_id, 'view': store.view(conn, theme_id), 'state': state, 'rung_label': rung['label'], 'updated': updated})\n   127\t    return out\n   128\t\n   129\t\n   130\tSTANCE_FIELDS = ['reference', 'implied_outcome']   # D260: what the market expects, and the figure or date the evidence implies\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\tclass JudgementRefused(Exception):\n   134\t    pass\n   135\t\n   136\t\n   137\tdef topological(conn, view_ids):\n   138\t    \"\"\"Dependencies first: a view comes after every view it depends on that is also in the set.\"\"\"\n   139\t    remaining = list(dict.fromkeys(view_ids))\n   140\t    ordered, placed = [], set()\n   141\t    while remaining:\n   142\t        progressed = False\n   143\t        for view_id in list(remaining):\n   144\t            deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n   145\t            if deps.isdisjoint(set(remaining) - {view_id}) or deps <= placed:\n   146\t                ordered.append(view_id)\n   147\t                placed.add(view_id)\n   148\t                remaining.remove(view_id)\n   149\t                progressed = True\n   150\t        if not progressed:                 # a cycle slipped through: keep going in the given order\n   151\t            ordered.extend(remaining)\n   152\t            break\n   153\t    return ordered\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   156\tdef _indented(text, indent='        '):\n   157\t    \"\"\"A stored value inside a block: every continuation line indented, so no physical line can read as a header or a listed id.\"\"\"\n   158\t    return str(text).replace('\\r\\n', '\\n').replace('\\n', '\\n' + indent)\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\tdef _dependency_lines_full(conn, target, dependency, state, rung_label, target_view, flag):\n   162\t    \"\"\"judging-v8 (D694, design §3): the read judgement's comparison point and the system's forecast in full, labelled as ours, beside\n   163\t    the reasons excerpt; the read question not judged yet shows only that.\"\"\"\n   164\t    if not state:\n   165\t        return [f'- {target} ({flag}; {dependency[\"direction\"]}) {target_view[\"question\"]}: now not judged yet']\n   166\t    return [f'- {target} ({flag}; {dependency[\"direction\"]}) {target_view[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"]} {rung_label}, judged {state[\"publication_day\"]}',\n   167\t            f'    Comparison point stated by that judgement: {_indented(state.get(\"reference\") or \"not stated\")}',\n   168\t            f'    The system\\'s forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): {_indented(state.get(\"implied_outcome\") or \"not stated\")}',\n   169\t            f'    Reasons (excerpt): {_indented((state.get(\"reasons\") or \"\")[:600])}']\n   170\t\n   171\t\n   172\tdef _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes=(), hidden=0, full_dependencies=False):\n   173\t    \"\"\"hidden: how many pieces in force are not shown. full_dependencies: the judging-v8 dependency block.\"\"\"\n   174\t    lines = [f'Thesis {view_id} ({v[\"object_id\"]}, {v[\"kind\"]}, focus \"{v[\"looks_at\"]}\", {v[\"year\"] or \"\"}, due {v[\"due_day\"]}): {v[\"question\"]}',\n   175\t             'Ladder: ' + ' / '.join(f'rung {r[\"position\"]} {r[\"label\"]}: {r[\"condition\"]}' for r in rungs), '',\n   176\t             f'[Evidence in view under this thesis, {len(pieces)} items]']\n   177\t    if hidden:\n   178\t        lines.append(f'[Not shown: {hidden} more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]')\n   179\t    for p in pieces:\n   180\t        src = p['sources'][0] if p['sources'] else {}\n   181\t        lines.append(f'- {p[\"evidence_id\"]} [{p[\"tier\"]}; {p[\"speaker\"]}; original source {p[\"original_who\"]} {p[\"original_day\"]}; from {src.get(\"input_id\")} {src.get(\"publication_day\")}] {p[\"one_line\"]}\\n    Quote: {p[\"quote\"][:400]}')\n   182\t        if p.get('replaced'):\n   183\t            lines.append(f'    It replaces {p[\"replaced\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"replaced\"][\"original_day\"]}): {p[\"replaced\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   184\t        if p.get('same_as'):\n   185\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as {\", \".join(p[\"same_as\"])}; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.')\n   186\t        if p.get('revises'):\n   187\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of {p[\"revises\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"revises\"][\"original_day\"]}); both are in force: {p[\"revises\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   188\t    if deps:\n   189\t        lines.append('\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]')\n   190\t        for d in deps:\n   191\t            target = d['resolved_to']\n   192\t            if not target:\n   193\t                lines.append(f'- the {d[\"wait_year\"]} thesis on chain {d[\"wait_chain_view_id\"]}: not yet created ({d[\"direction\"]})')\n   194\t                continue\n   195\t            tv = store.view(conn, target)\n   196\t            state = store.current_state(conn, target)\n   197\t            flag = 'historical only' if d['resolution'] == 'history_only' else ('closed' if not store.is_open(conn, target) else 'open')\n   198\t            rung_label = store.rungs_of(conn, target)[state['rung'] - 1]['label'] if state else 'not judged yet'\n   199\t            if full_dependencies:\n   200\t                lines.extend(_dependency_lines_full(conn, target, d, state, rung_label, tv, flag))\n   201\t                continue\n   202\t            lines.append(f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"] if state else \"-\"} {rung_label}; reasons: {(state or {}).get(\"reasons\", \"\")[:600]}')\n   203\t    if themes:\n   204\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Themes in force, {len(themes)}]')\n   205\t        for theme in themes:\n   206\t            tv, state = theme['view'], theme['state']\n   207\t            mark = f'; {UPDATED_MARK}' if theme['updated'] else ''\n   208\t            lines.append(f'- {theme[\"view_id\"]} ({tv[\"object_id\"]}, due {tv[\"due_day\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"]} {theme[\"rung_label\"]}; '\n   209\t                         f'judged {state[\"publication_day\"]}{mark}; reasons: {(state[\"reasons\"] or \"\")[:THEME_REASONS_CHARS]}')\n   210\t    if last:\n   211\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung {last[\"rung\"]}; reasons: {last[\"reasons\"][:800]}')\n   212\t    else:\n   213\t        lines.append('\\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.')\n   214\t    lines.append('\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; '\n   215\t                 + ('cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above' if deps else 'cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis')\n   216\t                 + ('; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views' if themes else '') + '.')\n   217\t    return lines\n   218\t\n   219\t\n   220\tdef build_prompt(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n   221\t    system, user, _ = request_parts(conn, view_id, prompt_version)\n   222\t    return system, user\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\tdef request_parts(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n   226\t    \"\"\"(system text, user text, the ids the request lists), all from the same rows at the same ledger state: the evidence pieces it\n   227\t    prints, the dependencies it gives an entry (resolved ones; a dependency still waiting has no id to cite) and the themes it prints.\"\"\"\n   228\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n   229\t    v = store.view(conn, view_id)\n   230\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   231\t    pieces, hidden = evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)\n   232\t    deps = store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id)\n   233\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   234\t    themes = themes_in_force(conn, view_id)\n   235\t    lines = _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes, hidden=hidden,\n   236\t                           full_dependencies=prompt_version in FULL_DEPENDENCY_BLOCK)\n   237\t    lists = {'evidence': sorted({p['evidence_id'] for p in pieces}), 'dependencies': sorted({d['resolved_to'] for d in deps if d['resolved_to']}),\n   238\t             'themes': sorted({theme['view_id'] for theme in themes})}\n   239\t    with open(config.prompt_path(prompt_version + '.txt'), encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n   240\t        return handle.read(), '\\n'.join(lines), lists\n   241\t\n   242\t\n   243\tdef retry_note(errors):\n   244\t    return '\\n\\nThe code returned the previous answer because: ' + '; '.join(errors) + '\\nCorrect it and answer again, citing only the listed ids.'\n   245\t\n   246\t\n   247\tdef themes_shown_in(request_text):\n   248\t    \"\"\"The theme ids of the block a request carried: the ids the answer may name, whatever the ledger says now.\"\"\"\n   249\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Themes in force, ', 1)\n   250\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (v\\d+) \\(', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   251\t\n   252\t\n   253\tdef evidence_shown_in(request_text):\n   254\t    \"\"\"The evidence ids the request listed: the ids the answer may cite and push.\"\"\"\n   255\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Evidence in ', 1)\n   256\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (e\\d+) \\[', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\tdef check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None, lists=None):\n   260\t    \"\"\"request_text: the user message that was sent, so theme and evidence ids are checked against what it carried (a theme may have\n   261\t    closed or opened since); without it, against what the ledger would show now. lists (judging-v12, D713): the ids the request\n   262\t    listed, from the rows that rendered it, the same lists its answer format closed; membership is checked against them.\"\"\"\n   263\t    errors = []\n   264\t    if lists is not None:\n   265\t        shown = set(lists['themes'])\n   266\t    else:\n   267\t        shown = themes_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {theme['view_id'] for theme in themes_in_force(conn, view_id)}\n   268\t    named = []\n   269\t    for entry in data.get('theme_pushes', []):\n   270\t        if entry['view_id'] in named:\n   271\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is named twice in theme_pushes')\n   272\t        elif entry['view_id'] not in shown:\n   273\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is not a theme in force shown to {view_id}')\n   274\t        named.append(entry['view_id'])\n   275\t    # the closed set is what the request showed (D198), read from the text that was sent, else computed from the ledger\n   276\t    if lists is not None:\n   277\t        allowed, deps = set(lists['evidence']), set(lists['dependencies'])\n   278\t    else:\n   279\t        allowed = evidence_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {p['evidence_id'] for p in evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)[0]}\n   280\t        deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n   281\t    not_allowed = lambda e: f'{e} is not among the evidence shown to {view_id}'\n   282\t    bad_push = lambda e: f'push on {e} which was not shown to {view_id}'\n   283\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   284\t    if not 1 <= data['rung'] <= len(rungs):\n   285\t        errors.append(f'rung {data[\"rung\"]} outside 1..{len(rungs)}')\n   286\t    for cited in data['cites']:\n   287\t        if cited not in allowed:\n   288\t            errors.append(not_allowed(cited))\n   289\t    for cited in data['cites_views']:\n   290\t        if cited not in deps:\n   291\t            errors.append(f'{cited} is not a dependency of {view_id}')\n   292\t    for push in data['pushes']:\n   293\t        if push['evidence_id'] not in allowed:\n   294\t            errors.append(bad_push(push['evidence_id']))\n   295\t    for field in STANCE_FIELDS:\n   296\t        if not str(data.get(field) or '').strip():\n   297\t            errors.append(f'{field} is empty: state what the market expects and the figure or date the evidence implies')\n   298\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   299\t    if last is None and not data['changed']:\n   300\t        errors.append('first judgement must say changed')\n   301\t    if last is not None and last['rung'] != data['rung'] and not data['changed']:\n   302\t        errors.append(f'rung moved from {last[\"rung\"]} to {data[\"rung\"]} but changed is false')\n   303\t    return errors\n   304\t\n   305\t\n   306\tdef _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer):\n   307\t    data = vocabulary.judgement_answer(answer.data)\n   308\t    judgement_id = store.insert(conn, 'judgements', view_id=view_id, seq=seq, input_id=item['input_id'], rung=data['rung'],\n   309\t                                reasons=data['reasons'], cited_evidence_json=json.dumps(data['cites']), cited_views_json=json.dumps(data['cites_views']),\n   310\t                                pushes_json=json.dumps(data['pushes'], ensure_ascii=False), changed=1 if data['changed'] else 0,\n   311\t                                theme_pushes_json=json.dumps(data.get('theme_pushes', []), ensure_ascii=False),\n   312\t                                reference=data.get('reference'), implied_outcome=data.get('implied_outcome'),\n   313\t                                why_changed=data['why_changed'], config_checksum=cfg.checksum, call_id=answer.call_id,\n   314\t                                request_sha256=answer.request_sha256, publication_day=item['publication_day'])\n   315\t    conn.commit()\n   316\t    return {'view_id': view_id, 'judgement_id': judgement_id, 'rung': data['rung'], 'changed': data['changed'], 'cost': answer.cost_usd, 'cached': answer.cached}\n   317\t\n   318\t\n   319\tJUDGE_WORKERS = 32     # a day's round is up to 40 judgements of about 16 s each in two dependency levels (themes, then assets); 32 at a time makes a round about two calls long (2026-11-02)\n   320\t\n   321\t\n   322\tdef upstream(conn, view_id):\n   323\t    \"\"\"Every view this one depends on, directly or through its dependencies.\"\"\"\n   324\t    seen, stack = set(), [view_id]\n   325\t    while stack:\n   326\t        for dep in store.dependencies_of(conn, stack.pop()):\n   327\t            target = dep['resolved_to']\n   328\t            if target and target not in seen:\n   329\t                seen.add(target)\n   330\t                stack.append(target)\n   331\t    return seen\n   332\t\n   333\t\n   334\tdef open_asset_views(conn):\n   335\t    return [v for v in store.open_views(conn) if store.view(conn, v)['object_kind'] != 'theme']\n   336\t\n   337\t\n   338\tdef queued_by_change(conn, view_id):\n   339\t    \"\"\"What a changed conclusion of this view queues: its dependants and, for a theme thesis, every open asset thesis. The one rule,\n   340\t    read for a live judgement and for the judgements a resumed round finds committed.\"\"\"\n   341\t    queued = store.dependants_of(conn, view_id)\n   342\t    if store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme':\n   343\t        queued = queued + open_asset_views(conn)\n   344\t    return queued\n   345\t\n   346\t\n   347\tdef queued_by_ledger(conn, seq):\n   348\t    \"\"\"What the judgements already committed at this sequence number queued; a fresh round finds nothing here, a resumed round\n   349\t    finds what the interrupted one had queued in memory.\"\"\"\n   350\t    queued = []\n   351\t    for row in conn.execute('select view_id from judgements where seq=? and changed=1 order by judgement_id', (seq,)):\n   352\t        queued.extend(queued_by_change(conn, row['view_id']))\n   353\t    return queued\n   354\t\n   355\t\n   356\tdef round_of_judgements(archive, cfg, conn, changed_views, seq, item, *, transport=None):\n   357\t    \"\"\"Judge the open views in changed_views, dependencies first; a changed conclusion queues open dependants.\n   358\t\n   359\t    Views that do not depend on each other are judged at the same time: each level of the dependency order is sent\n   360\t    to the model concurrently (the prompts are built and the answers checked and written in this thread, so the\n   361\t    database connection never crosses threads). A view waits while anything upstream of it is still pending, so a\n   362\t    dependant always reads this round's conclusion of its dependency. A view with no evidence in force stays\n   363\t    unjudged (D59), and a view already judged at this sequence number (a resumed round) is not judged twice.\n   364\t\n   365\t    Among the views ready to be judged, theme theses go first, so an asset thesis reads this round's theme conclusions; a theme judgement that says changed queues every open asset thesis with\n   366\t    evidence in force. The starting queue adds what the ledger says earlier commits at this sequence number queued.\"\"\"\n   367\t    import concurrent.futures\n   368\t\n   369\t    version = cfg.rejudging.prompt_version\n   370\t    closed = version in CLOSED_LISTS\n   371\t    open_schema = None if closed else schema_for(version)\n   372\t\n   373\t    def eligible(view_id):\n   374\t        return store.is_open(conn, view_id) and not store.has_judgement(conn, view_id, seq) and bool(store.evidence_in_force(conn, view_id))\n   375\t\n   376\t    def is_theme(view_id):\n   377\t        return store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme'\n   378\t\n   379\t    pending = [v for v in topological(conn, list(changed_views) + queued_by_ledger(conn, seq)) if eligible(v)]\n   380\t    done, results = set(), []\n   381\t    while pending:\n   382\t        ready = [v for v in pending if upstream(conn, v).isdisjoint(set(pending) - {v})] or pending[:1]\n   383\t        level = [v for v in ready if is_theme(v)] or ready\n   384\t        if closed:                                         # D713: the format's lists and the check's lists come from the rows that render the text\n   385\t            parts = {v: request_parts(conn, v, version) for v in level}\n   386\t            prompts = {v: parts[v][:2] for v in level}\n   387\t            lists = {v: parts[v][2] for v in level}\n   388\t            schemas = {v: schema_for(version, lists[v]) for v in level}\n   389\t        else:                                              # judging-v7/v8: through build_prompt, which harnesses wrap\n   390\t            prompts = {v: build_prompt(conn, v, version) for v in level}\n   391\t            lists = {v: None for v in level}\n   392\t            schemas = {v: open_schema for v in level}\n   393\t\n   394\t        def ask(view_id, note=''):\n   395\t            system, user = prompts[view_id]\n   396\t            return model.call(archive, cfg, cfg.rejudging, system, user + note, schemas[view_id], 'judgement', 'judge', transport=transport)\n   397\t\n   398\t        with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=JUDGE_WORKERS) as pool_:\n   399\t            answers = dict(zip(level, pool_.map(ask, level)))\n   400\t        for view_id in level:\n   401\t            answer = answers[view_id]\n   402\t            errors = check_answer(conn, view_id, answer.data, prompts[view_id][1], lists[view_id])\n   403\t            if errors:\n   404\t                archive.retire(answer.call_id)                 # a refused answer never answers the same request again (a resume re-samples)\n   405\t                answer = ask(view_id, retry_note(errors))\n   406\t                errors = check_answer(conn, view_id, answer.data, prompts[view_id][1], lists[view_id])\n   407\t                if errors:\n   408\t                    archive.retire(answer.call_id)\n   409\t                    raise JudgementRefused(f'{view_id}: ' + '; '.join(errors))\n   410\t            result = _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer)\n   411\t            done.add(view_id)\n   412\t            results.append(result)\n   413\t            if result['changed']:\n   414\t                for dependant in queued_by_change(conn, view_id):\n   415\t                    if dependant not in done and dependant not in pending and eligible(dependant):\n   416\t                        pending.append(dependant)\n   417\t        pending = [v for v in topological(conn, pending) if v not in done and eligible(v)]\n   418\t    return results\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '278,324p;350,396p;426,480p' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/rejected-answers.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 28. old-1 Q28 (C115) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: How much will NVIDIA’s committed equity-investment obligations to ecosystem partners total at the FY2027 annual report?\nA: The disclosed amount is at least $60 billion and below $80 billion.\nReason: The company-based reference is about $43 billion—$25 billion of commitments as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027—and sits in rung 2. The broader $70 billion Bank of America estimate and evidence of substantial 2026 commitment activity support a higher balance than that reference. I estimate about $75 billion outstanding at the FY2027 report: the $70 billion estimate is the anchor, with a modest net addition for later disclosed deals, discounted for possible overlap and for investments that may be funded before the report. That remains below the $80 billion boundary for rung 4.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | Distinct, large disclosed metric ($25B at July 26). The $75B answer anchors on BofA's cumulative $70B, which includes funded deals. Commitments get funded ($18B this year), so even with new deals the material points to rungs 1–2.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | Equity commitments are financially material and distinct from the earlier credit-support obligations. The $70 billion estimate is not an outstanding year-end balance, while the $18 billion may overlap the $25 billion; neither justifies choosing $60–80 billion.\n\n### 29. old-1 Q44 (C026) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\nQ: How much Vera CPU revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\nA: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion.\nReason: The reference is NVIDIA management’s stated potential of $20 billion in Vera CPU revenue in FY2027 and potential for more than double that in FY2028, which implies $40 billion-plus and sits in rung 1. The implied forecast remains about $35 billion: I discount that FY2028 potential because no shipment volume or realized Vera CPU revenue is disclosed, although full production and standalone sales support execution. AMD’s reported benchmark advantages are a competitive risk but do not quantify a revenue reduction large enough to move the estimate below $30 billion. Thus the forecast stays in rung 2, below the reference’s rung.\n- round-0/claude: supported | Management frames Vera as a $20B FY2027 potential that could more than double in FY2028, a revenue-changing bet distinct from the FY2027 total-CPU question. Discounting soft 'potential' guidance, with no realized revenue and AMD competition, to about $35B is reasonable.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | Vera revenue at this scale matters and is distinct from total revenue and FY2027 CPU revenue. Management’s potential exceeds $40 billion, but the material does not substantiate a $35 billion estimate or distinguish it from neighboring ranges.\n- round-1/claude: supported | Not too thin: management explicitly framed $20B FY2027 'potential', more than doubling. That puts $40B on the rung 1/2 boundary, so a modest haircut for unrealized, hedged 'potential' guidance reasonably lands in rung 2.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | Vera revenue at this scale matters and is distinct from the earlier questions. Management’s potential exceeds $40 billion, but the material does not quantify a discount or otherwise justify $30–40 billion over neighboring ranges.\n- round-2/claude: supported | Management's reiterated $20B FY2027 'potential' more than doubling gives an explicit anchor at the $40B boundary. Haircutting hedged, unrealized upside guidance to the adjacent rung is a reasoned, material-based choice, not one made on material too thin.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | Vera revenue at this scale is material and distinct from earlier bets. Management’s more-than-$40 billion potential is a useful anchor, but the evidence does not justify a discount to $30–40 billion over neighboring ranges.\n\n### 30. old-1 Q45 (C133) answer-mark=unsupported type=yes/no\nQ: Will NVIDIA complete an investment in Anthropic’s potential IPO by September 14, 2027?\nA: NVIDIA completes an investment in Anthropic by September 14, 2027.\nReason: The reference is rung 2 (No): absent a market consensus or stated completion schedule, the default is that NVIDIA has not completed an investment by September 14, 2027. The specific Reuters-attributed reports that NVIDIA is discussing or weighing an anchor investment of up to $10 billion, together with the report that Anthropic selected Nasdaq for a potential IPO, make completion by the deadline the more likely outcome. The reported investment is not agreed and the IPO remains potential, but the roughly one-year window and concrete size of the reported discussions keep the forecast on rung 1. The additional IPO fundraising and valuation figures do not materially change that assessment.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | A possible $10B NVIDIA stake in Anthropic is material capital and credit exposure. The answer still overrides its own 'No' reference on unagreed talks alone. Completion also needs an IPO that remains only potential within a year.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | A potential $10 billion investment is material to NVIDIA and distinct from earlier questions. Reported discussions and a possible Nasdaq listing do not establish that an IPO and investment are more likely than not to be completed within a year.\n\n### 31. old-1 Q7 (C122) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\nA: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and less than $1.2 trillion.\nReason: The market reference is BofA's $1.08 trillion 2027 estimate for five companies; using the carried Oracle adjustment of roughly $100–$150 billion, the four-company reference is about $930–$980 billion, in rung 3. NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion forecast for the top five, adjusted by the same Oracle amount, implies about $1.15–$1.20 trillion for these four, centered near $1.16 trillion and in rung 4. UBS's 25% 2027 growth view and financing and spending-flexibility risks pull down, while Morgan Stanley's forecast of over $1 trillion in AI infrastructure spending by the four, continued cloud growth, and NVIDIA's forecast keep the most likely outcome above $1.1 trillion. The evidence does not make $1.2 trillion or more the most likely outcome, so it does not reach rung 5.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | 2027 hyperscaler capex drives NVIDIA revenue; a distinct year from the 2026 card. Consensus (BofA, about $930–980B for four), UBS 25% growth and Morgan Stanley's >$1T all point to rung 3; rung 4 rests on NVIDIA's interested forecast.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | Hyperscaler spending is material to NVIDIA, and 2027 can differ from 2026. The adjusted consensus favors $930–$980 billion; Morgan Stanley’s over-$1 trillion forecast does not establish $1.1 trillion, and the reasons do not justify preferring NVIDIA’s higher estimate.\n\n### 32. old-2 Q16 (C127) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\nA: Total CPU revenue is greater than $25 billion.\nReason: The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The most likely total is just above $25 billion: the specific FY2027 Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027; carrying that recent Grace run-rate through the fiscal year puts the total slightly above the rung-1 boundary. Vera shipments and full production support the outlook, though the estimate is close to the boundary: Wolfe’s 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD’s performance claims are competitive risks without a quantified revision to NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU demand figure is a headwind, but it is not an explicit cap on NVIDIA’s specific Vera sales outlook.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | Total CPU revenue, including Grace, is a different bet from standalone Vera revenue. But 'nearly $20B' plus Grace near $5B (declining as Vera ramps) lands at or below $25B. 'Total' wording and Wolfe's figure point to rung 2–3.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | Total CPU revenue can differ materially from standalone sales. NVIDIA’s own nearly $20 billion total-CPU outlook does not justify adding Grace’s trailing revenue to a $20 billion Vera estimate; the sources may overlap, and trailing revenue is not FY2027 revenue.\n\n### 33. old-2 Q19 (C089) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\nQ: Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?\nA: The Senate has not passed the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31.\nReason: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote. On balance, non-passage by December 31 remains more likely.\n- round-0/claude: supported | RASA would extend export controls to cloud access, putting NVIDIA's Southeast Asian compute sales to Chinese users at risk, a material revenue issue with no earlier duplicate. Material shows no Senate action or schedule since January, so non-passage is justified.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | Remote-access export controls could materially affect demand for NVIDIA’s advanced chips. The bill’s pending status and alleged access cases do not establish whether passage or non-passage by year-end is more likely.\n- round-1/claude: supported | Keep supported: the material shows the bill stalled in the Senate from January through August 19, with no scheduled vote. That stall points to non-passage, and the reason correctly treats the alleged access cases as pressure, not legislative progress.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | Remote-access controls could materially affect demand for NVIDIA chips. The bill’s pending status and alleged access cases provide no Senate timetable or other basis for choosing non-passage over passage by year-end.\n### 36. old-2 Q29 (C153) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\nQ: How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\nA: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion.\nReason: The reference is Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; that sits in rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion), though its period and relation to outstanding commitments are unclear. The disclosed $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives a $43 billion base. Adding the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure, and risk-adjusting the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions, implies about $53 billion. The newly reported more than $42 billion of equity stakes purchased in the first half of FY2027 confirms an exceptional pace, but it is spending rather than an additional outstanding commitment balance, so I do not add it again. The $30 billion OpenAI investment was announced before the July 26 commitment snapshot and is not counted again. The implied figure remains in rung 3, below the $75 billion boundary for rung 4.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | Commitments of $25B against $99B of holdings can move cash, so it is worth tracking. But adding the $18B to the $25B likely double-counts. Funding will draw the year-end balance down, so rung 1–2 is likelier.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | Equity commitments are material to NVIDIA’s capital exposure and distinct from credit support. The $18 billion may overlap the $25 billion balance; the $70 billion estimate measures an unclear period and scope. Neither supports choosing the $50–75 billion range.\n- round-1/claude: unsupported | The $18B is likely part of the $25B balance, not extra. Funding draws the balance down by year-end. The $70B is a cumulative investment estimate, not outstanding commitments. Even without the double-count, the answer's own sum lands in rung 2.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | Equity commitments are material and distinct from credit support. The $18 billion may overlap the $25 billion balance, while the $70 billion estimate has unclear scope. Future commitments and funding are too uncertain to choose a year-end range.\n- round-2/claude: unsupported | The $18B is likely the in-year part of the $25B balance, so adding it double-counts. Funding then draws the balance down. Without that, the answer's own sum is about $35B, in rung 2. The $70B measures investments, not commitments.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | Equity commitments are material and distinct from credit support. The $18 billion may overlap the $25 billion balance, and the $70 billion estimate has unclear scope. Future commitments and payments are too uncertain to favor a range.\n\n### 37. old-2 Q39 (C172) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\nQ: Will the U.S. Justice Department conclude its antitrust review of NVIDIA’s Groq transaction by 2027-01-31?\nA: The Justice Department has not publicly concluded the review by 2027-01-31.\nReason: The reference is rung 2: no market consensus or DOJ completion timetable is supplied, and the review is reported to be active. The investigation and reported information request make a public conclusion by the deadline less likely than the review remaining open. The transaction’s scale and the legal and congressional scrutiny add context for a substantive review, but do not establish a conclusion date. The reported view that DOJ is unlikely to require an unwind concerns the possible remedy, not whether or when the review will conclude.\n- round-0/claude: supported | A DOJ antitrust review of a $17–20B Groq deal can bring material legal or remedy risk, and no earlier question covers it. The active probe, recent information request and lack of any timetable favour no public conclusion by January 2027.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | The review concerns a multibillion-dollar transaction, making it worth tracking. An active inquiry and information request establish no completion timetable or basis for choosing whether public conclusion is more likely before or after the deadline.\n- round-1/claude: supported | The material shows an active probe with a recent formal information request and no settlement or timetable. Such investigations rarely end publicly within four months, so 'not concluded' is favoured over its neighbour, not left undetermined.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | The multibillion-dollar transaction makes the review worth tracking. The active inquiry and undated information request provide no basis for judging whether public conclusion is more likely before or after January 31.\n- round-2/claude: supported | Keep supported. The probe is active nine months in, with a formal information request outstanding and no timetable or settlement signs. DOJ rarely announces conclusions publicly, so the material favours 'not concluded' and is not too thin.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | The transaction’s scale makes the review worth tracking. The inquiry is active, but the information request is undated and no completion timetable or relevant base rate is supplied to favor either deadline outcome.\n\n### 38. old-2 Q6 (C166) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\nA: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and less than $1.2 trillion.\nReason: The market reference is approximately $1.08 trillion for calendar 2027, which is rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate four-to-five share proxy (about $730 billion of $800 billion) to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five estimate. That is above rung 4’s $1.1 trillion floor but below rung 5’s $1.2 trillion floor. New grid-connection and other execution constraints increase downside risk, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to cross either boundary; repeated reports of the $1.3 trillion estimate do not independently raise it. The share proxy is approximate because the underlying figures do not have perfectly matched scope.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | Big-4 2027 capex drives NVIDIA revenue. Independent BofA consensus is $1.08T for five companies including Oracle, so about $1T for four (rung 3). The reason ignores this and adopts NVIDIA's self-interested $1.3T proxy despite grid-freeze downside.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | Hyperscaler spending is material to NVIDIA, and 2026 and 2027 outcomes can differ. The $1.19 trillion estimate relies on a mismatched 2026 spending proxy and an unverified stable share; the competing $1.08 trillion estimate covers five companies.\n- round-1/claude: unsupported | Material points to rung 3. BofA's $1.08T covers five companies including Oracle, and UBS's 25% growth on $730B gives about $910B. The reason admits the market reference is rung 3 but adopts NVIDIA's mismatched-scope $1.19T proxy, ignoring downside risks.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | Hyperscaler spending is material to NVIDIA, and 2026 and 2027 are distinct outcomes. The $1.19 trillion estimate depends on a mismatched, unverified share proxy; BofA’s lower estimate covers five companies. Neither establishes which rung is better supported.\n- round-2/claude: unsupported | Not thin: BofA $1.08T (five incl. Oracle), UBS 25% on ~$730B (~$910B), and Benzinga's $916B-to-$1.2T interpolation all point to rung 3. The reason concedes rung 3, then follows one NVIDIA-sourced, scope-mismatched proxy.\n- round-2/codex: unsupported | Hyperscaler spending is material to NVIDIA, and 2027 is a distinct outcome from 2026. BofA’s $1.08 trillion estimate covers five companies, pointing below $1.1 trillion for four; the selected rung relies on an unsupported, mismatched-scope share proxy.\n\n### 39. old-2 Q9 (C150) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nA: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion.\nReason: The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp. SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates is a meaningful upside input: applying it to the roughly $250 billion of second-half revenue implied by the $414 billion reference would point to about $464 billion for the year. I discount that forecast because it does not state its underlying estimate and its implied Q4 is much higher than the company has quantified; the resulting central estimate remains near $410 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Supply and competition risks, and NVIDIA's exclusion of China compute revenue from its outlook, temper the upside.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | FY27 Data Center revenue is a core bet; no earlier listed question duplicates it. Q4 jumps from ~$122B to $146B (+46% sequentially, versus a ~12% Q3 guide) without new quantified evidence, despite supply-constraint items; rung 3 fits better.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | Data Center revenue is central to NVIDIA’s business and distinct from the earlier questions. The $146 billion Q4 estimate requires roughly 46% sequential growth; qualitative hyperscale reacceleration and the Rubin ramp do not justify that jump over the previous estimate.\n\n### 40. old-3 Q14 (C021) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: What will NVIDIA report as total CPU revenue for FY2027?\nA: Total CPU revenue is at least $25 billion.\nReason: The reference is NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2; it is not a forecast of total CPU revenue. Adding Grace’s reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion as the available run-rate puts total CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing rung 1’s boundary. Vera is in full production, shipments are underway, and customer deployments support the $20 billion outlook. AMD’s performance claims and reports of customers designing their own CPUs are downside risks, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook or establish a Grace decline.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | A ~$20B+ CPU line can move NVIDIA revenue and profit. NVIDIA itself calls ~$20B its 'total CPU'/'total server CPU' figure. Adding a backward-looking, fading Grace run-rate to 'nearly $20B' only edges past $25B, and risks double-counting.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | CPU revenue at this scale is material and distinct from total company revenue. Nearly $20 billion plus Grace’s trailing revenue does not establish FY2027 revenue above $25 billion: Grace may decline, and the figures’ scopes may overlap.\n\n### 44. old-3 Q3 (C030) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027?\nA: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion (110% of the $392 billion analyst estimate).\nReason: The market reference is about $411 billion (e4360, e5406), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The evidence-based run-rate is about $419 billion before the additional second-half data-center-compute uplift: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; Q3 is about $113.6 billion using the $108 billion midpoint guidance and the cited 3.1% average beat over the high end; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth pace forward gives about $127 billion for Q4. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute adds about $27–28 billion, implying roughly $446 billion and crossing the rung-1 boundary. The analyst view of 90% Q3 and 65% Q4 year-over-year growth is a counter-signal, but it does not outweigh the guided-growth run-rate and the specific second-half compute tracking. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that would require a sharp Q4 decline after the reported first half and Q3 guidance, so it is inconsistent with the nearer-term run-rate.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | FY2027 revenue is core and first listed. The run-rate (~$419B) and consensus ($398–411B) both sit in rung 2. Stacking SemiAnalysis's 20%-above-consensus uplift on an already above-consensus run-rate double-counts.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | FY2027 revenue is fundamental to NVIDIA. The $446 billion estimate adds SemiAnalysis’s uplift over consensus to an already above-consensus run-rate, risking double counting; the 12% Q4 growth assumption is not guidance. The $411 billion consensus falls in rung 2.\n\n### 45. old-3 Q33 (C071) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\nQ: At NVIDIA’s FY2027 annual filing, what amount of equity investment commitments will NVIDIA report as outstanding or committed?\nA: NVIDIA reports at least $30 billion but less than $50 billion.\nReason: The company-based reference is about $43 billion, in rung 3: $25 billion of equity investment commitments reported at July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027. The newer spending and deal reports show a large investment pace, but do not establish an additional, non-overlapping year-end commitment balance; some may already be within the $18 billion, and executed investments are not themselves outstanding commitments. The unspecified-period $70 billion estimate and the unconfirmed or “up to” amounts do not justify moving the forecast across the $50 billion rung boundary.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | $25B equity commitments can move cash materially. But the $43B adds $18B 'committed for rest of year' to the $25B, which likely overlaps it. Funding during the year should shrink the balance, pointing nearer rung 2.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | The commitments are financially material and distinct from earlier questions. The $18 billion may overlap the $25 billion and may be invested before year-end, so adding them does not establish a $43 billion filing balance or justify choosing a range.\n- round-1/claude: unsupported | The latest balance, $25B, is rung 2. The $18B to be funded this year most likely comes out of that $25B, so adding them double-counts. The material is enough to choose and points to rung 2, not rung 3.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | The commitments are financially material and distinct from earlier questions. The $18 billion may overlap the $25 billion and may be invested before year-end; unknown new commitments and outflows leave too little basis to choose a range.\n- round-2/claude: unsupported | Rung 3 comes from adding $18B to the $25B balance, though the $18B is probably the part of the $25B to be funded this year. The reported $25B balance is rung 2, and no evidence lifts it past $30B.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | Equity commitments are material and distinct from earlier questions. The $18 billion may overlap the $25 billion, and investments could reduce the balance before year-end. New commitments and timing are too uncertain to favor a range.\n\n### 46. old-3 Q36 (C144) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\nQ: Will NVIDIA begin shipping Rubin Ultra by December 31, 2027?\nA: NVIDIA has begun shipping Rubin Ultra by December 31, 2027.\nReason: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or specific Rubin Ultra shipment deadline is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no shipment by December 31, 2027. The evidence makes a 2027 start more likely than not: AWS explicitly includes Rubin Ultra in its 2027–2028 deployment plan, and NVIDIA is testing Rubin Ultra versions; Vera Rubin being in full production adds support for progress on the adjacent generation. The AWS total is shared across Blackwell Ultra, Rubin, and Rubin Ultra and across two years, so it does not establish Rubin Ultra volume or guarantee a 2027 delivery. The testing of revised memory configurations is an execution risk, but not a confirmed delay. The most likely outcome therefore remains shipment by the deadline, though with meaningful uncertainty.\n- round-0/claude: supported | Rubin Ultra timing drives FY2028 data-center revenue. Kyber has a 2028 deadline, so its answer can diverge. AWS's 2027–28 plan names Rubin Ultra and Vera Rubin is in full production, so Yes edges ahead despite the memory-cut risk.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | Rubin Ultra is a potentially material product, distinct from Vera Rubin. AWS’s two-year, multi-product deployment plan does not establish a 2027 shipment, and testing and Vera Rubin production do not supply enough timing evidence to favor either answer.\n- round-1/claude: supported | Keep supported: AWS's 2027–28 plan names Rubin Ultra, silicon is already in testing 16 months before the deadline, and Vera Rubin is in full production on cadence. That modestly favours Yes, and the reason states the memory-cut risk.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | Rubin Ultra is potentially material and distinct from earlier questions. AWS’s two-year, multi-product plan does not indicate when Rubin Ultra will ship; testing and Vera Rubin production provide too little timing evidence to favor either outcome.\n- round-2/claude: supported | Keep supported: Rubin Ultra silicon is in testing 16 months before the deadline, AWS's 2027–28 plan names it, and Vera Rubin is in full production. Together these modestly favour Yes, and the reason flags the timing and memory risks.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | Rubin Ultra is material and distinct from earlier questions. AWS’s plan spans two years and three products, while testing and Vera Rubin production provide too little evidence to favor a 2027 shipment over a later one.\n\n### 47. old-3 Q37 (C174) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\nA: FY2028 gross margin is at least 71% and no greater than 72%.\nReason: The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2028 gross-margin guidance of 72%–73%: exactly 72% is rung 3, while outcomes above 72% through 73% are rung 2 (the range midpoint, 72.5%, is in rung 2). I forecast about 72.0%, at the guidance floor: negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery, but further memory-price increases and tight supply make the floor more likely than a result above 72%. That is rung 3, not below it; the new supply details strengthen the downside pressure but do not quantify a NVIDIA-specific cost impact or revise its FY2028 guidance enough to imply a different rung.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | FY2028 gross margin is a material bet; only the later identical card duplicates it. Guidance of 72–73% (midpoint 72.5%) already reflects the memory costs, and price increases are negotiated, so the material points to rung 2, not 72.0% exactly.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | FY2028 gross margin materially affects NVIDIA’s profit and is distinct from FY2027 margin. Management’s 72%–73% guidance favors rung 2; memory pressures were already reflected in that guidance, and the material does not justify selecting exactly 72% over higher outcomes.\n\n### 48. old-3 Q41 (C063) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\nA: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion.\nReason: The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 expectation of more than double its $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, implying more than $40 billion and placing the reference in rung 2. I discount that forward target to roughly $38–39 billion: the evidence shows broad intended deployment and an Anthropic evaluation, but not Vera shipment volume in hand or a sized customer commitment; AMD’s reported performance comparisons add execution and competitiveness risk. That estimate remains below the $40 billion rung-2 boundary.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is a $40B-scale bet and differs from the FY2027 CPU questions. Repeated CFO and CEO guidance of more than doubling $20B points to rung 2; vendor AMD benchmarks don't justify an arbitrary trim just below $40B.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | Vera revenue at this scale is material, and FY2028 can diverge from the earlier FY2027 question. Management’s forecast points above $40 billion; the cited competitive and execution risks do not justify the specific discount below that boundary.\n\n### 49. old-3 Q7 (C125) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\nQ: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\nA: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and below $1.2 trillion.\nReason: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount; Microsoft’s year-over-year capex growth and the demand/backlog evidence support continued spending. The $1.08 trillion estimates and construction, labor, and grid constraints temper the forecast, but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to keep the scoped estimate below $1.1 trillion. The estimate is borderline because no supplied evidence directly quantifies the scope discount.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | 2027 hyperscaler capex drives NVIDIA revenue; a distinct year from the 2026 question. BofA's $1.08T covers five companies' total capex. The narrower four-company, data-center scope should fall lower, within rung 3, not above $1.1T.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | Hyperscaler investment materially affects NVIDIA’s demand, and 2027 spending is distinct from 2026. The $1.08 trillion and $1.3 trillion forecasts cover five companies; neither quantifies the adjustments needed to justify choosing above $1.1 trillion for four-company data-center spending.\n- round-1/claude: unsupported | Consensus is $1.08T for five firms' total capex, already in rung 3, and narrowing to four firms and data-center spending only lowers it. UBS, Bloomberg and Hazen all point near $0.9–1.0T. Only NVIDIA's $1.3T supports rung 4.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | 2027 hyperscaler spending materially affects NVIDIA and is distinct from 2026. The $1.08 trillion and $1.3 trillion estimates cover broader scopes; neither quantifies the adjustments needed to choose confidently between the neighboring ranges.\n- round-2/claude: unsupported | Keep. The material leans clearly to rung 3. Consensus for five firms' total capex is $1.08T, and narrowing to four firms and data-center spending only lowers it. Only NVIDIA's own promotional $1.3T supports rung 4.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | 2027 hyperscaler spending materially affects NVIDIA and is distinct from 2026. The $1.08 trillion and $1.3 trillion forecasts cover five companies; the material does not quantify the scope adjustment or justify choosing between adjacent ranges.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
