{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,pathlib,re,collections,decimal\nD=decimal.Decimal\ndef db(i,name='\\\\''views'\\\\''):\n c=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod'\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\''.sqlite'\\\\'')).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,j):\n c=a.execute('\\\\''select * from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],)).fetchone()\n assert c and c['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\'']==j['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\''] and c['\\\\''purpose'\\\\'']=='\\\\''judge'\\\\'',dict(j)\n d=json.loads(c['\\\\''request_json'\\\\''])\n return d,json.loads(c['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\nEVID=re.compile(r'\\\\''\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\\\\Z)'\\\\'',re.M|re.S)\ndef evidence(t):\n b=t.split('\\\\''[Evidence in view under this thesis, '\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0]\n m=dict((x.group(1),x.group(0).strip()) for x in EVID.finditer(b))\n assert len(m)==int(b.split()[0]),(len(m),b[:20])\n return m\ndef user(d):\n return next(m['\\\\''content'\\\\''] for m in d['\\\\''messages'\\\\''] if m['\\\\''role'\\\\'']=='\\\\''user'\\\\'')\nfor i,ids,day in [(2,['\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v29'\\\\''],'\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\''),(3,['\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v41'\\\\''],'\\\\''2026-09-20'\\\\'')]:\n v=db(i); a=db(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\'')\n for id in ids:\n  j=v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day<=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1'\\\\'',(id,day)).fetchone()\n  if not j: print('\\\\''missing'\\\\'',i,id);continue\n  d,raw=req(a,j); t=user(d); e=evidence(t)\n  print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',i,id,j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''],j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],j['\\\\''seq'\\\\''],t.split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n[Evidence'\\\\'')[0], '\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',len(e), re.findall(r'\\\\''\\\\[Not shown:.*?\\\\]'\\\\'',t), '\\\\''themes'\\\\'',re.findall(r'\\\\''\\\\[Themes in force, \\\\d+\\\\]'\\\\'',t))\n  print('\\\\''RAW'\\\\'', {k:x for k,x in raw.items() if k not in ('\\\\''pushes'\\\\'','\\\\''theme_pushes'\\\\'')})\n  for k in json.loads(j['\\\\''cited_evidence_json'\\\\'']): print(e[k])\n  print(t[t.index('\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\''):].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nAnswer'\\\\'')[0])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"\n 2 v9 2026-09-20 8737 1884 Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion. \n 123 ['[Not shown: 1164 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]'] themes ['[Themes in force, 9]']\nRAW {'rung': 2, 'reasons': \"The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp. SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates is a meaningful upside input: applying it to the roughly $250 billion of second-half revenue implied by the $414 billion reference would point to about $464 billion for the year. I discount that forecast because it does not state its underlying estimate and its implied Q4 is much higher than the company has quantified; the resulting central estimate remains near $410 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Supply and competition risks, and NVIDIA's exclusion of China compute revenue from its outlook, temper the upside.\", 'cites': ['e74', 'e2517', 'e2574', 'e4995', 'e2848', 'e3219', 'e2567', 'e3222', 'e4765', 'e976', 'e2510', 'e4556', 'e4557', 'e2562', 'e2572', 'e109', 'e153', 'e4894', 'e4996'], 'cites_views': [], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': \"The rung moves from 3 to 2 because the quantified SemiAnalysis forecast for second-half revenue above analyst estimates lifts the implied FY2027 outcome across $400 billion, while the Q3 guide and first-half reported revenue anchor the estimate. The updated China-authorization theme adds some upside possibility, but NVIDIA's stated no-China-revenue outlook prevents treating that as booked revenue.\", 'reference': 'The market reference supplied by the previous conclusion is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion).', 'implied_outcome': \"Approximately $410 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $146 billion estimated for Q4. The Q4 estimate reflects management's expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp, with a discounted contribution from SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half Data Center revenue would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\"}\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169, e278, e602, e680, e771, e1708, e1716, e1790; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2517 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago\n- e2574 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000073:q2fy27cfocommentary.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported second-quarter FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, driven by the Blackwell Ultra ramp.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue for the second quarter was a record $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago and up 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Blackwell Ultra infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2608; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4995 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA generated $164 billion in Data Center segment revenue in the first six months of FY2027.\n    Quote: The company has generated $164 billion in revenue from its data center segment in the first six months of fiscal 2027.\n- e2848 [party_statement; techcrunch.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided to third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, with some revenue expected from Rubin GPUs.\n    Quote: Nvidia said it expects revenue to reach $108 billion in the third quarter, some of which will come from its next-gen Rubin GPUs.\n- e3219 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source fool.com (Daniel Sparks) 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that FY2027 Q3 Data Center revenue would be $99.9 billion if its share of total revenue stayed at FY2027 Q2's 92.5%.\n    Quote: Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\n- e2567 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expected Vera Rubin to account for approximately 20% of Q3 FY2027 Data Center revenue.\n    Quote: We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2732, e3500, e3974; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3222 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] NVIDIA said hyperscale-business growth should reaccelerate in FY2027 Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n    Quote: And the company said growth in its hyperscale business (sales to the giant cloud computing providers and the biggest consumer internet companies) should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds.\n- e4765 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA management said ACIE, about 45% of Data Center revenue, would drive most sequential Data Center revenue growth in fiscal Q3 2027, while hyperscaler demand could accelerate more in Q4 and FY2028.\n    Quote: ACIE already accounts for about 45% of Nvidia's Data Center revenue. Management expects ACIE to drive most of the sequential revenue growth for Data Center in the third quarter, while hyperscaler demand could pick up more strongly in the fourth quarter and in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4052; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\n- e2510 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Amazon Web Services 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:aws-and-nvidia-to-deliver-2-million-additional-gpus-and-next-generation-infrastructure-for-agentic-and-physical-ai 2026-08-26] AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra GPUs across its global infrastructure in 2027–2028.\n    Quote: AWS plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra GPUs in 2027-2028 across AWS Global Infrastructure, including AI factories.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2536, e2839; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4556 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Anthropic and SpaceX ; from gnews:6490e4ac2a408fad6a7e99a1 2026-09-12] Anthropic entered into a SpaceX cloud-services agreement worth $1.25 billion per month through 2029 for access to compute across supercomputers featuring roughly 325,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Anthropic entered into a cloud services agreement with SpaceX worth $1.25 billion a month through 2029. The deal provides Anthropic with access to compute capacity across SpaceX's supercomputers, Colossus and Colossus II, which feature roughly 325,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4557 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Google Cloud ; from gnews:6490e4ac2a408fad6a7e99a1 2026-09-12] Google Cloud committed $920 million per month through mid-2029 for access to roughly 110,000 Nvidia GPUs, CPUs, memory, and related networking equipment.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Google Cloud committed $920 million a month for access to roughly 110,000 Nvidia GPUs, CPUs, memory, and related networking kits through the middle of 2029.\n- e2562 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA's forward outlook included no China Data Center compute revenue.\n    Quote: And given ongoing geopolitical uncertainty, there is no China data center compute revenue in our forward outlook.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2349, e3676; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2572 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said its available supply supported approximately 70% of expected FY2028 growth, while demand was substantially higher.\n    Quote: we have supply for 70%. We have more supply than 70%, but about 70%. Our demand is much higher than that.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2696, e2739, e3526; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA’s AI computing products.\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they’ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4894 [party_statement; Huawei spokesperson, quoted by TechCrunch; original source Huawei 2026-09-17; from gnews:d95188870b47d6bc96119e87 2026-09-17] Huawei said its Ascend 960DT AI chip is expected to be ready in Q1 2027, ahead of its previously planned Q3 2027 launch.\n    Quote: “The Ascend 960DT is expected to be ready in Q1 2027. Ascend 960 chips are launching ahead of schedule, doubling performance and advancing year by year,” the spokesperson said\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4877; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4996 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-18; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] The author calculated an annualized Data Center revenue run rate of just under $330 billion from NVIDIA’s first-half FY2027 revenue.\n    Quote: That translates into an annual run rate of just under $330 billion for the full year.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The current evidence implies about $385–390 billion: the first two quarters produced about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and Rubin orders lift the Q4 estimate modestly, but the 2027–2028 deployment plans are not all FY2027 revenue, and supply availability limits the upside.\n\n 2 v14 2026-09-20 8729 1884 Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion. \n 119 ['[Not shown: 1210 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]'] themes ['[Themes in force, 9]']\nRAW {'rung': 2, 'reasons': 'The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next year” and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.', 'cites': ['e5056', 'e4702', 'e4377', 'e238', 'e2497', 'e2499', 'e3943', 'e4761', 'e4762', 'e5073', 'e4769', 'e4901'], 'cites_views': [], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung is unchanged, but the current assessment incorporates the newly specified estimate of more than $100 billion of demand left unfulfilled next year as a supply-side counterweight. Its timing and revenue impact are not specific enough to reduce the FY2027 run-rate estimate.', 'reference': \"Market expectation is about $411 billion for FY2027, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\", 'implied_outcome': 'About $419 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion, estimated Q3 of about $113.5 billion (the $110.16 billion guidance high end plus the reported average 3.1% beat), and Q4 of about $127 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.'}\n- e5056 [third_party_view; supercarblondie.com; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:01edb4d1fa96705133b29207 2026-09-19] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA to generate around $411 billion of revenue in FY2027.\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts expect Nvidia to generate around $411 billion in revenue in fiscal 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4097; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4702 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks consensus estimates NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue growth of 87.4% year over year and EPS growth of 93.5%.\n    Quote: The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVDA’s current fiscal 2027 revenues and EPS indicates a year-over-year surge of 87.4% and 93.5%, respectively.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e751 (2026-07-22); both are in force: Zacks consensus estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth of 80% year over year.\n- e4377 [fact; fool.com (Sara Appino); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:be51df202b76715f3d9023f3 2026-09-10] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year.\n    Quote: According to its latest annual report, in the fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026, revenue reached approximately $215.9 billion. This represents a 65.5% increase year over year, driven by intense demand for AI chips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e577; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e238 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\n    Quote: For the first quarter of fiscal 2027, revenue jumped 85% year-over-year (YOY) to $81.6 billion, easily beating analyst expectations.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e223, e248, e268, e967; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2497 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 18% quarter over quarter and 106% year over year.\n    Quote: NVIDIA (NASDAQ: NVDA) today reported revenue for the second quarter ended July 26, 2026, of $96.2 billion, up 18% from the previous quarter and up 106% from a year ago.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2515, e2573, e2666, e2709, e2722, e2853, e4914; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2499 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2518, e2566; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3943 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04] NVIDIA's FY2027 Q3 guidance implied total revenue growth of 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\n    Quote: In the meantime, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint.\n- e4761 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan forecast NVIDIA fiscal Q3 2027 revenue of around $113 billion to $114 billion.\n    Quote: However, I think Nvidia could report third-quarter revenue of around $113 billion to $114 billion.\n- e4762 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan said NVIDIA exceeded the high end of revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, averaging 3.1% above the high end over the past four quarters.\n    Quote: Nvidia has exceeded the high end of its revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters. Over the past four quarters, actual revenue has averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance.\n- e5073 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-09-20; from gnews:e5b9c11e2c8ef9754946a897 2026-09-20] NVIDIA's year-over-year revenue growth rate accelerated for four consecutive quarters, from 62% to 73%, 85%, and 106% in Q2 FY2027.\n    Quote: The company's year-over-year revenue growth rate has now accelerated for four straight quarters, moving from 62% last fall to 73%, then 85%, and now 106%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4599; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4769 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain constrained through FY2028 and said higher memory prices are adding pressure.\n    Quote: Management expects supply to remain constrained through fiscal 2028, while higher memory prices are also adding pressure.\n- e4901 [third_party_view; crn.com; original source crn.com 2026-09-18; from gnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33 2026-09-18] CRN estimated that NVIDIA could leave more than $100 billion of demand for AI infrastructure gear unfulfilled next year because of supply constraints.\n    Quote: This suggests that Nvidia could leave demand unfulfilled for more than $100 billion in AI infrastructure gear, which includes its GPUs as well as other kinds of chips it designs, such as CPUs and DPUs, that are becoming more pervasive in its systems.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The market reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2; the Zacks estimate of 87.4% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $405 billion, also rung 2. The run-rate points somewhat higher: use reported Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, estimate Q3 at about $113.5 billion (the specific $113–114 billion forecast, consistent with NVIDIA's recent history of beating the high end of guidance), then carry roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4, or about $127 billion. That implies approximately $418 billion for FY2027. Supply limits temper the estimate, but the evidence does not quantify a further reduction within FY2027; $418 billion remains above $400 billion and below the $450 billion boundary.\n\n 2 v6 2026-09-19 8595 1856 Thesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures by the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2027, due 2028-03-31): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $800 billion: Combined spending is less than $800 billion. / rung 2 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1.1 trillion. / rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and less than $1.2 trillion. / rung 5 $1.2 trillion or more: Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion. \n 31 ['[Not shown: 56 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]'] themes []\nRAW {'rung': 4, 'reasons': 'The market reference is approximately $1.08 trillion for calendar 2027, which is rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate four-to-five share proxy (about $730 billion of $800 billion) to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five estimate. That is above rung 4’s $1.1 trillion floor but below rung 5’s $1.2 trillion floor. New grid-connection and other execution constraints increase downside risk, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to cross either boundary; repeated reports of the $1.3 trillion estimate do not independently raise it. The share proxy is approximate because the underlying figures do not have perfectly matched scope.', 'cites': ['e1926', 'e2535', 'e3391', 'e3645', 'e3646', 'e4756', 'e4789', 'e4857', 'e4990'], 'cites_views': [], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung and central implied estimate are unchanged, but the new evidence includes a concrete Texas freeze on new grid connections, alongside specific utility hurdles. These add to the downside-risk facts; they do not quantify a spending reduction that would move the implied estimate across a rung boundary.', 'reference': 'Calendar-2027 hyperscaler spending consensus is about $1.08 trillion (e1926), in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion).', 'implied_outcome': 'About $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate $730 billion/$800 billion four-to-five share proxy to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five calendar-2027 CapEx estimate (e2535; repeated in e4756 and later reports).'}\n- e1926 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com, citing Bank of America; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Bank of America said analyst consensus for hyperscalers’ calendar 2027 spending rose from $480 billion in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion in August 2026, an increase of about 127%.\n    Quote: What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1559, e2008; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2535 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said cloud-industry backlog exceeded $2 trillion and top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures were expected to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e465, e3124, e3306, e3307, e3372, e3401, e3405, e3485, e4112; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3391 [third_party_view; Ellen Hazen, F.L. Putnam Investment Management chief market strategist and portfolio manager, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Ellen Hazen ; from gnews:9f03bbc66269c49a5ec8afee 2026-08-30] Ellen Hazen of F.L. Putnam said hyperscaler capital expenditures expected to exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027 may not all be spent, citing labor, permitting, local-opposition and component constraints.\n    Quote: “The biggest risk is whether or not the trillion dollars in hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent — we have labor shortages, we have permitting delays, we have NIMBY increasing in volume, and we have component shortages,” Hazen said.\n- e3645 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Texas officials ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n    Quote: Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n- e3646 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Utilities in other markets ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Utilities in other markets are demanding upfront cash or proof that developers control proposed data-center sites.\n    Quote: Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled.\n- e4756 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Nvidia's own earnings call also highlighted the scale of the spending cycle. The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\nA\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4095, e4780, e4789, e4990; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4789 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged the top five hyperscalers' CapEx at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4756, e4857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4857 [party_statement; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4fbadb3b4cb5846297a34b0a 2026-09-16] NVIDIA estimates top-five hyperscaler capex could reach $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027, up from $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia, for instance, estimates that the capex of the top five hyperscalers could increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027, up from $800 billion this year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4789; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4990 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA projects the top five U.S. hyperscalers’ capital expenditure will reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: Nvidia projects that this figure will jump to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4756; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The market reference is the $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the approximate 730/800 four-to-five share proxy to the $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate. That clears rung 4’s $1.1 trillion floor but remains below rung 5’s $1.2 trillion floor. Power, permitting, labor, component and financing concerns are downside risks, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the central estimate below $1.1 trillion. The share proxy is approximate because the four-company figure is AI-infrastructure spending while the top-five figures are capital expenditures.\n\n 2 v16 2026-09-20 8735 1884 Thesis v16 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $20 billion and at most $25 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total CPU revenue is at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total CPU revenue is at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total CPU revenue is less than $10 billion. \n 39 ['[Not shown: 47 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]'] themes ['[Themes in force, 9]']\nRAW {'rung': 1, 'reasons': 'The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The most likely total is just above $25 billion: the specific FY2027 Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027; carrying that recent Grace run-rate through the fiscal year puts the total slightly above the rung-1 boundary. Vera shipments and full production support the outlook, though the estimate is close to the boundary: Wolfe’s 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD’s performance claims are competitive risks without a quantified revision to NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU demand figure is a headwind, but it is not an explicit cap on NVIDIA’s specific Vera sales outlook.', 'cites': ['e666', 'e668', 'e723', 'e724', 'e794', 'e896', 'e897', 'e2311', 'e2542', 'e2543', 'e2544', 'e2636', 'e2735', 'e3007', 'e3087', 'e4398', 'e4797', 'e5047', 'e5048', 'e5049', 'e5050', 'e5051', 'e5052', 'e5053', 'e5054'], 'cites_views': [], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung remains 1, but later evidence adds support for execution of the FY2027 Vera outlook—shipments to named customers, full production, and an AWS adoption agreement—and supplies further competitive-performance evidence. These change the supporting facts, though they do not establish a quantified change to the FY2027 revenue estimate.', 'reference': 'NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion falls at rung 3.', 'implied_outcome': 'Approximately $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: about $20 billion from Vera standalone CPU sales plus a roughly $5 billion Grace contribution, using Grace’s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the recent run-rate proxy.'}\n- e666 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source Wolfe Research ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 per chip and shipments of about 1.3 million units in calendar 2026.\n    Quote: Wolfe Research said in May that it expected the average selling price to be about $5,000 per Vera chip, and forecast that Nvidia would ship about 1.3 million of them this year.\n- e668 [party_statement; CNBC.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said OpenAI planned to deploy Vera CPUs in large quantities starting in calendar Q3 2026.\n    Quote: Coutand said that Vera was in \"early innings\" of adoption. The company didn't list any major cloud service providers except Oracle on its list of partners but said OpenAI plans to deploy Vera chips in large quantities starting this quarter.\n- e723 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said it delivered Vera chips to OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia said Vera chips were delivered to clients including OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June, according to CNBC .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e664; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e724 [fact; qz.com; original source Hewlett Packard Enterprise ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveiled the Vera-powered ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12 server, with availability expected in fall 2026.\n    Quote: A separate Computex announcement saw Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveil a Vera-powered server , the ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12, targeted at agentic AI orchestration and reinforcement learning workloads, with availability expected in fall 2026.\n- e794 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput.\n    Quote: AMD estimates its EPYC 9965 (Turin) chip delivers approximately 2.4x the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA's Vera baseline in a modeled 100kW deployment, with the next-generation EPYC 6 (Venice) projected to extend that advantage to roughly 3.3x total throughput, according to BofA's characterization of AMD's own estimates.\n- e896 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027.\n    Quote: Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, said Vera “opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before,” adding: “We have visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, setting us up to become the world-leading CPU supplier.”\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e443, e2439; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e897 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales.\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang: “The $20 billion is for standalone CPU.”\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e429; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2311 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6c6c4a4aa3b22bf433133e4d 2026-08-24] NVIDIA expects stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027.\n    Quote: Nvidia is now selling its Vera server CPU as a stand-alone product, and it expects to generate $20 billion in revenue from its sales this year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e429, e4398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2542 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call.\n    Quote: Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3379; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2543 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said Vera CPU shipments were underway to lead partners including OCI and SpaceX AI, with AWS shipments starting in Q2 FY2027, and expected deployment by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    Quote: We expect Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM with shipments already underway to our lead partners, including OCI, SpaceX AI and starting this quarter, AWS.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2522, e2850, e4397; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2544 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: We continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. And based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal '28\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2735, e2794, e3381, e3382, e4797; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2636 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted in the CNBC interview transcript; original source Amazon Web Services, as reported by Jensen Huang ; from extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said Amazon Web Services was going to buy millions of CPUs; no purchase period or exact quantity was specified.\n    Quote: They're going to buy millions of CPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3126; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2735 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said Vera CPU was in full production and expected total CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028, citing customer demand and an improving supply outlook.\n    Quote: The company also said it is in full production of its Vera CPU and expects CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal 2028, based on customer demand and its improving supply outlook.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3007 [fact; CNBC's Jim Cramer; original source Amazon Web Services ; from gnews:fa03217ed7ae3070a82acd79 2026-08-27] Amazon Web Services may buy millions of NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the purchase is conditional and no date was specified.\n    Quote: potentially millions of its new Vera CPUs\n- e3087 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source AWS ; from gnews:d341dec96cd1045ab0e0a92f 2026-08-28] AWS agreed to buy 2 million NVIDIA GPUs and adopt NVIDIA's Vera CPU, with some GPUs integrated with Rubin.\n    Quote: AWS agreed to buy 2 million Nvidia GPUs and adopt its new Vera CPU, some integrated with Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2730; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4398 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA was already shipping Vera CPUs to key customers and expected $20 billion in Vera CPU sales in FY2027.\n    Quote: The company is already shipping this product to key customers and expects to sell $20 billion worth of Vera CPUs this year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2311; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4797 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5c5a96c670714dee86769d87 2026-09-16] NVIDIA estimates the total annual server CPU market at approximately $20 billion.\n    Quote: Nvidia estimates that the total annual server CPU market is worth approximately $20 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5047 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026 testing.\n    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e884; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5048 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2x the per-core performance and 2.24x the platform-level performance of a Vera-based platform in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e887; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5049 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system delivered 3.4x the throughput of a Vera-based platform in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack.\n    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n- e5050 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported 37% higher overall SPEC 2026 performance for 192-core 5th Gen EPYC CPUs and 2.37x performance for 256-core 6th Gen EPYC Venice versus NVIDIA Vera.\n    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n- e5051 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported EPYC 9006 CPUs achieved a 12% per-core performance gain in Agentic AI benchmarks, averaging 20% in the a4 subtests.\n    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n- e5052 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD’s Agentic AI benchmark results showed EPYC Venice up to 2.01x faster per core in stockfish_r, with a lead above 30% in many tests against NVIDIA Vera.\n    Quote: The lead is shown as high as 2.01x in per-core performance tests such as stockfish_r, while many tests show a lead over 30%.\n- e5053 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus 1.2 TB/s of platform bandwidth.\n    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n- e5054 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice’s 96 cores had access to 8% more memory bandwidth per core than NVIDIA Vera’s 88 cores.\n    Quote: Despite the higher core count (96 vs 88), the AMD EPYC Venice cores get access to 8% higher bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera's 88 cores.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU sales; that reference is rung 3. The most likely total is just over $25 billion: the more specific FY2027 Vera statements put Vera CPU sales at about $20 billion, while Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027. The CFO’s broader “total CPU revenue” wording makes the addition less certain, but the separate Vera and Grace figures support treating Grace as additional rather than replacing the Vera outlook. Wolfe’s 2026 shipment estimate implies only about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, and AMD’s performance claims add execution risk; neither provides a quantified revision to the FY2027 outlook or enough to move the central estimate below \n\n 2 v29 2026-09-20 8734 1884 Thesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments are at least $100 billion. \n 35 ['[Not shown: 52 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]'] themes ['[Themes in force, 9]']\nRAW {'rung': 3, 'reasons': \"The reference is Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; that sits in rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion), though its period and relation to outstanding commitments are unclear. The disclosed $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives a $43 billion base. Adding the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure, and risk-adjusting the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions, implies about $53 billion. The newly reported more than $42 billion of equity stakes purchased in the first half of FY2027 confirms an exceptional pace, but it is spending rather than an additional outstanding commitment balance, so I do not add it again. The $30 billion OpenAI investment was announced before the July 26 commitment snapshot and is not counted again. The implied figure remains in rung 3, below the $75 billion boundary for rung 4.\", 'cites': ['e1732', 'e1919', 'e2080', 'e2207', 'e2404', 'e2620', 'e2643', 'e2889', 'e3474', 'e3880', 'e3934', 'e4486', 'e4487', 'e4648', 'e4669', 'e4729', 'e5011'], 'cites_views': [], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': \"The rung and approximate implied outcome remain the same, but the newly supplied report that NVIDIA spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in the first half of FY2027 is a material new fact supporting the forecast's scale. It informs the pace assessment, without being added to the outstanding commitment balance.\", 'reference': \"Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; this is rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion). Its period and whether it measures outstanding commitments are unclear.\", 'implied_outcome': 'About $53 billion: $25 billion of commitments reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, then the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure and a risk-adjusted contribution from the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions.'}\n- e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.\n    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n- e1919 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information 2026-08-07; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] The Information reported on August 7, 2026, that NVIDIA planned to invest up to $3 billion in Lancium: an initial $2 billion for about 20% equity, plus up to $1 billion tied mainly to securing additional grid interconnections.\n    Quote: According to a report from The Information, published on August 7, NVIDIA will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium, a power infrastructure developer and the firm behind the Stargate data center project in Abilene, Texas. The deal is structured in two parts: an initial $2 billion for a roughly 20% equity share in Lancium, followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go\n- e2080 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:a8855077e115780d8a3e8e06 2026-08-18] Bank of America estimated that NVIDIA had committed approximately $300 billion to ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual-value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    Quote: Bank of America estimates that NVIDIA has committed approximately $300 billion in capital to its ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095, e2170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2207 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA and Poolside, as reported by Newcomer based on a Poolside investor letter 2026-08-20; from gnews:1e66644cc2e64e6295fd657a 2026-08-21] NVIDIA’s Poolside transaction includes a $6 billion non-exclusive Model Factory license, a $1 billion investment at a $12 billion pre-money valuation, and the transfer of 109 employees to NVIDIA.\n    Quote: The transaction, first reported by Newcomer on August 20 based on a Poolside investor letter and subsequently confirmed by Bloomberg and The Information, involves a $6 billion payment for a non-exclusive license to Poolside's Model Factory — the system the company uses to build its open-weight Laguna coding model family. In structural terms, a non-exclusive license grants Nvidia the right to utili\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2220, e3340; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2404 [fact; Latitude Media; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:74b684fc710dce162d8f4fe1 2026-08-24] NVIDIA announced a strategic investment in Lancium and a $1.5 billion investment in SB Energy in August 2026.\n    Quote: Also this month, the company announced both a strategic investment in Lancium , a Blackstone-backed AI data center developer integrating grid interconnection, behind-the-meter generation, and storage, and a $1.5-billion investment in SB Energy , which builds “power-first” AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1957; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2620 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of 2026-07-26.\n    Quote: These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2929, e3258, e3477, e3889, e3917; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2643 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted in the CNBC interview transcript; original source Jensen Huang 2026-08-26; from extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA’s large investments in AI labs in the current year were not expected to repeat next year.\n    Quote: And those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat.\n- e2889 [party_statement; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:aec1fae1b903de5c43ba7fdf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said on Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year, in addition to $47.9 billion held in private companies.\n    Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2960; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3474 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:baa07a74334dc91adc7d85bc 2026-08-31] NVIDIA is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is investing $3.5 billion in Taiwan-based MediaTek through convertible bonds, taking an existing AI partnership a step further.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3558; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3880 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:08b1b514a1f4b7067e0dcda4 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said it committed over $40 billion to financing rounds across the AI stack in 2026.\n    Quote: The company has ramped up dealmaking for financing rounds across the AI stack in the previous 12 months, with over $40 billion committed in 2026.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3890; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3934 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA is reportedly discussing an approximately $2.5 billion investment in Thinking Machines Lab; no completion date is stated.\n    Quote: Nvidia is separately in discussions to invest approximately $2.5 billion\n- e4486 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] OpenAI announced $110 billion of new investment at a $730 billion pre-money valuation, including $30 billion each from NVIDIA and SoftBank and $50 billion from Amazon.\n    Quote: In late February, OpenAI announced $110 billion of new investment at a $730 billion pre-money valuation, including $30 billion each from Nvidia and SoftBank Group and $50 billion from Amazon.\n- e4487 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang said an earlier plan for NVIDIA to invest up to $100 billion in OpenAI was never a commitment.\n    Quote: Huang said that same month that an earlier, widely discussed plan for Nvidia to invest up to $100 billion in OpenAI \"was never a commitment.\"\n- e4648 [rumour; Calcalist Tech, citing Reuters; original source Unnamed people familiar with the discussions cited by Reuters ; from gnews:767d795fbdf40bc90ce11e15 2026-09-14] People familiar with discussions told Reuters that Nvidia could invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic’s potential IPO.\n    Quote: Nvidia could invest as much as $10 billion\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4669; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4669 [rumour; InvestingNews; original source Reuters ; from gnews:65814ac898f65c6b360be321 2026-09-14] Reuters reported that NVIDIA is weighing a US$10 billion investment commitment in Anthropic's IPO; no signing date is stated.\n    Quote: According to a Reuters exclusive , the chipmaker is weighing a US$10 billion commitment to the offering, serving as an early backer that could signal strong institutional confidence to public markets preparing to absorb the astronomical valuations and capital demands of frontier artificial intelligence labs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4729 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6261d9e9a1fe8d22c05f5e82 2026-09-15] NVIDIA spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of FY2027, versus about $1.2 billion in the prior-year first half.\n    Quote: Nvidia spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of the fiscal year, up from about $1.2 billion a year earlier.\n- e5011 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2052184744bec4b89ecab744 2026-09-19] NVIDIA has invested in Cohere; Cohere is in talks to raise up to $3 billion.\n    Quote: Nvidia (NVDA) has invested in another AI name most investors have never heard of. This time it's Cohere, a Toronto-based company in talks to raise up to $3 billion.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is Bank of America’s roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA’s equity investments in ecosystem partners, which sits at rung 3; its period and whether it measures outstanding commitments are unclear. The company’s $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives a $43 billion base. Adding the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure, and risk-adjusting the later $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions, implies about $53 billion. That remains in rung 3 and below the $75 billion rung-4 boundary. The $30 billion OpenAI investment was announced before the July 26 commitment snapshot, so I do not count it again; reported equity spending and portfolio value are not additional\n\n 3 v3 2026-09-20 9183 1884 Thesis v3 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion (110% of the $392 billion analyst estimate). / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion (within 10% below the analyst estimate). / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion (more than 20% below the analyst estimate). \n 200 ['[Not shown: 2264 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]'] themes ['[Themes in force, 10]']\nRAW {'rung': 1, 'reasons': 'The market reference is about $411 billion (e4360, e5406), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The evidence-based run-rate is about $419 billion before the additional second-half data-center-compute uplift: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; Q3 is about $113.6 billion using the $108 billion midpoint guidance and the cited 3.1% average beat over the high end; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth pace forward gives about $127 billion for Q4. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute adds about $27–28 billion, implying roughly $446 billion and crossing the rung-1 boundary. The analyst view of 90% Q3 and 65% Q4 year-over-year growth is a counter-signal, but it does not outweigh the guided-growth run-rate and the specific second-half compute tracking. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that would require a sharp Q4 decline after the reported first half and Q3 guidance, so it is inconsistent with the nearer-term run-rate.', 'cites': ['e4360', 'e5406', 'e2754', 'e2715', 'e5089', 'e5088', 'e4190', 'e60', 'e5192', 'e4510'], 'cites_views': [], 'changed': True, 'why_changed': 'The rung remains 1, but newly supplied Q3/Q4 growth estimates add a meaningful counter-signal, and theme v10 has been updated since the prior judgement. The implied outcome remains around $446 billion because the guided-growth run-rate and the second-half compute uplift still place the forecast above $431.2 billion.', 'reference': 'Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4360, e5406), placing the reference in rung 2.', 'implied_outcome': 'About $446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, approximately $113.6 billion for Q3 using the guidance midpoint and the cited average beat, and approximately $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, then adding about $27–28 billion for the estimated 20% second-half data-center-compute uplift.'}\n- e4360 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e 2026-09-06] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA total revenue of $411 billion for FY2027.\n    Quote: For FY 2027 (ending in January 2027), Wall Street analysts expect $411 billion in revenue.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e25 (); both are in force: Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA total revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\n- e5406 [third_party_view; supercarblondie.com; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:01edb4d1fa96705133b29207 2026-09-19] Wall Street analysts expected NVIDIA total revenue of around $411 billion for FY2027.\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts expect Nvidia to generate around $411 billion in revenue in fiscal 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2754 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported revenue of $177.837 billion for the first six months of FY2027, versus $90.805 billion in the same period of FY2026.\n    Quote: Revenue\n$\n96,221\n$\n46,743\n$\n177,837\n$\n90,805\n- e2715 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 total revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\n    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2740, e2792, e3454; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5089 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan calculated that NVIDIA's actual revenue averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance over the past four quarters.\n    Quote: Over the past four quarters, actual revenue has averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance.\n- e5088 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan estimated NVIDIA could report fiscal Q3 2027 revenue of about $113 billion to $114 billion.\n    Quote: However, I think Nvidia could report third-quarter revenue of around $113 billion to $114 billion.\n- e4190 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04] NVIDIA's FY2027 Q3 guidance implied 12% sequential total revenue growth at the midpoint.\n    Quote: In the meantime, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint.\n- e60 [third_party_view; CNBC, reporting SemiAnalysis’s projection; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue for the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e140, e1116; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5192 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:ea4633ed3e4ad9d71383673f 2026-09-17] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue growth of 90% in FY2027 Q3 and 65% in FY2027 Q4.\n    Quote: For the remainder of fiscal 2027, Wall Street analysts expect 90% growth in Q3 and 65% growth in Q4.\n- e4510 [party_statement; Jon Quast; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] NVIDIA guided to 70% revenue growth for the coming year, relative to the revenue it is reporting this year.\n    Quote: It's guiding for 70% revenue growth on top of what it's reporting this year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The market reference is about $411 billion (e5406), which is rung 2. The evidence-based run-rate remains above the rung-1 boundary: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion (e2754); applying the observed 3.1% average beat over the high end of guidance to the $108 billion Q3 guide implies roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 (e2715, e5088, e5089); carrying the 12% sequential growth pace forward implies about $127 billion for Q4 (e4190). That gives roughly $418 billion before SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift to second-half data-center compute, which adds about $27–28 billion when applied only to that portion, for an implied total near $446 billion (e60)—above $431.2 billion. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026’s $215.9 billion, a bearish counter-signal, but it is l\n\n 3 v41 2026-09-20 9166 1884 Thesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion. \n 15 [] themes ['[Themes in force, 10]']\nRAW {'rung': 3, 'reasons': 'The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 expectation of more than double its $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, implying more than $40 billion and placing the reference in rung 2. I discount that forward target to roughly $38–39 billion: the evidence shows broad intended deployment and an Anthropic evaluation, but not Vera shipment volume in hand or a sized customer commitment; AMD’s reported performance comparisons add execution and competitiveness risk. That estimate remains below the $40 billion rung-2 boundary.', 'cites': ['e3660', 'e4687', 'e4689', 'e4691', 'e4692', 'e4764', 'e4856', 'e4857', 'e5044', 'e5399', 'e5400', 'e5401', 'e5402', 'e5403', 'e5404'], 'cites_views': [], 'changed': False, 'why_changed': 'The rung and implied estimate remain the same as the previous conclusion. The repeated more-than-double guidance does not add a new quantified forecast; the Anthropic evaluation is not a sized commitment, and the performance comparisons reinforce rather than change the existing execution-risk assessment.', 'reference': 'NVIDIA management’s stated expectation that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the $20 billion FY2027 expectation implies more than $40 billion, which is rung 2.', 'implied_outcome': 'Roughly $38–39 billion for FY2028: the stated more-than-double expectation points above $40 billion, but is discounted for a forward Vera target without shipment volume in hand, with the competitive evidence adding execution risk.'}\n- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5443; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5444; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 expectation of more than double its $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which implies more than $40 billion and sits at rung 2. I discount that forecast because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand; the new AMD performance comparisons add execution and competitiveness risk, while broad deployment expectations and market-growth estimates support demand but do not quantify NVIDIA’s share. I therefore imply roughly $38–39 billion, below the $40 billion boundary and within rung 3.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
