. This remains below the $50 billion rung-4 threshold. The company-based reference is approximately $43 billion: $25 billion of commitments as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027. That reference sits in rung 3 ($30 billion to less than $50 billion).\n- e2841 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26, 2026.\n    Quote: We have made, and may continue to make, investments and commitments in our ecosystem to enhance our growth opportunities, cultivate our ecosystem, and strengthe\n- e3123 [party_statement; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:aec1fae1b903de5c43ba7fdf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of FY2027, in addition to $47.9 billion in existing private-company holdings.\n    Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal yea\n3 v7 capex 44 0 dates []\n3 v41 vera 21 7 dates ['09-13', '09-15', '09-16', '09-17', '09-18', '09-19', '09-20']\n8134 2026-09-13 High-$30 billions (roughly $38–39 billion): the more-than-$40-billion management target is discounted because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand. That estimate remains below the $40 billion rung-2 boundary. NVIDIA management's expectation that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double FY2027's $20 billion level implies more than $40 billion, which is rung 2. No separate market consensus is supplied.\n- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CP\n- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one cla\n9166 2026-09-20 Roughly $38–39 billion for FY2028: the stated more-than-double expectation points above $40 billion, but is discounted for a forward Vera target without shipment volume in hand, with the competitive evidence adding execution risk. NVIDIA management’s stated expectation that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the $20 billion FY2027 expectation implies more than $40 billion, which is rung 2.\n- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CP\n- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one cla\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'dependency|numer|ladder|one home|guidance|evidence in view| D198|arithmetic|no evidence|reference' docs/prd/06-views.md | cut -c1-340; rg -n 'close_due|round_of_judgements|changed_views|due_day' src/hivemind/pull* src/hivemind/round.py scripts/replay.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'dependency|numer|ladder|one home|guidance|evidence in view| D198|arithmetic|no evidence|reference' docs/prd/06-views.md | cut -c1-340; rg -n 'close_due|round_of_judgements|changed_views|due_day' src/hivemind/pull* src/hivemind/round.py scripts/replay.py\"","aggregated_output":"3:> **2026-11-05 decision: incomplete research is still research.** Alex: “down the road, we can always have agents do research individually to get the actual dates and information that didn't go in while this time it was going in the thesis DB … alleviate these options as conditional. Don't hardgate it based on the manual code”. A worthw\n7:> Status: foundation frozen, October 20, 2026. D149 adds extraction-owned grouping; D165 puts it in the default pipeline. D169: the matching step runs on Jev, with the collision judge for a candidate whose list holds a thesis of its own subject and measure (D190, D258); the replay default is `poc-v117` without seeded questions or separa\n12:A thesis records an investment question and its evidence. A date, ladder and other research details are conditional on the information available; missing details can be researched later. Evidence moves its state when it can be judged. Search then compare candidates with existing theses to merge, continue, create or skip; compare eviden\n16:- Clear structures connect theses, ladders and evidence to source items.\n18:- A fixed, verifiable ladder states in advance what would disprove the view, analogous to explicit analyst scenarios or predefined investment exit conditions. Do not change it after observing the outcome.\n19:- The model judges from the evidence in view (the arrivals since its fifth most recent judgement and the basis its last ten judgements cited; the rest stays in force, D198) and dependency conclusions, rather than adjusting its previous score. A revision follows statement date, so a late older statement is stored as one more piece and n\n20:- A theme's outcome can help one asset and harm another. Theme ladders describe outcomes; asset dependencies specify their own direction.\n22:- Search finds plausible matches and the model compares their meaning. A match inherits the existing ladder and due date; a merely similar question is new. D155's current POC additionally gives code ownership of fully determined duplicate identity, with its limits in the improvement plan.\n23:- Evidence needs the same search-then-compare discipline. Otherwise February's 12% capex guidance and April's 25% revision sit side by side unrelated, forcing every judgement to rediscover which is current; the comparison records the revision, and both stay in force (D202).\n36:│   └─ Thesis → A question + a ladder + a due date (Immutable once written. Five rungs, or two for yes/no. When matching opens a thesis, only one may be open for the same subject and focus at a time.)\n37:│       ├─ State → The thesis's current ladder rung (Evidence pushes towards either end; the model forecasts the rung most likely at the due date, against a reference (D260). A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it.)\n39:│       ├─ Dependency → Another thesis (Direction specifies which end of the other thesis is bullish for an asset thesis, or whether the two ladders have the same or opposite orientation for a theme thesis.)\n59:<!-- generated: ladder -->\n61:Two kinds of ladder (Illustrative ranges)\n62:├─ Asset thesis: numeric → NVDA FY2027 China revenue versus FY2026 | Five rungs describing NVDA's business impact (Due on the FY2027 annual earnings date)\n76:1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed. Decided\n77:2. Each rung is verifiable: a numeric interval or event stage. Rungs must be exhaustive and non-overlapping, so exactly one applies at expiry. For example: up at least 50%; up 10% to under 50%; change under 10% either way; down 10% to under 80%; down at least 80%. Every boundary belongs to one rung. “Up a lot/a little” cannot be checke\n78:3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.\n79:4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.\n82:<!-- /generated: ladder -->\n89:| State | Our judgement of the thesis's current rung; evidence pushes toward either end | The model judges the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, against a reference (below). It reads the evidence in view, the arrivals since its fifth most recent judgement and the basis its last ten judgements cited, the r\n91:- Every judgement states its basis in a fixed response shape: evidence IDs such as e12 and e15, plus dependency thesis IDs such as #1 when their changes triggered rejudgement.\n92:- Code checks cited IDs against the request that was sent: evidence must be among the pieces shown to this thesis (D198); cited theses must be its dependencies. Accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise record the reason and ask for one corrected judgement. If that also fails, use normal failure handling: retry from this step, then hum\n93:- Each judgement also declares whether the conclusion changed and explains why, comparing with the state and reasons before this round, including during retries. That earlier conclusion is for comparison, not incremental scoring. A rung change or a material change in the supporting facts/numbers counts: AMZN capex guidance moving from \n95:- The rung is a forecast against a reference (D260): the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, judged against what the market expects (consensus, guidance, the stated plan or deadline). The judgement names the reference and its rung and the outcome the evidence implies (a figure or date derived from reported \n104:| Party statement | Company guidance, management statements and official speeches | The strongest input to the forecast |\n118:B depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)\n122:│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)\n130:- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.\n131:- Dependencies and the theme block are the two ways theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. The theme block (D191, D195) shows every open, judged theme thesis to every asset thesis's judgement, one to all, and the judgement states which way each theme it weighed pushe\n132:- Count a statement once. A statement about A is A's evidence, including when two candidates arise from one item. B learns it through A's conclusion. Daily filing gives a statement one home: the narrowest open thesis it bears on, as that thesis's own figure, step or estimate or through the demand, supply, competition or pricing behind\n133:- Rejudge an open thesis when its inputs change: new evidence, replacement, a new link to existing evidence, a dependency declaring a changed conclusion, or (for an asset thesis) a theme thesis declaring one. Additional source references alone do not count. Binding an awaited A or moving to its successor also changes B's inputs. Close\n134:- The matching call chooses a new thesis's dependencies, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction (D51). Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Jev, the default matcher, is not asked for them, so new theses do not receive dependencies thr\n143:- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.\n153:| Chain | Succession: Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 → 2028 | Revision: AMZN capex guidance 12% growth → 25%, both in force |\n154:| What counts as current | Open theses on the chain; numeric theses may have one for this year and one for next year | Every stored piece stays in force; revisions form a chain the judge reads (D202) |\n166:  ↓ 2 Receive evidence (Code): Retries do not duplicate evidence. A revised input updates only its own source reference. A revised retelling does not alter the original evidence. If the revision is from the original source, compare its statements normally: a changed statement is stored as a revision of the old one, both in force (D202\n168:  ↓ 4 Match (Model): The matcher does not read the original item. Jev answers, with a score, each candidate's relation to every thesis on its list (shown with its subject, question, focus, ladder, due date and open/closed state) and each piece of evidence's relation to every stored piece on its list under each linked thesis; code turn\n169:  ↓ 5 Validate (Code): Destination IDs must be in the shown lists or the relevant thesis's evidence. Merge only into open theses. New subjects must resolve within the universe using classification's identifier rules; otherwise skip. Dependencies must reference shown theses or an explicit awaited chain/year and remain acyclic. Return i\n170:  ↓ 6 Write to the library (Code): Resolve extraction's group associations to final theses and save them in this step. Each subject has its own writing queue; write theme theses before asset theses. Save new theses with their dependency edges. Store every incoming piece once, as its own record with all its links (D204): a repeat also \n171:  ↓ 7 Run the day's rejudgement round (Model): Once a day, after the day's last item (D63), rejudge only open theses whose inputs changed that day: new or newly linked evidence (a repeat or a revision is a new piece), or a changed dependency conclusion, newly created awaited thesis or successor. Judge dependencies first, then theme th\n180:| Merge into existing | The candidate concerns the same focus or a part of it, such as H20 China revenue within China revenue. Merge only into open theses; closed theses can only be succeeded. | Compare its evidence within the target thesis and preserve grouping under this section's rules. Use the existing ladder and due date. |\n182:| New thesis | Distinct or merely similar: Blackwell export licences and H20 export licences are different questions | Create a thesis with its ladder and due date fixed from now on |\n196:| Replace | A later account of the same matter: a speaker revises guidance, forecast or event progress; an official confirms/denies a rumour; or the original source revises its statement | Store the later statement beside the earlier one and record the pair as a revision, ordered by statement date; both stay in force (D202); rejudge. \n213:│   └─ #2 NVDA asset thesis → FY2026 China revenue: numeric (Evidence: expected charges up to approximately $5.5 billion (party statement); the licence theme #1 reaches it through the theme block (an explicit dependency, easing bullish, would be chosen in matching, D51, not yet on the default Jev path))\n227:| February 5 call: capex guidance up 12% | New evidence; none previously under this thesis | Add g1 | Rejudge |\n228:| February 6 Reuters report retells the guidance | Repeat of g1 | Add r1, recorded as the same claim as g1 | Rejudge; one claim, conclusion unchanged |\n230:| April 30 call: guidance revised to 25% growth | Replace g1 | Add g2 beside g1, both in force; the revision g1 → g2 is recorded | Rejudge, reading “12% → 25%” |\n235:After six months the thesis may have dozens of evidence items, all in force. A rejudgement reads those in view, the arrivals since its fifth most recent judgement and the basis its last ten judgements cited (D198), each revision beside the piece it revises: company guidance of 25% growth revising February's 12%, Morgan Stanley's 27% f\n260:| Shown to the model | Ranked top N thesis matches per candidate; top N evidence matches within each thesis; extraction block-5 theses and same-item new theses available for dependency selection |\n272:├─ Open → Rejudge when its inputs change, under the rejudgement rule (When matching opens theses, only one for the same focus at a time; numeric quantities may have one each for this year and next year)\n306:| Source | Upstream ID, category, publication day and location. A number repeated in text and tables within one item adds references to the same piece; a retelling or repeated statement in another item is its own piece, with the pair recorded as the same claim (D204). |\n318:- One queue writes each thesis: the theme's queue for theme theses; the company, cryptocurrency or commodity queue for asset theses. Within an item, write themes before assets. Rejudgement order follows dependencies under the round rule. Other queues send requests, such as dependency changes or replacement links, to the owning queue w\n320:- A day's items form one round: each item's evidence links, thesis creation, succession and dependencies are written in sequence order, and after the day's last item the round rejudges in dependency order (D63). Dependants read conclusions freshly judged in that round. Judge each thesis once. A widely depended-on thesis such as the Fe\n321:- Append-only records: questions, ladders and due dates are immutable. Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labels append records with sequence numbers.\n323:- A revised input changes only its own source reference. Revising a retelling does not change original evidence. For an original-source revision, a changed statement is stored as a revision of the old one, both in force; nothing is retired (D202). Rejudge each open thesis linked to affected statements once. A retelling-reference chang\n334:| Extraction | Receive sourced evidence and candidates. Before each item, supply the item's subjects' open questions and all open theme questions with takes lines, as committed before the item; both experimental split calls share that snapshot (D375). No states, evidence, dependency links or list of stored groups. |\n343:- Every thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date; five rungs or two for yes/no, with exhaustive, non-overlapping, verifiable outcomes.\n349:- Rejudge only open theses with changed inputs: new or newly linked evidence (repeats and revisions are new pieces), or changed dependency conclusions. Memo-derived theses without evidence remain unjudged. Cite evidence and relevant dependency IDs, and declare conclusion change. First judgement and rung changes must declare a change. \n353:- Append knowledge in arrival order. Store a late older statement as one more piece with its true date and no revision (D202); accept newly learned matters with their true dates. A revised retelling changes only its own source reference. An original-source revision's changed statements are stored as revisions, both in force; nothing i\n376:- D38–D40: question/ladder/due-date structure, state, evidence links/tiers, dependencies/succession, round-based intake, history and matching labels.\n377:- D41/D42: align frozen modules with the rebuilt library, separate evidence/candidates, compare within-thesis evidence, and permit binary ladders; the extraction request they described (with dependencies) became open theses with their takes lines (D211, D264). D375 supersedes D251's day-start timing with the latest committed questions\n379:- D46/D49: one state, concrete ladders, ordinary ingestion of decisive evidence, years based on publication day, top-N whole-library search.\n380:- D47: consistent terms, evidence comparison after candidate resolution, dependency references in judgement reasons and Reuters example correction.\n382:- D51: rejudge on changed inputs, explicit conclusion-change declaration, dependency selection within matching, seven intake steps and no consensus calculation.\n386:- D58: clarify dependency direction, waiting, due-date scope, matching-time uniqueness, triggers and manual-operation records; freeze all except final verdict/pull mode.\n395:- D149 requirement: organise theses by the continuing business process they discuss, called a research group. Vera Rubin readiness and customer deployment can share a group while shipment dates, SK factory launch and OpenAI deployment retain separate questions, ladders and due dates. A group may start with one thesis; a thesis may bel\n397:- The full-text call shows no list of stored groups; an open thesis's takes line names the groups it belongs to (D264). During that call, extraction declares named and scoped groups. A candidate that tracks an ongoing matter (a product rollout and its deployments, a deal, a policy process, an investment or demand cycle) cites one or m\n400:- Groups do not copy evidence. Read sources through members' real evidence links, filtered by the requested sequence and excluding evidence a legacy replacement record had retired by then (no new one is written, D202). No link means no evidence. A new member or an existing thesis change flag updates its groups. A repeated quote is sto\n402:- Historical implementation reference: D147's isolated grouping prototype and its field names are preserved in the research records. D165 and D169 subsequently integrated grouping into the default pipeline. See docs/poc/pipeline-plan.md for the current implementation and measured limits; the old prototype does not establish large-libr\nrg: src/hivemind/round.py: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nsrc/hivemind/pull.py:11:def closing_reason(conn, view_id, due_day, day):\nsrc/hivemind/pull.py:14:        return f'due {due_day}, closed on {day} with no judgement: {NO_DATA}'\nsrc/hivemind/pull.py:16:    return f'due {due_day}, frozen on {day} at rung {last[\"rung\"]} {label[\"label\"] if label else \"\"}'.rstrip()\nsrc/hivemind/pull.py:31:    due = [row for row in conn.execute('select view_id, due_day from views where due_day <= ? order by created_seq', (day,)).fetchall()\nsrc/hivemind/pull.py:36:        store.close_view(conn, row['view_id'], closing_reason(conn, row['view_id'], row['due_day'], day), 'pull', seq)\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
