)}\ndef figures(t,unit):\n out=[]\n for m in FIG.finditer(t or '\\\\'''\\\\''):\n  u=m['\\\\''unit'\\\\''].lower()\n  if (u in MULT)\"'!=(unit=='\"'\\\\''USD'\\\\''):continue\n  mult=MULT.get(u,1)\n  if m['\\\\''sign'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''-'\\\\'','\\\\''−'\\\\''):mult=-mult\n  for g in ('\\\\''lo'\\\\'','\\\\''hi'\\\\''):\n   if m[g]:out.append(D(m[g].replace('\\\\'','\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))*mult)\n return out\ndef snippets(ed):\n return [re.split(r'\\\\''\\\\n    (?:It replaces|Opinion, not verified:)'\\\\'',b,1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1] for b in ed.values()]\ndef prior_text(t):\n return t.split('\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nAnswer per'\\\\'',1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1]\ndef tag(x,pattern): return bool(re.search(pattern,x or '\\\\'''\\\\'',re.I))\ndef record(i):\n v=db(i);a=db(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\'')\n return (v,a,v.execute(\\\"select j.*,k.unit from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.unit in ('\\\\''USD'\\\\'','\\\\''percent'\\\\'') order by judgement_id\\\"))\nfor i,caps in {1:['\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\'','\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\''],2:['\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''v17'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\''],3:['\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'','\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v44'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\'']}.items():\n v,a,_=record(i)\n for id in caps:\n  sel=[]\n  for j in v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id'\\\\'',(id,)):\n   o=j['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''];r=j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\'']\n   if id in ({1:['\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\''],2:['\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\''],3:['\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'']}[i]):\n    if tag(o+r,r'\\\\''\\\\b(residu\\\\w*|back(?:ing|ed|s)? out|remainder|leav(?:es|ing)|requiring|requires|subtract\\\\w*)\\\\b'\\\\'') and tag(o+r,r'\\\\''Q4|fourth quarter'\\\\''):\n     sel.append((j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''][5:],j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],o,r))\n   elif i==2 and id in ('\\\\''v17'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\''):\n    if j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\'']>='\\\\''2026-08-26'\\\\'' and any(D(70)==x for x in figures(o+r,'\\\\''percent'\\\\'')) and any(x in figures(o+r,'\\\\''USD'\\\\'') for x in ([D(367),D(366.9)] if id=='\\\\''v21'\\\\'' else [D(396),D(673)])):\n      sel.append((j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''][5:],j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],o,r))\n  if sel:\n   print('\\\\''DRAW'\\\\'',i,id,len(sel),[(j[0],j[1]) for j in sel])\n   for j in sel:print(j[0],j[1],j[2] if (i==2 and id in ('\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''v17'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')) else j[2]+'\\\\'' :: '\\\\''+ '\\\\'' '\\\\''.join(x for x in j[3].split('\\\\''. '\\\\'') if tag(x,r'\\\\''\\\\b(residu\\\\w*|back(?:ing|ed|s)? out|remainder|leav(?:es|ing)|requiring|requires|subtract\\\\w*)\\\\b'\\\\'')))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"DRAW 1 v4 1 [('07-24', 1782)]\n07-24 1782 About $425 billion: carry the Q1 actual and Q2 guidance run-rate forward, then apply the existing estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above Wall Street expectations only to that portion of revenue. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold. :: Carrying forward the evidence-implied estimate of about $425 billion—based on Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance carried forward, with the existing SemiAnalysis uplift applied only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute—leaves the forecast about $6.2 billion short of rung 1’s $431.2 billion boundary\nDRAW 1 v8 9 [('08-26', 5093), ('08-27', 5304), ('08-31', 6166), ('09-02', 6413), ('09-03', 6604), ('09-04', 6817), ('09-11', 7763), ('09-17', 8613), ('09-18', 8816)]\n08-26 5093 About $400.4 billion: Morningstar's $361 billion estimate less actual Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue ($75.25 billion and $89.0 billion) gives a $196.75 billion H2 baseline; applying SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 upside forecast gives about $236.1 billion for H2. Q3 guidance implies roughly $99–$100 billion of Data Center revenue from the recent segment mix, leaving about $136 billion implied for Q4. :: The implied estimate is about $400.4 billion: start with Morningstar's $361 billion annual estimate, subtract reported Q1 revenue of $75.25 billion and reported Q2 revenue of $89.0 billion to get a $196.75 billion H2 baseline, then apply SemiAnalysis's forecast that H2 will exceed analyst estimates by 20%, yielding about $236.1 billion for H2 and $400.35 billion for the year NVIDIA's $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide, with recent Data Center mix around 92%–93%, implies roughly $99–$100 billion of Q3 Data Center revenue; the annual estimate therefore requires about $136 billion in Q4\n08-27 5304 Approximately $400 billion for FY2027: reported Q1 and Q2 total $164.25 billion; applying the recent 92.5% Data Center mix to NVIDIA's $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide implies about $100 billion of Q3 Data Center revenue. Applying SemiAnalysis's 20% second-half upside forecast to the prior $196.75 billion H2 estimate implies about $236 billion for H2 and roughly $400 billion for the year, including an aggressive implied Q4 of about $136 billion. :: Combining that with the reported first half and SemiAnalysis's forecast for second-half revenue 20% above estimates implies roughly $400 billion for FY2027, but requires about $136 billion in Q4—an aggressive ramp\n08-31 6166 Q1 and Q2 actuals total $164.25 billion; the $108 billion Q3 companywide guide at the latest 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $99.9 billion. Taking Morningstar’s $361 billion estimate less actual H1 gives a $196.75 billion H2 baseline; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $236.1 billion of H2 and approximately $400.35 billion for FY2027. :: Morningstar’s annual estimate therefore leaves $196.75 billion as its implied H2 baseline; SemiAnalysis’s forecast of H2 revenue 20% above analyst estimates lifts H2 to about $236.1 billion and FY2027 to about $400.35 billion\n09-02 6413 Q1–Q2 reported revenue is $164.25 billion. The $361 billion FY reference implies $196.75 billion of H2 estimates; applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside gives $236.1 billion for H2 and about $400.35 billion for FY2027. Q3 is about $99.9 billion if the $108 billion total-revenue guide is combined with the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, leaving roughly $136.2 billion implied for Q4. :: That H2 figure requires roughly $136.2 billion in Q4: aggressive, but the Q3 companywide guide implies about $99.9 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest 92.5% mix, and NVIDIA says hyperscale growth should reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds\n09-03 6604 Q1–Q2 actual revenue totals $164.25 billion. Starting from Morningstar’s $361 billion estimate leaves $196.75 billion of baseline H2 estimates; applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside gives $236.1 billion for H2 and about $400.35 billion for FY2027. At the recent 92.5% mix, Q3 guidance implies roughly $99.9 billion of Data Center revenue, leaving about $136.2 billion for Q4. :: The supplied run-rate calculation is still just above rung 1: Q1–Q2 revenue totals $164.25 billion; subtracting that from $361 billion gives $196.75 billion of baseline H2 estimates, and SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies $236.1 billion for H2 and $400.35 billion for FY2027 That requires about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the latest 92.5% mix and $136.2 billion in Q4\n09-04 6817 About $400 billion, marginally above the rung-1 boundary. Q1–Q2 revenue totals $164.25 billion; Q3 is approximately $99.9 billion using the $108 billion guidance midpoint and Q2’s 92.5% Data Center mix. The earlier H2 estimate baseline of $196.75 billion, uplifted 20% in SemiAnalysis’s H2 forecast, implies about $236.1 billion for H2 and roughly $400.4 billion for FY2027. :: That leaves about $135.9 billion for Q4 to reach $400 billion\n09-11 7763 About $418 billion: the carried H2 analyst baseline of roughly $212 billion, increased 20% per SemiAnalysis’s forecast, gives about $254 billion for H2; adding Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and $89.0 billion gives about $418 billion for FY2027. Q3’s $108 billion total-revenue midpoint at a 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $99.9 billion, so the H2 estimate requires roughly $154 billion in Q4. :: The Q3 outlook and recent mix imply about $99.9 billion of Q3 Data Center revenue, leaving roughly $154 billion for Q4 under that H2 forecast—a steep sequential increase\n09-17 8613 About $418 billion: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the latest 92.5% mix to the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue outlook implies about $99.9 billion for Q3. The carried H2 estimate, uplifted by the cited 20% SemiAnalysis view, is about $254 billion for Q3–Q4 combined, leaving roughly $154 billion for Q4 and putting the FY2027 total above $400 billion. :: \n09-18 8816 About $395 billion: $164.25 billion reported in the first half plus about $99.9 billion implied for Q3 leaves roughly $131 billion for Q4. That represents a substantial Q4 step-up consistent with expected reacceleration, but below the prior forecast’s implied Q4 contribution. :: \nDRAW 2 v9 1 [('08-27', 5139)]\n08-27 5139 Approximately $417 billion: the $372 billion reference less Q1 actual revenue of $75.25 billion and the Q2 estimate of $85.8 billion implies about $211 billion of H2 estimates; applying SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 outperformance forecast gives about $253 billion for H2. Adding Q1 actual revenue and Q2 actual revenue of $89 billion yields about $417 billion. The $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide, applied to the recent roughly 92.5% Data Center share, implies about $100 billion of Q3 Data Center revenue, leaving the forecast dependent on a strong Q4. :: The Q3 total-revenue guide is consistent with roughly $100 billion of Data Center revenue using the recent segment mix; the implied second-half total requires a substantial Q4 ramp, so the estimate relies on SemiAnalysis's specific second-half forecast rather than treating the guide alone as sufficient\nDRAW 2 v21 14 [('08-28', 5474), ('08-29', 5604), ('08-30', 5751), ('08-31', 5969), ('09-01', 6185), ('09-04', 6598), ('09-06', 6862), ('09-07', 6967), ('09-08', 7064), ('09-12', 7678), ('09-14', 7928), ('09-18', 8505), ('09-19', 8628), ('09-20', 8730)]\n08-28 5474 Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion totals about $285.8 billion. Carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth over Q2 into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion in Q4, or about $407 billion for FY2027—approximately 88.5% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\n08-29 5604 About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.5% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus the $108 billion Q3 guide total $285.8 billion, and extending Q3's approximately 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion. The CFO-attributed 70% full-year growth guidance is a lower anchor, implying roughly $367 billion.\n08-30 5751 Using reported Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion, and carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion in Q4 and roughly $407 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is approximately 88.5% growth; NVIDIA's 70% full-year outlook is a lower anchor, implying about $367 billion.\n08-31 5969 Carrying Q3's approximately 12.3% sequential growth forward one quarter implies Q4 revenue of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion, roughly 88.5% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion. NVIDIA's separate 70% full-year outlook implies about $367 billion; both implied outcomes are above the 60% rung-1 threshold.\n09-01 6185 About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88.5% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion. This uses reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and carries the roughly 12.3% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward once to estimate Q4 at about $121 billion.\n09-04 6598 About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with Q4 estimated at about $121 billion by carrying Q3's 12% sequential growth forward.\n09-06 6862 About $407B of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.5% growth over FY2026's $215.94B: reported Q1 and Q2 plus the $108B Q3 midpoint, with Q4 estimated by carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward.\n09-07 6967 About $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: reported Q1 $81.6B plus Q2 $96.2B, Q3 guidance of $108B, and Q4 at 12% sequential growth from Q3 (about $121.0B).\n09-08 7064 Using reported FY2027 Q1 revenue of $81.6B and Q2 revenue of $96.2B, Q3 midpoint guidance of $108B, and carrying forward the stated 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance, Q4 is about $121.0B. That implies FY2027 revenue of about $406.8B versus FY2026's $215.94B, or approximately 88.4% growth (rung 1); the separate 70% full-year outlook implies about $367B, also rung 1.\n09-12 7678 About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or 88.5% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to the $108 billion Q3 guide, then carry Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 (about $121 billion). This is a run-rate extrapolation, not an assertion that Q4 revenue is already established.\n09-14 7928 FY2026 revenue was $215.94 billion; Q1 and Q2 FY2027 revenue totaled $177.8 billion, and the $108 billion Q3 guide brings the first three quarters to $285.8 billion. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide into Q4 gives about $121 billion, or roughly $407 billion for FY2027 and 88.5% year-over-year growth.\n09-18 8505 Carry the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4: $108 billion × 1.12 is about $121 billion. Adding that to FY2027 Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion yields about $407 billion; versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is approximately 88% growth.\n09-19 8628 FY2026 revenue was $215.94 billion; FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue plus the Q3 guidance midpoint total $285.8 billion. Carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives roughly $121 billion for Q4, about $407 billion for FY2027, or approximately 88% year-over-year growth.\n09-20 8730 Q1 and Q2 revenue plus the Q3 guidance midpoint total $285.8 billion; extending the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion, or approximately $407 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 88% growth; the CFO's approximately 70% full-year outlook is a lower check, implying about $367 billion, but also falls in rung 1.\nDRAW 2 v17 11 [('08-26', 4931), ('08-27', 5124), ('08-28', 5466), ('08-29', 5588), ('09-06', 6864), ('09-07', 6977), ('09-08', 7065), ('09-09', 7177), ('09-17', 8316), ('09-19', 8625), ('09-20', 8725)]\n08-26 4931 Approximately 70% FY2028 total revenue growth, NVIDIA's preliminary, supply-constrained outlook (e2568). Applied to the $396 billion FY2027 consensus baseline, that implies about $673 billion of FY2028 revenue (e2697), consistent with roughly 70% growth and within rung 1.\n08-27 5124 Approximately 70% FY2028 revenue growth, from NVIDIA's preliminary outlook (e2532). Applied to the $396 billion FY2027 consensus estimate, that implies about $673 billion in FY2028 revenue (e2697), or roughly 70% growth.\n08-28 5466 Approximately 70% FY2028 growth, from NVIDIA’s stated outlook. Applying that growth to the cited $396 billion FY2027 consensus revenue gives about $673 billion for FY2028.\n08-29 5588 NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook implies about $673 billion of revenue using the $396 billion FY2027 consensus base ($396 billion × 1.70). The outlook is explicitly supply-constrained, so I use the guided growth rate rather than the higher unconstrained demand indication.\n09-06 6864 Approximately 70% FY2028 revenue growth, or about $673 billion of revenue applying 70% growth to the roughly $396 billion FY2027 consensus base. A 70% growth outcome clears rung 1's 60% threshold.\n09-07 6977 Approximately 70% FY2028 total revenue growth, based on NVIDIA's direct supply-constrained outlook. Applying 70% to the roughly $396 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $673 billion of FY2028 revenue; the separate approximately 77% next-fiscal-year report is less directly specified for FY2028 and does not replace that outlook.\n09-08 7065 The direct FY2028 outlook is approximately 70% growth. Applying that rate to the cited roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $673–675 billion of FY2028 revenue; reports using a higher FY2027 base put the implied total near $700 billion.\n09-09 7177 Approximately 70% FY2028 total revenue growth, using NVIDIA's supply-constrained outlook; applied to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base, that implies about $673–700 billion of FY2028 revenue. The 70% growth figure is above the rung-1 boundary of 60%.\n09-17 8316 Approximately 70% FY2028 total revenue growth. This is NVIDIA's direct outlook; applied to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate, it implies about $699 billion of FY2028 revenue (70% growth), while the cited $673 billion figure uses a lower FY2027 base.\n09-19 8625 Use NVIDIA's supply-constrained FY2028 growth outlook of approximately 70%. Applied to the $411 billion FY2027 estimate, that growth implies about $699 billion of FY2028 revenue; the reported $673 billion projection reflects applying roughly 70% to a lower FY2027 base.\n09-20 8725 Approximately 70% FY2028 revenue growth, or about $673 billion using the cited roughly $396 billion FY2027 base. This follows NVIDIA’s explicit supply-constrained outlook; the forecast of twice as many chips sold in calendar 2027 is supportive but does not supply a directly comparable FY2028 revenue-growth figure.\nDRAW 2 v35 8 [('09-06', 6868), ('09-07', 6971), ('09-08', 7073), ('09-09', 7173), ('09-17', 8314), ('09-18', 8493), ('09-19', 8626), ('09-20', 8732)]\n09-06 6868 Applying NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $673–675 billion of FY2028 revenue. That falls in rung 2; the $555 billion trailing-twelve-month estimate for September 2027 is a supporting run-rate signal, not a directly comparable FY2028 total.\n09-07 6971 Applying NVIDIA's approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base implies about $673–675 billion; allowing for small base variation gives roughly $673–680 billion. That is rung 2, below the $700 billion rung-1 threshold.\n09-08 7073 Applying NVIDIA’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base implies about $673–675 billion ($396–397 billion × 1.70). That is rung 2, below the $700 billion rung 1 threshold.\n09-09 7173 About $673–675 billion: apply NVIDIA’s stated 70% FY2028 revenue-growth outlook to the roughly $396–397 billion FY2027 base. This falls in rung 2.\n09-17 8314 NVIDIA's 70% growth outlook applied to the cited FY2027 base range of roughly $396–$411 billion implies about $673–$699 billion for FY2028. The analyst estimate of $678 billion and the 63.2% growth estimate applied to $411 billion imply about $678 billion and $671 billion, respectively; the central forecast is therefore below $700 billion and in rung 2.\n09-18 8493 About $673 billion: NVIDIA's stated FY2028 outlook is 70% growth to roughly that amount. The consensus calculations imply roughly $671–678 billion as well, keeping the forecast below the $700 billion rung-1 threshold.\n09-19 8626 About $673 billion to $678 billion: NVIDIA’s 70% FY2028 growth outlook is stated as about $673 billion, while the analyst FY2027 estimate of $411 billion with 65% growth implies about $678 billion. Both figures fall in rung 2.\n09-20 8732 About $671–$678 billion: NVIDIA's 70% FY2028 growth outlook implies about $673 billion, analyst estimates put FY2028 at $678 billion, and applying Zacks' 63.2% growth estimate to the cited $411 billion FY2027 base gives about $671 billion.\nDRAW 3 v3 2 [('08-30', 6002), ('09-07', 7296)]\n08-30 6002 About $415 billion: the reported $177.837 billion first half plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, then Q4 estimated by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against the implied FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion. The 70% full-year growth guide implies about $367 billion instead, but would require Q4 revenue near $81 billion, below Q3. :: The reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus the $108 billion Q3 guide leaves the forecast dependent mainly on Q4 The reported 70% full-year growth guide is a meaningful counterweight: applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, it implies about $367 billion, requiring Q4 revenue around $81 billion\n09-07 7296 About $407 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half, plus $108 billion guided for Q3, plus about $121 billion for Q4 by carrying the 12% sequential growth pace implied by Q3 guidance forward once. The separate 70% full-year growth statement implies about $367 billion from the $215.9 billion FY2026 base, but would require a sharp Q4 drop and is outweighed by the reported run-rate and near-term guidance. :: The reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion leaves about $121 billion for Q4 if the guided 12% sequential growth pace carries forward, implying about $407 billion for FY2027\nDRAW 3 v35 1 [('08-30', 6001)]\n08-30 6001 Q1 and Q2 total about $164.2 billion; Q3 is about $99.9 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent Data Center revenue mix. Carrying that run-rate into Q4, with hyperscale growth expected to reaccelerate, implies roughly $110–115 billion in Q4 and about $374–379 billion for FY2027. :: The Q3 guide assumes no China Data Center compute revenue, leaving that as possible upside rather than part of the base estimate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,pathlib,re,collections,decimal\nD=decimal.Decimal\ndef db(i,name='\\\\''views'\\\\''):\n c=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod'\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\''.sqlite'\\\\'')).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,j):\n c=a.execute('\\\\''select * from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],)).fetchone()\n assert c and c['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\'']==j['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\''] and c['\\\\''purpose'\\\\'']=='\\\\''judge'\\\\'',dict(j)\n d=json.loads(c['\\\\''request_json'\\\\''])\n return d,json.loads(c['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\nEVID=re.compile(r'\\\\''\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\\\\Z)'\\\\'',re.M|re.S)\ndef evidence(t):\n b=t.split('\\\\''[Evidence in view under this thesis, '\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0]\n m=dict((x.group(1),x.group(0).strip()) for x in EVID.finditer(b))\n assert len(m)==int(b.split()[0]),(len(m),b[:20])\n return m\ndef user(d):\n return next(m['\\\\''content'\\\\''] for m in d['\\\\''messages'\\\\''] if m['\\\\''role'\\\\'']=='\\\\''user'\\\\'')\nUNIT=r'\\\\''(?:trillion(?:s)?|billions?|millions?|bn|mm|[BTM]|%|percent(?:age points?)?)'\\\\''\nNUM=r'\\\\''\\\\d+(?:,\\\\d{3})*(?:\\\\.\\\\d+)?'\\\\''\nFIG=re.compile(rf'\\\\''(?<\"'!['\"\\\\w.])(?P<sign>[+−-])?\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<lo>{NUM})(?:\\\\s*[-–—]\\\\s*\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<hi>{NUM}))?\\\\s*(?P<unit>{UNIT})(?\"'![a-zA-Z0-9])'\"'\\\\'',re.I)\nMULT={'\\\\''trillion'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''trillions'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''t'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''billion'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''billions'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''bn'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''b'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''million'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''millions'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''mm'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''m'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\'')}\ndef figures(t,unit):\n out=[]\n for m in FIG.finditer(t or '\\\\'''\\\\''):\n  u=m['\\\\''unit'\\\\''].lower()\n  if (u in MULT)\"'!=(unit=='\"'\\\\''USD'\\\\''):continue\n  mult=MULT.get(u,1)\n  if m['\\\\''sign'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''-'\\\\'','\\\\''−'\\\\''):mult=-mult\n  for g in ('\\\\''lo'\\\\'','\\\\''hi'\\\\''):\n   if m[g]:out.append(D(m[g].replace('\\\\'','\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))*mult)\n return out\ndef snippets(ed):\n return [re.split(r'\\\\''\\\\n    (?:It replaces|Opinion, not verified:)'\\\\'',b,1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1] for b in ed.values()]\ndef prior_text(t):\n return t.split('\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nAnswer per'\\\\'',1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1]\ndef tag(x,pattern): return bool(re.search(pattern,x or '\\\\'''\\\\'',re.I))\ndef record(i):\n v=db(i);a=db(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\'')\n return (v,a,v.execute(\\\"select j.*,k.unit from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.unit in ('\\\\''USD'\\\\'','\\\\''percent'\\\\'') order by judgement_id\\\"))\nfor i,pair in {1:('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\''),2:('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\''),3:('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')}.items():\n v,a,_=record(i); xs=[]\n for id in pair:\n  xs.append({j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\'']:j for j in v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id'\\\\'',(id,))})\n common=sorted(set(xs[0])&set(xs[1]))\n print(i,len(common),sum(d>='\\\\''2026-08-26'\\\\'' for d in common))\n for day in common:\n  w,p=[x[day] for x in xs]\n  v1=figures(w['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''],'\\\\''USD'\\\\'');v2=figures(p['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''],'\\\\''USD'\\\\'')\n  if (i==3 and day<'\\\\''2026-08-26'\\\\'') or (v1 and v2 and v2[0]>v1[0]+2 and (v2[0]>=300 or v1[0]<300)):\n   print(day,w['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],p['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],w['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''], '\\\\''|'\\\\'',p['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,pathlib,re,collections,decimal\nD=decimal.Decimal\ndef db(i,name='\\\\''views'\\\\''):\n c=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod'\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\''.sqlite'\\\\'')).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,j):\n c=a.execute('\\\\''select * from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],)).fetchone()\n assert c and c['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\'']==j['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\''] and c['\\\\''purpose'\\\\'']=='\\\\''judge'\\\\'',dict(j)\n d=json.loads(c['\\\\''request_json'\\\\''])\n return d,json.loads(c['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\nEVID=re.compile(r'\\\\''\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\\\\Z)'\\\\'',re.M|re.S)\ndef evidence(t):\n b=t.split('\\\\''[Evidence in view under this thesis, '\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0]\n m=dict((x.group(1),x.group(0).strip()) for x in EVID.finditer(b))\n assert len(m)==int(b.split()[0]),(len(m),b[:20])\n return m\ndef user(d):\n return next(m['\\\\''content'\\\\''] for m in d['\\\\''messages'\\\\''] if m['\\\\''role'\\\\'']=='\\\\''user'\\\\'')\nUNIT=r'\\\\''(?:trillion(?:s)?|billions?|millions?|bn|mm|[BTM]|%|percent(?:age points?)?)'\\\\''\nNUM=r'\\\\''\\\\d+(?:,\\\\d{3})*(?:\\\\.\\\\d+)?'\\\\''\nFIG=re.compile(rf'\\\\''(?<\"'!['\"\\\\w.])(?P<sign>[+−-])?\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<lo>{NUM})(?:\\\\s*[-–—]\\\\s*\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<hi>{NUM}))?\\\\s*(?P<unit>{UNIT})(?\"'![a-zA-Z0-9])'\"'\\\\'',re.I)\nMULT={'\\\\''trillion'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''trillions'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''t'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''billion'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''billions'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''bn'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''b'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''million'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''millions'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''mm'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''m'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\'')}\ndef figures(t,unit):\n out=[]\n for m in FIG.finditer(t or '\\\\'''\\\\''):\n  u=m['\\\\''unit'\\\\''].lower()\n  if (u in MULT)\"'!=(unit=='\"'\\\\''USD'\\\\''):continue\n  mult=MULT.get(u,1)\n  if m['\\\\''sign'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''-'\\\\'','\\\\''−'\\\\''):mult=-mult\n  for g in ('\\\\''lo'\\\\'','\\\\''hi'\\\\''):\n   if m[g]:out.append(D(m[g].replace('\\\\'','\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))*mult)\n return out\ndef snippets(ed):\n return [re.split(r'\\\\''\\\\n    (?:It replaces|Opinion, not verified:)'\\\\'',b,1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1] for b in ed.values()]\ndef prior_text(t):\n return t.split('\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nAnswer per'\\\\'',1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1]\ndef tag(x,pattern): return bool(re.search(pattern,x or '\\\\'''\\\\'',re.I))\ndef record(i):\n v=db(i);a=db(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\'')\n return (v,a,v.execute(\\\"select j.*,k.unit from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.unit in ('\\\\''USD'\\\\'','\\\\''percent'\\\\'') order by judgement_id\\\"))\nfor i,pair in {1:('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v8'\\\\''),2:('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\''),3:('\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\'')}.items():\n v,a,_=record(i); xs=[]\n for id in pair:\n  xs.append({j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\'']:j for j in v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id'\\\\'',(id,))})\n common=sorted(set(xs[0])&set(xs[1]))\n print(i,len(common),sum(d>='\\\\''2026-08-26'\\\\'' for d in common))\n for day in common:\n  w,p=[x[day] for x in xs]\n  v1=figures(w['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''],'\\\\''USD'\\\\'');v2=figures(p['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''],'\\\\''USD'\\\\'')\n  if (i==3 and day<'\\\\''2026-08-26'\\\\'') or (v1 and v2 and v2[0]>v1[0]+2 and (v2[0]>=300 or v1[0]<300)):\n   print(day,w['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],p['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],w['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''], '\\\\''|'\\\\'',p['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"1 74 26\n2026-08-20 4074 4076 The quarterly path of $81.6 billion Q1, about $94–$95 billion Q2, $107–$108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 using 12% sequential growth implies about $404 billion before uncertain incremental CPU and SpaceX upside. Discounting that upside for timing and execution, and weighing Morningstar's 80% growth estimate, implies roughly $420 billion for FY2027. | About $390bn for FY2027. Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25bn; the $91bn Q2 total-revenue guide and management’s acceleration commentary support continued growth, while demand and capacity evidence lifts the prior roughly $370bn estimate. The OpenAI campus is not expected to come online until 2028, and HBM supply constraints limit the near-term upside.\n2026-08-25 4580 4589 A forward quarterly path of about $81.6 billion in Q1, $94–95 billion in Q2, $107–108 billion in Q3, and roughly $120 billion in Q4 totals about $403–405 billion. Partial credit for the unshipped standalone-CPU opportunity and early SpaceX demand lifts the central estimate to about $410 billion, still within rung 2. | Approximately $401 billion: Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 estimate less Q1 actual revenue of $75.25 billion and the $86.3 billion Q2 analyst estimate implies about $199.45 billion for H2; applying SemiAnalysis's forecast of 20% upside to H2 analyst estimates gives about $239.34 billion for H2 and approximately $400.89 billion for FY2027.\n2026-08-26 5092 5093 Q1 reported $81.62 billion and Q2 reported $96.2 billion; Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion. Carrying Q3's approximately 12.3% sequential growth over Q2 into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027 in total. | About $400.4 billion: Morningstar's $361 billion estimate less actual Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue ($75.25 billion and $89.0 billion) gives a $196.75 billion H2 baseline; applying SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 upside forecast gives about $236.1 billion for H2. Q3 guidance implies roughly $99–$100 billion of Data Center revenue from the recent segment mix, leaving about $136 billion implied for Q4.\n2026-09-04 6818 6817 Using the reported $81.62 billion Q1 and $96.2 billion Q2, the $108 billion Q3 guide, and carrying approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion gives Q4 FY2027 of about $128.5 billion and FY2027 total revenue of about $414.3 billion. This is rung 2, below $431.2 billion. | About $400 billion, marginally above the rung-1 boundary. Q1–Q2 revenue totals $164.25 billion; Q3 is approximately $99.9 billion using the $108 billion guidance midpoint and Q2’s 92.5% Data Center mix. The earlier H2 estimate baseline of $196.75 billion, uplifted 20% in SemiAnalysis’s H2 forecast, implies about $236.1 billion for H2 and roughly $400.4 billion for FY2027.\n2026-09-05 6928 6936 About $414.3 billion: carry the Q3 guide’s approximately 89% year-over-year growth into Q4, implying about $128.5 billion against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion, then add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and the $108 billion Q3 guide. | About $419 billion for FY2027: Q1–Q2 Data Center revenue totals $164.25 billion; Q3 is about $99.9 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue midpoint and Q2's 92.5% Data Center mix. Carrying Q3's implied total-revenue growth into Q4 gives a roughly $112 billion Q4 baseline; applying SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 upside forecast to the resulting roughly $212 billion H2 baseline gives about $254 billion in H2 and about $419 billion for the year.\n2026-09-06 7116 7111 About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion Q1 + $96.2 billion Q2 + $108 billion Q3 guidance + approximately $128.5 billion for Q4, derived by carrying the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. | About $418 billion: Q1 and Q2 sum to $164.25 billion; Q3 is about $99.9 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue outlook and 92.5% Data Center mix. Carrying the 12.3% sequential total-revenue growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives a roughly $212 billion H2 baseline; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion for H2 and about $418 billion for FY2027.\n2026-09-07 7206 7210 About $416–421 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, then an estimated roughly $129–135 billion in Q4. The baseline Q4 estimate carries the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion; the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration supports a modest uplift. | About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $99.9 billion from the $108 billion total-revenue guidance and 92.5% mix. Carrying the sequential growth into Q4 gives an H2 baseline of about $212 billion; applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside yields about $254 billion for H2 and approximately $419 billion for FY2027.\n2026-09-08 7278 7281 Q1 and Q2 total $177.82 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate forward to the $68 billion prior-year Q4 base implies about $128.5 billion in Q4, or $414.3 billion for FY2027. Allowing for the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration and Rubin ramp, but tempering for supply limits, supports roughly $414–421 billion. | About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; Q3’s $108 billion total-revenue guidance midpoint at the recent 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $99.9 billion. Carrying that sequential growth into Q4 gives an H2 analyst baseline of about $212 billion; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion for H2 and approximately $419 billion for FY2027.\n2026-09-09 7395 7394 Q1 and Q2 reported revenue totals $177.82 billion; adding the $108 billion Q3 guide and carrying its 89% year-over-year growth rate onto the $68 billion prior-year Q4 implies about $128.5 billion for Q4 and $414.3 billion total FY2027 revenue. | About $419 billion: Q3 Data Center revenue is estimated at $99.9 billion from the $108 billion total-revenue midpoint and 92.5% mix; carrying that sequential growth into Q4 gives an analyst-baseline H2 of about $212 billion. SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion in H2, plus $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89 billion in Q2.\n2026-09-10 7594 7600 About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying roughly 89% year-over-year growth onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. | Approximately $418 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue sum to $164.25 billion; Q3 implies about $99.9 billion from the $108 billion total-revenue midpoint at a 92.5% mix; the carried-forward H2 baseline of about $212 billion, raised 20% in line with SemiAnalysis’s forecast, implies roughly $254 billion for H2.\n2026-09-11 7753 7763 About $414.3 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion; estimate Q4 at about $128.5 billion by carrying the guided 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. | About $418 billion: the carried H2 analyst baseline of roughly $212 billion, increased 20% per SemiAnalysis’s forecast, gives about $254 billion for H2; adding Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and $89.0 billion gives about $418 billion for FY2027. Q3’s $108 billion total-revenue midpoint at a 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $99.9 billion, so the H2 estimate requires roughly $154 billion in Q4.\n2026-09-12 7888 7881 About $414.3 billion: Q1 actual of $81.62 billion plus Q2 actual of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an estimated Q4 of about $128.5 billion by carrying the Q3 guide’s 89% year-over-year growth forward from prior-year Q4 revenue of $68 billion. This falls in rung 2. | About $418 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue sum to $164.25 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $99.9 billion from the $108 billion guidance midpoint and 92.5% recent mix. The carried H2 analyst baseline of about $212 billion, raised 20% by SemiAnalysis, implies about $254 billion for H2, for roughly $418 billion for FY2027.\n2026-09-13 7986 7994 About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3’s 89% year-over-year growth rate forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. | About $418 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion guidance midpoint and 92.5% Data Center mix, and about $154 billion in Q4. The Q4 estimate follows from the carried H2 analyst baseline of about $212 billion, increased 20% by SemiAnalysis to about $254 billion; the resulting full-year figure exceeds the rung-1 $400 billion boundary.\n2026-09-14 8180 8178 About $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion (Q1) + $96.2 billion (Q2) + $108 billion (Q3 guidance) + about $128.8 billion for Q4, calculated by carrying Q3's roughly 89.4% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. | About $418 billion: Q1 and Q2 contribute $164.25 billion; Q3 is implied at about $99.9 billion from the $108 billion total-revenue guidance midpoint and the recent 92.5% Data Center mix; carrying the H2 analyst baseline forward with SemiAnalysis’ 20% upside gives about $254 billion for H2. That implies about $154 billion in Q4 and places FY2027 above the $400 billion rung boundary.\n2026-09-16 8504 8502 Q1 and Q2 actual revenue totaled $177.82 billion. Using the analyst's $113.6 billion Q3 estimate, based on NVIDIA's recent guidance-beat record, implies about 99% Q3 year-over-year growth; carrying that growth rate onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of about $68 billion gives roughly $135.5 billion for Q4 FY2027 and about $426.9 billion for the full year. | About $418 billion: Q1 and Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; Q3 is about $99.9 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guidance midpoint and recent 92.5% mix. Carrying forward the prior H2 analyst baseline with SemiAnalysis’ stated 20% H2 upside implies roughly $254 billion for H2, including about $154 billion in Q4, putting the FY total above $400 billion.\n2026-09-17 8609 8613 About $404 billion: $81.62 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, plus a $113.6 billion Q3 estimate, plus roughly $112.2 billion for Q4 (Q4 FY2026 revenue of about $68 billion grown by analysts' 65% estimate). This is an estimate-based forecast, not a claim that the FY2027 total is already established. | About $418 billion: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the latest 92.5% mix to the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue outlook implies about $99.9 billion for Q3. The carried H2 estimate, uplifted by the cited 20% SemiAnalysis view, is about $254 billion for Q3–Q4 combined, leaving roughly $154 billion for Q4 and putting the FY2027 total above $400 billion.\n2026-09-18 8810 8816 Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.82 billion. Applying the recent average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion for Q3; applying analysts' 65% Q4 growth estimate to FY2026 Q4 revenue of $68 billion gives about $112.2 billion, for roughly $403.6 billion total. | About $395 billion: $164.25 billion reported in the first half plus about $99.9 billion implied for Q3 leaves roughly $131 billion for Q4. That represents a substantial Q4 step-up consistent with expected reacceleration, but below the prior forecast’s implied Q4 contribution.\n2 69 26\n2026-07-12 517 520 About $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of approximately $91 billion, I carry the roughly 11.5% sequential increase forward to Q3 and Q4. This remains within rung 3 and below $400 billion. | About $400–410B of FY2027 Data Center revenue. I use the Q1 $75.25B result and Q2 growth outlook as the operating base, then give partial—not full—weight to the roughly $1T Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility across calendar 2026–27 because it spans multiple periods and products; the discounted estimate is just above the $400B rung boundary.\n2026-07-13 592 593 Approximately $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. | About $415B of FY2027 Data Center revenue: the guide-derived sequential-growth run-rate is about $356B, but Q1's 21% sequential segment growth, management's Q2 acceleration statement, and quantified 2027 hyperscaler spending and demand evidence imply a discounted uplift of roughly $59B. This is below the $450B rung-1 boundary.\n2026-07-14 722 728 About $387 billion: starting with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carry the approximately 11.5% sequential increase into Q3 and Q4, yielding about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. | About $420B of FY2027 Data Center revenue. The guided Q2 total and Q1 segment mix imply a roughly $356B run-rate baseline when guided sequential growth is carried forward; Q1’s 21% sequential Data Center growth, management’s stated Q2 acceleration, and demand evidence justify an uplift, but not to $450B.\n2026-07-16 1016 1017 About $387 billion: start with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry their roughly 11.5% sequential increase forward to Q3 and Q4, implying about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion. | About $390B for FY2027 Data Center revenue. FY2026’s $193.7B carried forward at Q1’s 92% year-over-year growth implies about $372B; Q2 acceleration, order visibility, and supportive demand/capacity signals lift the estimate, tempered by supply, competition, and China risks.\n2026-07-17 1093 1092 About $387 billion: start with reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91.0 billion, then carry their approximately 11.5% sequential growth rate into Q3 and Q4, implying about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion for those quarters. | Approximately $395B of FY2027 Data Center revenue: FY2026’s $193.7B base carried forward at about 2.04x, reflecting Q1’s 92% year-over-year growth, management’s Q2 growth-acceleration signal, and stronger demand and capacity evidence. The implied figure is below $400B.\n2026-07-20 1217 1219 About $387 billion: start with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry their roughly 11.5% sequential growth forward to implied Q3 revenue of about $101.5 billion and Q4 revenue of about $113.2 billion. | About $395B for FY2027 Data Center revenue: FY2026’s $193.7B carried forward at the latest 92% year-over-year growth rate implies about $372B, with Q1’s $75.25B actual revenue and management’s Q2 acceleration guidance supporting a higher full-year estimate. The $1T Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026–2027 and multiple platforms, so it is discounted rather than counted as FY2027 Data Center revenue.\n2026-07-21 1347 1350 About $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and the Q2 guide of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for approximately $387.3 billion total. | Approximately $395B for FY2027 Data Center revenue: FY2026's $193.7B carried forward at Q1's 92% year-over-year growth implies about $372B, with Q2 growth acceleration and recent demand signals supporting a modest uplift. The estimate remains below $400B.\n2026-07-22 1450 1453 About $387.3 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying it through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. As a cross-check, 80% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion; both estimates fall in rung 3. | About $405B for FY2027: carrying $193.7B of FY20