)}\ndef figures(t,unit):\n out=[]\n for m in FIG.finditer(t or '\\\\'''\\\\''):\n  u=m['\\\\''unit'\\\\''].lower()\n  if (u in MULT)\"'!=(unit=='\"'\\\\''USD'\\\\''):continue\n  mult=MULT.get(u,1)\n  if m['\\\\''sign'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''-'\\\\'','\\\\''−'\\\\''):mult=-mult\n  for g in ('\\\\''lo'\\\\'','\\\\''hi'\\\\''):\n   if m[g]:out.append(D(m[g].replace('\\\\'','\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))*mult)\n return out\ndef snippets(ed):\n return [re.split(r'\\\\''\\\\n    (?:It replaces|Opinion, not verified:)'\\\\'',b,1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1] for b in ed.values()]\ndef prior_text(t):\n return t.split('\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nAnswer per'\\\\'',1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1]\ndef tag(x,pattern): return bool(re.search(pattern,x or '\\\\'''\\\\'',re.I))\ndef record(i):\n v=db(i);a=db(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\'')\n return (v,a,v.execute(\\\"select j.*,k.unit from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.unit in ('\\\\''USD'\\\\'','\\\\''percent'\\\\'') order by judgement_id\\\"))\nv,a,_=record(2)\nfor call in (8729,8737):\n j=v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where call_id=?'\\\\'',(call,)).fetchone(); d,raw=req(a,j); ed=evidence(user(d))\n print(call)\n for e in ed.values():\n  if tag(e,r'\\\\''SemiAnalysis'\\\\'') and tag(e,r'\\\\''\\\\b20%|20 percent'\\\\''): print(e[:420])\nq=v.execute(\\\"select created_from_input from evidence where evidence_id='\\\\''e1926'\\\\''\\\").fetchone()[0]\nfor c in a.execute(\\\"select call_id,request_json from model_calls where purpose='\\\\''extract'\\\\'' and instr(request_json,?)>0 limit 1\\\",(q,)):\n s=user(json.loads(c['\\\\''request_json'\\\\'']))\n p=s.find('\\\\''What analysts expect the hyperscalers'\\\\'')\n print('\\\\''BofA source context:'\\\\'', c['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],s[p-1250:p+580])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"8729\n8737\n- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst es\nBofA source context: 3435 used.\nKeeping that spending off their own books is precisely the point, and the fact that such a structure is needed at all tells investors a great deal about where the constraints in the AI boom now lie.\nThe financial engineering rests on a single reclassification. Graphics processing units (GPUs) have always been treated as equipment that loses value quickly, superseded whenever a faster generation arrives.\nNvidia raises over €21.5bn in first bond sale since 2021 as AI growth race continues\nAnalysis: Will Big Tech's colossal AI spending crush Europe's data sovereignty?\nNvidia is effectively asking lenders to treat them instead as long-lived infrastructure, closer to a toll road or a power plant, that can be borrowed against for years.\n\"These are revenue-generating assets now,\" Huang said, describing them as productive, long-lived and transferable between customers.\nWhy the money had to come from somewhere else\nThe timing reflects a squeeze that has been building all year.\nMicrosoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\nWhat analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\nThe pattern has repeated at every stage.\nAnalysts who already considered last year's investment unsustainable then watched the hyperscalers guide higher at the start of 2026, revise those figures upward again through the year, and pencil in larger sums still for next year and 2028.\nMoody's has warned that spending on this s\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,pathlib,re,collections,decimal\nD=decimal.Decimal\ndef db(i,name='\\\\''views'\\\\''):\n c=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod'\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\''.sqlite'\\\\'')).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,j):\n c=a.execute('\\\\''select * from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],)).fetchone()\n assert c and c['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\'']==j['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\''] and c['\\\\''purpose'\\\\'']=='\\\\''judge'\\\\'',dict(j)\n d=json.loads(c['\\\\''request_json'\\\\''])\n return d,json.loads(c['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\nEVID=re.compile(r'\\\\''\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\\\\Z)'\\\\'',re.M|re.S)\ndef evidence(t):\n b=t.split('\\\\''[Evidence in view under this thesis, '\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0]\n m=dict((x.group(1),x.group(0).strip()) for x in EVID.finditer(b))\n assert len(m)==int(b.split()[0]),(len(m),b[:20])\n return m\ndef user(d):\n return next(m['\\\\''content'\\\\''] for m in d['\\\\''messages'\\\\''] if m['\\\\''role'\\\\'']=='\\\\''user'\\\\'')\nUNIT=r'\\\\''(?:trillion(?:s)?|billions?|millions?|bn|mm|[BTM]|%|percent(?:age points?)?)'\\\\''\nNUM=r'\\\\''\\\\d+(?:,\\\\d{3})*(?:\\\\.\\\\d+)?'\\\\''\nFIG=re.compile(rf'\\\\''(?<\"'!['\"\\\\w.])(?P<sign>[+−-])?\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<lo>{NUM})(?:\\\\s*[-–—]\\\\s*\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<hi>{NUM}))?\\\\s*(?P<unit>{UNIT})(?\"'![a-zA-Z0-9])'\"'\\\\'',re.I)\nMULT={'\\\\''trillion'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''trillions'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''t'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''billion'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''billions'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''bn'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''b'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''million'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''millions'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''mm'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''m'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\'')}\ndef figures(t,unit):\n out=[]\n for m in FIG.finditer(t or '\\\\'''\\\\''):\n  u=m['\\\\''unit'\\\\''].lower()\n  if (u in MULT)\"'!=(unit=='\"'\\\\''USD'\\\\''):continue\n  mult=MULT.get(u,1)\n  if m['\\\\''sign'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''-'\\\\'','\\\\''−'\\\\''):mult=-mult\n  for g in ('\\\\''lo'\\\\'','\\\\''hi'\\\\''):\n   if m[g]:out.append(D(m[g].replace('\\\\'','\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))*mult)\n return out\ndef snippets(ed):\n return [re.split(r'\\\\''\\\\n    (?:It replaces|Opinion, not verified:)'\\\\'',b,1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1] for b in ed.values()]\ndef prior_text(t):\n return t.split('\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nAnswer per'\\\\'',1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1]\ndef tag(x,pattern): return bool(re.search(pattern,x or '\\\\'''\\\\'',re.I))\ndef record(i):\n v=db(i);a=db(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\'')\n return (v,a,v.execute(\\\"select j.*,k.unit from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.unit in ('\\\\''USD'\\\\'','\\\\''percent'\\\\'') order by judgement_id\\\"))\nfor i,ids in {1:['\\\\''v28'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\''],2:['\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v29'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\''],3:['\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v33'\\\\'','\\\\''v37'\\\\'','\\\\''v41'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\'']}.items():\n v,a,_=record(i)\n for id in ids:\n  j=v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1'\\\\'',(id,)).fetchone(); d,raw=req(a,j);ed=evidence(user(d))\n  assert set(raw['\\\\''cites'\\\\''])<=set(ed)\n  print(i,id,j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''][-5:],raw['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],raw['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n  if id in ('\\\\''v7'\\\\'','\\\\''v37'\\\\'') and i\"'!=2:\n   for key,x in ed.items():\n    if key in raw['\"'\\\\''cites'\\\\''] and tag(x,r'\\\\''Oracle|72%|1\\\\.3 trillion'\\\\''):\n     print(x.split('\\\\''\\\\n    Opinion'\\\\'')[0][:680])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sqlite3,json,pathlib,re,collections,decimal\nD=decimal.Decimal\ndef db(i,name='\\\\''views'\\\\''):\n c=sqlite3.connect((pathlib.Path(f'\\\\''poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{i}/prod'\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\''.sqlite'\\\\'')).resolve().as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\n c.row_factory=sqlite3.Row\n return c\ndef req(a,j):\n c=a.execute('\\\\''select * from model_calls where call_id=?'\\\\'',(j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],)).fetchone()\n assert c and c['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\'']==j['\\\\''request_sha256'\\\\''] and c['\\\\''purpose'\\\\'']=='\\\\''judge'\\\\'',dict(j)\n d=json.loads(c['\\\\''request_json'\\\\''])\n return d,json.loads(c['\\\\''answer_json'\\\\''])\nEVID=re.compile(r'\\\\''\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\\\\Z)'\\\\'',re.M|re.S)\ndef evidence(t):\n b=t.split('\\\\''[Evidence in view under this thesis, '\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n['\\\\'',1)[0]\n m=dict((x.group(1),x.group(0).strip()) for x in EVID.finditer(b))\n assert len(m)==int(b.split()[0]),(len(m),b[:20])\n return m\ndef user(d):\n return next(m['\\\\''content'\\\\''] for m in d['\\\\''messages'\\\\''] if m['\\\\''role'\\\\'']=='\\\\''user'\\\\'')\nUNIT=r'\\\\''(?:trillion(?:s)?|billions?|millions?|bn|mm|[BTM]|%|percent(?:age points?)?)'\\\\''\nNUM=r'\\\\''\\\\d+(?:,\\\\d{3})*(?:\\\\.\\\\d+)?'\\\\''\nFIG=re.compile(rf'\\\\''(?<\"'!['\"\\\\w.])(?P<sign>[+−-])?\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<lo>{NUM})(?:\\\\s*[-–—]\\\\s*\\\\\"'$?'\"\\\\s*(?P<hi>{NUM}))?\\\\s*(?P<unit>{UNIT})(?\"'![a-zA-Z0-9])'\"'\\\\'',re.I)\nMULT={'\\\\''trillion'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''trillions'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''t'\\\\'':1000,'\\\\''billion'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''billions'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''bn'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''b'\\\\'':1,'\\\\''million'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''millions'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''mm'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\''),'\\\\''m'\\\\'':D('\\\\''.001'\\\\'')}\ndef figures(t,unit):\n out=[]\n for m in FIG.finditer(t or '\\\\'''\\\\''):\n  u=m['\\\\''unit'\\\\''].lower()\n  if (u in MULT)\"'!=(unit=='\"'\\\\''USD'\\\\''):continue\n  mult=MULT.get(u,1)\n  if m['\\\\''sign'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''-'\\\\'','\\\\''−'\\\\''):mult=-mult\n  for g in ('\\\\''lo'\\\\'','\\\\''hi'\\\\''):\n   if m[g]:out.append(D(m[g].replace('\\\\'','\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))*mult)\n return out\ndef snippets(ed):\n return [re.split(r'\\\\''\\\\n    (?:It replaces|Opinion, not verified:)'\\\\'',b,1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1] for b in ed.values()]\ndef prior_text(t):\n return t.split('\\\\''[Previous conclusion'\\\\'',1)[1].split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\nAnswer per'\\\\'',1)[0].split('\\\\''] '\\\\'',1)[1]\ndef tag(x,pattern): return bool(re.search(pattern,x or '\\\\'''\\\\'',re.I))\ndef record(i):\n v=db(i);a=db(i,'\\\\''archive'\\\\'')\n return (v,a,v.execute(\\\"select j.*,k.unit from judgements j join view_keys k using(view_id) where k.unit in ('\\\\''USD'\\\\'','\\\\''percent'\\\\'') order by judgement_id\\\"))\nfor i,ids in {1:['\\\\''v28'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\''],2:['\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v29'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\''],3:['\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v3'\\\\'','\\\\''v33'\\\\'','\\\\''v37'\\\\'','\\\\''v41'\\\\'','\\\\''v7'\\\\'']}.items():\n v,a,_=record(i)\n for id in ids:\n  j=v.execute('\\\\''select * from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1'\\\\'',(id,)).fetchone(); d,raw=req(a,j);ed=evidence(user(d))\n  assert set(raw['\\\\''cites'\\\\''])<=set(ed)\n  print(i,id,j['\\\\''call_id'\\\\''],j['\\\\''publication_day'\\\\''][-5:],raw['\\\\''reference'\\\\''],raw['\\\\''implied_outcome'\\\\''])\n  if id in ('\\\\''v7'\\\\'','\\\\''v37'\\\\'') and i\"'!=2:\n   for key,x in ed.items():\n    if key in raw['\"'\\\\''cites'\\\\''] and tag(x,r'\\\\''Oracle|72%|1\\\\.3 trillion'\\\\''):\n     print(x.split('\\\\''\\\\n    Opinion'\\\\'')[0][:680])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"1 v28 9016 09-20 The company-based reference is about $43 billion ($25 billion of commitments as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027), which is rung 2. No dated market consensus is supplied. About $75 billion outstanding at the FY2027 annual report: use Bank of America’s approximately $70 billion commitment estimate as the anchor and allow a modest net increase for later disclosed deals, discounted for overlap and funding before the report.\n1 v7 8890 09-19 BofA's $1.08 trillion 2027 consensus estimate covers five companies. Subtracting the carried Oracle estimate of roughly $100–$150 billion gives about $930–$980 billion for the four companies in this question, which is rung 3. About $1.15–$1.20 trillion, centered near $1.16 trillion: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast less the carried roughly $100–$150 billion Oracle estimate. This is in rung 4.\n- e2685 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said cloud-industry backlog exceeds $2 trillion and top-five hyperscaler CapEx is expected to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e3281 [third_party_view; insidermonkey.com; original source Steve Eisman 2026-08-13; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Steve Eisman estimated on August 13 that OpenAI and Anthropic generate roughly 70% of Microsoft, Amazon, Google, and Oracle’s AI-related revenue and 25%–35% of their cloud revenue.\n    Quote: \"Big Short\" investor Steve Eisman warned on CNBC's \"Fast Money\" on August 13 that OpenAI and Anthropic account for roughly 70% of AI-related revenue at Microsoft, Amazon, Google, and Oracle, and as much as 25% to 35% of those companies' cloud revenue.\n- e4256 [party_statement; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:49351bd6de4706de524baf5f 2026-09-05] NVIDIA expects the top five hyperscalers' combined capital expenditures to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: Nvidia also expects the combined capital expenditures of the top five hyperscalers to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e5031 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst), citing Reuters’ account of BofA Global Research estimates; original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle would spend roughly $795 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.08 trillion in calendar 2027, a 36% increase year over year.\n    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026 before pu\n- e5046 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e5076 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-16; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] The top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e5086 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e5155 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4fbadb3b4cb5846297a34b0a 2026-09-16] NVIDIA estimates that the top five hyperscalers’ capital expenditures could reach $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027, up from $800 billion in 2026.\n    Quote: Nvidia, for instance, estimates that the capex of the top five hyperscalers could increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027, up from $800 billion this year.\n- e5308 [party_statement; Harsh Chauhan, fool.com, paraphrasing NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA projected that the top five U.S. hyperscalers’ capital expenditures will total $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Even better, Nvidia projects that this figure will jump to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e5328 [party_statement; fool.com (author); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] The projected data center capital expenditures of the five largest AI hyperscalers rise to $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Next year, that figure is projected to rise to $1.3 trillion.\n2 v16 8735 09-20 NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion falls at rung 3. Approximately $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: about $20 billion from Vera standalone CPU sales plus a roughly $5 billion Grace contribution, using Grace’s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the recent run-rate proxy.\n2 v29 8734 09-20 Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; this is rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion). Its period and whether it measures outstanding commitments are unclear. About $53 billion: $25 billion of commitments reported as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027, then the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure and a risk-adjusted contribution from the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 billion Thinking Machines discussions.\n2 v6 8595 09-19 Calendar-2027 hyperscaler spending consensus is about $1.08 trillion (e1926), in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion). About $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the prior approximate $730 billion/$800 billion four-to-five share proxy to NVIDIA’s $1.3 trillion top-five calendar-2027 CapEx estimate (e2535; repeated in e4756 and later reports).\n2 v9 8737 09-20 The market reference supplied by the previous conclusion is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion). Approximately $410 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, about $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $146 billion estimated for Q4. The Q4 estimate reflects management's expected hyperscale reacceleration and Rubin ramp, with a discounted contribution from SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half Data Center revenue would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\n3 v14 9182 09-20 NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the relevant company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); it is not a total-CPU figure. Just above $25 billion: carry the approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward and add Grace’s reported trailing-12-month revenue run-rate of more than $5 billion.\n3 v3 9183 09-20 Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4360, e5406), placing the reference in rung 2. About $446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, approximately $113.6 billion for Q3 using the guidance midpoint and the cited average beat, and approximately $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, then adding about $27–28 billion for the estimated 20% second-half data-center-compute uplift.\n3 v33 9175 09-20 The company-based reference is approximately $43 billion: $25 billion of commitments as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027. That reference sits in rung 3 ($30 billion to less than $50 billion). Approximately $40–45 billion, centered near $43 billion: carry forward the reported $25 billion commitment balance together with the stated $18 billion remainder-of-FY2027 commitment, without adding later deal headlines that may overlap or count unfulfilled obligations as completed investments. This remains below the $50 billion rung-4 threshold.\n3 v37 9173 09-20 NVIDIA management's FY2028 gross-margin guidance is 72%–73%. The range spans rung 3 at 72% and rung 2 above 72% through 73%; its midpoint of 72.5% lies in rung 2. About 72.0% for FY2028: I place the result at the floor of management's 72%–73% guidance because negotiated Q1 price increases support recovery, while rising memory prices and persistent supply constraints weigh against exceeding 72%.\n- e2788 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA forecast Q3 FY2027 GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 73.5%–74.5%, Q4 FY2027 margins of 71%–72%, and FY2028 margins of 72%–73%.\n    Quote: For Q3, we expect GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to be 74% plus or minus 50 basis points. We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price increases take effect in Q1.\n3 v41 9166 09-20 NVIDIA management’s stated expectation that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the $20 billion FY2027 expectation implies more than $40 billion, which is rung 2. Roughly $38–39 billion for FY2028: the stated more-than-double expectation points above $40 billion, but is discounted for a forward Vera target without shipment volume in hand, with the competitive evidence adding execution risk.\n3 v7 9044 09-19 Bank of America's reported analyst consensus of $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capital spending (e2080) is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion). The separate $1.08 trillion BofA estimate includes Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and Oracle (e5056), so it is not an exact match for this four-company, data-center-only question. About $1.1 trillion, marginally above the rung-4 boundary. This is a judgmental scope-adjusted synthesis: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 capex forecast (e2759) is discounted for the fifth company and non-data-center capex, with the lower $1.08 trillion estimate (e5056) and execution constraints tempering it; no supplied evidence directly quantifies that discount.\n- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n- e5056 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend $1.08 trillion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2027, about 36% more than in 2026.\n    Quote: before pushing that figure to $1.08 trillion in 2027. That would represent an increase of about 36% in a single year\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'def is_shared|def check_bounds|def judgement_answer' src/hivemind/{views/identity.py,extraction.py,vocabulary.py}; sed -n '32,60p' src/hivemind/views/identity.py; sed -n '490,509p' src/hivemind/extraction.py; rg -n 'reference|no number' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'def is_shared|def check_bounds|def judgement_answer' src/hivemind/{views/identity.py,extraction.py,vocabulary.py}; sed -n '32,60p' src/hivemind/views/identity.py; sed -n '490,509p' src/hivemind/extraction.py; rg -n 'reference|no number' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md\"","aggregated_output":"src/hivemind/vocabulary.py:67:def judgement_answer(data):\nsrc/hivemind/extraction.py:493:def check_bounds(rungs):\nsrc/hivemind/views/identity.py:13:def is_shared(value):\n            value.get('period_start'), value.get('period_end'), value.get('basis'), value.get('unit'), value.get('event_type'))\n\n\nGENERATION_MILESTONES = ('launch', 'shipment')\n\n\ndef same_generation_milestones(key, candidate):\n    \"\"\"D248 item 3: a product generation's launch and its shipments are milestones of one question, asked at the step that brings\n    revenue; the judge of sameness (Jev) says whether they are the same generation, this only lifts the event-type conflict.\"\"\"\n    return key.get('measure') == 'event' and key.get('event_type') in GENERATION_MILESTONES and candidate.get('event_type') in GENERATION_MILESTONES\n\n\ndef one_process(key, candidate):\n    \"\"\"D258 (R2-f): a policy process's steps are one process; the model's event-type word for it (licence, approval, other) is not\n    identity. One 14-day draw opened three questions on one licensing decision because that word varied.\"\"\"\n    return 'policy_event' in (key.get('measure'), candidate.get('measure'))\n\n\ndef conflicts(key, candidate, *, merging):\n    \"\"\"Known different quantities/events cannot merge; corrected scopes/periods bind only corrected keys. An event's period is its\n    deadline: another deadline never conflicts (D248 item 2: while the earlier-deadline question is open, a later deadline is its\n    evidence; a closed one is succeeded by `can_succeed`, which compares the periods itself). `event` and `policy_event` are one\n    measure family (D258, R2-f): the model typed one licensing decision both ways, and the names and the judge of sameness decide.\"\"\"\n    if key['measure'] != candidate['measure'] and not (key['measure'] in EVENT_MEASURES and candidate['measure'] in EVENT_MEASURES):\n        return True\n    if (key['event_type'] != candidate.get('event_type', 'n/a') and not same_generation_milestones(key, candidate)\n            and not one_process(key, candidate)):\n        return True\n    for field in ('basis', 'unit'):\n    return file_reading(read_item(archive, cfg, item, text, shown_views, universe, transport=transport), item, shown_views)\n\n\ndef check_bounds(rungs):\n    \"\"\"None when the rungs' numeric bounds form one unbroken partition (or none carries bounds), else the problem.\"\"\"\n    bounded = [r for r in rungs if r.get('lo') is not None or r.get('hi') is not None]\n    if not bounded:\n        return None\n    if len(bounded) != len(rungs):\n        return 'some rungs carry bounds and some do not'\n    ordered = sorted(rungs, key=lambda r: -1e300 if r.get('lo') is None else float(r['lo']))\n    for a, b in zip(ordered, ordered[1:]):\n        if a.get('hi') is None or b.get('lo') is None or float(a['hi']) != float(b['lo']):\n            return 'rung bounds overlap or leave a gap'\n    if ordered[-1].get('hi') is not None:\n        return 'the top rung must be open above'\n    return None\n\n\ndef check_group_refs(c, declared):\n20:  1. `reference`：到 2026-07-14 为止市场预期这道题的答案是什么（一致预期、公司指引、既定计划或截止日），出自哪几条事项；材料里没有就写\n35:- **第二层，读不是数**：分数之外，对一个样本的判断，由没写代码的读者看系统写的理由（从 judging-v6 起还有 reference 和 implied_outcome）\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '10,28p' src/hivemind/views/identity.py; sed -n '12,35p' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md; nl -ba src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt | sed -n '1,14p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '10,28p' src/hivemind/views/identity.py; sed -n '12,35p' docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md; nl -ba src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt | sed -n '1,14p'\"","aggregated_output":"EVENT_MEASURES = ('event', 'policy_event')\n\n\ndef is_shared(value):\n    \"\"\"A shared quantity: by its admission reason on a candidate, by its stored scope source on a key.\"\"\"\n    return value.get('measure') not in (None, 'other', *EVENT_MEASURES) and (value.get('scope_source') == 'shared' or value.get('opens_as') in SHARED_REASONS)\n\n\ndef automatic_key(value, object_id=None):\n    \"\"\"Return a complete, code-owned key, or None when identity still needs judgement.\"\"\"\n    if value.get('key_version') != KEY_VERSION:\n        return None\n    if value.get('measure') in (None, 'other'):\n        return None\n    if is_shared(value):\n        return (object_id or value.get('object'), value['measure'], '', value.get('period_start'), value.get('period_end'),\n                value.get('basis'), value.get('unit'), value.get('event_type'))\n    if value.get('scope_source') not in ('none', 'watch'):\n        return None\n\n- **单位**：一道金标题 × 一个时点（14 天窗口末，2026-07-14 收盘后）。23 道题：22 道种子（`poc/seeds/nvda-2026-07-01-v2.json` v1–v22，每次运行里\n  编号固定）加金标 must_open 里种子没覆盖的 1 道（俄亥俄租约担保是/否，2026-07-01 的 must_open）。其余 27 条 must_open 都是这 23 道的同一赌注\n  （信用支持金额 ×10、Kyber 出货 ×11、云厂商资本开支 ×5、内存价格 ×1、中国放行 H200 ×2），映射写在 `questions-v1.json` 里，Alex 签。\n- **材料**：签过的抽取标准对这 14 天的读法，即金标 golden-v5（dev，118 篇）的 309 条事项（核心 86、辅助 151、可选 72），带日期、标题、\n  来源层、说话人、原文和意思。不用系统的账本（账本随每臂变，尺子不能随臂变），不用原始全文（那是重做抽取）。这和抽取的尺子是同一把：标准第 5 节\n  说的\"分析员会记的\"就是这些。\n- **分析师写什么**（每道题一条，`reader-brief.md` 是他拿到的全部说明，里面只有标准和 PRD 06 的原话，没有系统的提示词）：\n  1. `reference`：到 2026-07-14 为止市场预期这道题的答案是什么（一致预期、公司指引、既定计划或截止日），出自哪几条事项；材料里没有就写\n     \"没有，参照 = 现状\"。\n  2. `implied_outcome`：材料推出来的结果——数字题给一个数或一个区间，事件题给是/否和日期——怎么推的，出自哪几条。\n  3. `direction`：结果相对参照：above / in_line / below；事件题 yes / no。\n  4. `confidence`：high / medium / low（材料把这件事定到什么程度）。\n  5. `decisive_items`：撑起这个看法的 2–6 条事项编号；`against`：最强的反面一条。\n  6. `no_view`：材料里没有任何关于这道题的东西时为 true；这道题不进分数，记\"没练到\"。\n  7. `reasoning`：三到六句。只用材料，不用材料外的知识，每个说法都要有编号。\n- **分析师是谁**：两位互相独立、都没写过这套代码也没看过系统输出的模型读者（A：Codex；B：一个干净上下文的 Claude 读者），各自从同一个包\n  写一遍。都标明\"模型写的\"。**Alex 定（2026-11-02，D262）：金标 = 读者 B 的 23 条答案，原样；读者 A 的答案留作对照记录**，两者的差别\n  （区间或是/否不同 3/23：q09、q12、q23；方向标签不同 12/20）就是这把尺子自身噪声的记录（`reconcile-v1.md`）。\n- **判\"对\"的方法**（`score_judgement.py`，全部是代码）：取一次运行里每道题在 2026-07-14 及之前最后一次判断。数字题：金标的区间\n  [low, high] 和系统所选那一档的 [lo, hi) 有没有交集，有 = 一致；事件题：是/否相同 = 一致。没判过的题单独数（那是证据没到，D2 的事，不算\n  判断错）。另外打印两条零假设：**查表基线**（把金标写的参照数落进哪一档，就选那一档）和**永远中性基线**；判断要在这两条线之上才算有判断力。\n  读者之间的分歧率一起打印，那是尺子自身的噪声。\n- **第二层，读不是数**：分数之外，对一个样本的判断，由没写代码的读者看系统写的理由（从 judging-v6 起还有 reference 和 implied_outcome）\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n     4\t- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n     9\t- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n    10\t- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n    11\t- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"agent_message","text":"The product checks that a forecast cites real evidence, but it never checks what the cited numbers measure, how they were combined, or whether related answers can both be true. On September 20, one judge put NVIDIA’s Q4 Data Center revenue at **$146B** while another put **the entire company at $127B**. Both answers passed. The same design lets one judge add two commitments whose overlap is unknown, turn a five-company spending forecast into a four-company estimate using an unrelated ratio, or call yesterday’s forecast the market’s view. I would keep the investment questions separate and have related answers use one shared, source-backed calculation. The model would still decide what the evidence implies. Code would check what each number refers to, recalculate the arithmetic, and catch contradictions before publication.\n\n## Traces\n\nI joined each saved answer to its archived request by call ID and checked the request hash. I read the archived response, the evidence actually shown, and, where the source or filing mattered, the extraction request and saved evidence links. The call IDs below are local to each draw. [Old-2 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite), [Old-3 archive](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/archive.sqlite)\n\n| Case | How the answer got its number |\n|---|---|\n| **E · Old-2 v9, Data Center, September 20, call 8737** | **$164B** first-half revenue was quoted in `e4995` and corroborated by the two reported quarters. **About $100B** for Q3 came from the $108B company-wide guide and the 92.5% segment mix. `e3219` already showed that calculation as $99.9B. **$146B** for Q4 came from the judge. Management expected growth to pick up, and `e976` quoted SemiAnalysis at 20% above analyst estimates for the second half, without naming the estimate underneath. The judge used its own **$414B** comparison to calculate a **$464B** upside scenario, then discounted that scenario without sizing the discount. The published numbers add to **$410B**, clearing $400B. Equivalently, $146B is what $410B requires after the first half and Q3. The trace establishes that arithmetic, while the order in which the judge chose those numbers is unknown. |\n| **Old-2 v14, total revenue, September 20, call 8729** | **$81.6B** and **$96.2B** were reported Q1 and Q2 sales. The judge took the high end of the $108B ±2% Q3 guide, **$110.16B**, and applied the quoted historical average beat of **3.1%** to get **about $113.6B**. It then carried the **12%** sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide into Q4 to get **about $127B**. That carry-forward is the judge’s assumption. The sum rounds to **$419B**. The **$411B** market comparison came from `e5056`. The annual numbers look compatible, and both answers land in the $400–450B band. The contradiction appears one level down, in Q4. |\n| **O · Old-2 v29, equity commitments, September 20, call 8734** | The filing quoted in `e2620` showed **$25B** of commitments as of July 26. `e2889` said **$18B** was committed for the rest of the year, in addition to **$47.9B already held**. The answer treated the $18B as additional to the **$25B**, giving **$43B**. The sources leave that overlap unresolved. It then added **$3.5B** for MediaTek and implicitly about **$6.5B** from talks reported at up to **$10B** for Anthropic and about **$2.5B** for Thinking Machines. It gave no weights and did not calculate funding that would reduce the balance. The result was **$53B**. Its **$70B** comparison was a Bank of America estimate of ecosystem investments with unclear timing and coverage. Removing only the disputed $18B takes the answer’s own arithmetic to $35B, across the $50B boundary. That sensitivity does not establish a $35B forecast. |\n| **S · Old-2 v6, four hyperscalers’ data-center capex, September 19, call 8595** | The request showed **over $730B** of 2026 *AI infrastructure spending* for four companies in `e1786`, plus NVIDIA’s forecasts of **nearly $800B** in 2026 and **$1.3T** in 2027 for five companies’ *capex* in `e2535`. The judge carried forward its prior rule, `730 ÷ 800 × 1.3T ≈ $1.19T`, and moved above the **$1.1T** boundary. The arithmetic works. The ratio mixes spending definitions and assumes the same share a year later. The **$1.08T** comparison in `e1926` also covers broader hyperscaler spending. The judge acknowledged the mismatch and still used it to pick the higher band. |\n| **E · Old-3 v41, FY2028 Vera CPU, September 20, call 9166** | Management’s **$20B** FY2027 figure and “more than double” language point above **$40B**. The request included Vera-specific wording about *potential*, broader CPU wording, deployment reports, competitive benchmarks, and no sized Vera shipment commitment. These can inform a discount. The **$38–39B** figure was the judge’s own estimate, carried from September 19. The answer gave no bridge from those risks to a discount large enough to cross $40B. |\n| **O · Old-3 v3, total revenue, September 20, call 9183** | **$177.837B** reported in the first half, roughly **$113.6B** for Q3 and **$127B** for Q4 give about **$419B** before adjustments. `e60` said second-half **Data Center compute** would be **20% above consensus**. The judge added **$27–28B** to its own run rate, giving **$446B** and crossing $431.2B. The answer did not show the compute base or what upside the run rate already assumed. The risk is stacking two adjustments to the same sales. The precise amount of overlap is unknown. |\n\nI also traced filing on the lead case. Extraction call **1736** quoted the SemiAnalysis claim and assigned it to v9 alone. The saved link kept that assignment. It was visible to v9 on both September 19 and 20, but v14’s September 20 request showed no equivalent SemiAnalysis 20% claim. Extraction call **8011** put the historical 3.1% beat under v14. The two requests also had no dependency joining them. The last step accepted each answer because its citations were legal and its chosen band was in range. [Extraction and filing](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/extraction.py:468), [Judgement checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:259)\n\n## Counts and checks\n\nI used the three finished stores, July 1 through September 20, opened with `mode=ro`. The numeric population is the 54 questions stored with USD or percent units. The text comparison below normalizes units, recognizes ranges, and matches figures within 0.5%. It looks inside the actual request’s evidence block and excludes its ladder, themes, and previous answer.\n\n| What I counted | Old-1 | Old-2 | Old-3 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Saved numeric judgements | 840 | 840 | 749 |\n| First same-unit figure in the answer absent from the evidence shown | 387 / 839 | 447 / 839 | 319 / 749 |\n| Request said some evidence in force was hidden | 611 | 595 | 495 |\n| Earlier reference census flag, among answers with a predecessor | 15 / 821 | 29 / 823 | 0 / 731 |\n| Explicitly used `$25B + $18B ≈ $43B` as a base or comparison, among equity-commitment answers | 6 / 35 | 17 / 37 | 25 / 37 |\n| Used the exact `730 ÷ 800 × 1,300` proxy, among four-company 2027 capex answers | 0 / 46 | 18 / 43 | 0 / 44 |\n\n- **New figures are common.** The first-figure comparison flagged **1,153 of 2,427** readable answers. It correctly found $146B, $53B, and $1.19T missing from their evidence blocks. It also flagged v14’s legitimate calculated total of $419B. This counts figures the judge had to derive or carry. It cannot tell whether the derivation is sound, or whether a matching dollar amount refers to the same thing.\n- **The contradiction predates September 20.** I joined total and Data Center answers by draw and publication day and read the paired outcomes after the August 26 earnings release. I confirmed a stated Q4 segment figure above the stated whole-company figure on **at least 13 of 26** paired days in old-1 and **at least 1 of 26** in old-2. I found no explicit breach in the **25** old-3 pairs. These are lower bounds because some answers left the second-half adjustment unallocated. Old-2 already had **20 consecutive paired days, August 26–September 14**, with the full-year Data Center estimate above total revenue. On **17 of 18** paired days across the three draws from September 15–20, the Data Center calculation used the $108B Q3 company guide while the total-revenue answer used an adjusted Q3 estimate. Different assumptions can be defensible, but the product published them without reconciliation.\n- **The quarter can be whatever the annual forecast needs.** I confirmed at least **six explicit** old-1 Data Center answers that built a second-half figure first, then backed out Q4 using Q3. For example, call **6413** took Morningstar’s $361B annual estimate, subtracted $164.25B of first-half actuals, applied the 20% uplift, and subtracted $99.9B for Q3. The result was **$136.2B** for Data Center Q4 versus **$128.8B** for the company. A residual can be valid. This one depended on assigning SemiAnalysis’s uplift to a baseline the source did not identify. The September 20 $146B answer is arithmetically consistent with a residual, so I kept it out of the explicit count.\n- **The repeated formulas are narrow counts.** I checked the $43B matches in the saved forecast, comparison, or reasons for an actual addition, including calls **4938**, **8734**, and **9175**. The first clear instances were August 26. I checked the capex formula against call **4915**, which introduced it on August 26, and **8595**, which still used it on September 19. The other two draws used different adjustments to the same broad capex input, so their zeroes mean zero uses of this formula.\n- **The self-made reference is real.** I reproduced the earlier **44 of 2,375** flags. That instrument checks for carry-forward wording and an amount missing from the rest of the request. I separately traced old-2 v9’s **$414B** through all six September 15–20 answers. On September 14 it was the system’s forecast, built from $164.25B plus $249.6B. On September 15 it became the comparison, and by September 18 it was called market consensus. The 44 are candidates for this mechanism. The wording screen can catch disclosed arithmetic and miss a copied value that drops the carry-forward wording. [Reference census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/self-reference/reference_census.py)\n- **The production check let the numbers through.** I ran it in memory on six saved answers. All six passed with their original text, and all six still passed after I substituted a contradictory numeric forecast or an unsupported market reference. All six refused a citation absent from the request. Across the three stores there were **zero dependencies in 139 questions** and **zero dependency citations in 4,932 judgements**. [Judgement checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:259)\n\n## Ranked design causes\n\n1. **Judging publishes numeric conclusions as prose with an answer-wide citation list.** This is the broadest cause. It permits all three mechanisms in **17 of the 24** earlier double-rejected answers, including **12 of the 14** from the old draws in that selected set. These are shares of the reviewed set, not error rates for production. Judging-v7 already calls for scope and consistency. It also asks for a market comparison in every answer, says fresh evidence removes guidance alone as a reason to stay, and asks the judge to extrapolate and size effects. The output gives code no way to tell a source number from an assumption, or an estimated increment from a second count of expected sales. The prompt creates pressure to produce a number. The answer contract turns that number into a published claim. [Judging prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt:3), [Rejected answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/rejected-answers.md)\n2. **Matching and writing leave related questions to calculate shared quantities independently.** This explains the confirmed cross-question breaches. Jev can recognize a part and a whole when comparing questions, but the v118 path returns empty dependency lists. The writer can store edges, but receives none here. Question keys identify the question, while evidence records carry quotes, summaries, and timing text. There is no shared definition of a numeric input or a check that a quarter used on one card agrees with the same quarter used elsewhere. **The unit of publication can stay one question. The shared calculation needs a wider unit.** [Jev matching](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:304), [Evidence writing](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:29)\n3. **Filing and evidence selection make the first two problems easier to trigger.** Old-2 v9 and v14 shared **106 stored evidence IDs** out of **1,287** and **1,329**, respectively. Their September 20 requests shared **five** IDs and cited **zero** in common. Narrowest filing explains why some inputs went to different questions. With zero dependencies, the code that removes broader links along parent edges could not have removed them in these runs. D198 then limits which of each question’s own evidence is shown. It tells the model both that the previous answer carries omitted material and that the answer is for comparison only. That helps explain why old assumptions persist. It cannot explain the decisive September 20 jump by itself because the SemiAnalysis quote was already visible the day before. [Narrowest filing](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/extraction.py:55), [Evidence selection](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:45)\n4. **Opening’s bands amplify a bad calculation. Pull mode preserves it.** The whole-company top band started at **$431.2B** in old-1 and old-3 and **$450B** in old-2. Opening therefore changes when the same dollar error changes the displayed answer. D186 would improve how those bands relate to guidance. It would leave the $146B/$127B contradiction and the incompatible capex ratio intact. Pull mode simply freezes the last answer on the due day. D191’s shared-quantity concept helps identify an aggregate question. The existing matching helper already handles some such identities, while the figures used *inside* the answer remain unchecked. [Views PRD](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/06-views.md:76), [Shared-quantity matching](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:111), [Pull mode](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/pull.py:11)\n\n## The fix I would choose and the test that could refute it\n\n**Give each published number a calculation a reader can follow, and reuse the same inputs across answers that share them.**\n\n- **Extraction and writing** would keep the quote and identify any figure used in a calculation by its source, company or population, measure, period, units, and what kind of number it is, such as a reported result, guidance, or an estimate. Keep one evidence record. Related forecasts can refer to the same numeric input without copying the article into every question.\n- **Matching** would identify specific relationships such as *this segment belongs to this total*, *these estimates use the same prior-year base*, or *the relationship is unresolved*. The model can interpret ambiguous source language. Code can verify declared identities and apply known accounting relationships. Identical dollar values alone establish nothing.\n- **Judging and publication** would use the same dated inputs for a related group of forecasts. Each answer would identify its external comparison, if available, and show the inputs, calculations, and assumptions that drive its forecast. Code would recompute the math and check known relationships, including part versus whole and the same growth rate applied to the same base. The model would decide which signals matter, what to discount, and when the evidence supports only a broad range or a conditional scenario. A guess about overlap or a haircut could still be discussed. It would stay visibly an assumption and could not pass for a sourced increment. A failed reconciliation would be visible as unresolved rather than publishing two incompatible updates together.\n\nThis matches the frozen gold’s reasoning. **q02** derives a segment comparison from an outside total and a sourced mix, then uses **q01**’s total forecast to reason about the segment. **q22** reuses q01’s FY2027 base for FY2028. **q19** discounts the CPU target using the selling window and what the target would require in units. **q01** considers the CPU target as an upside driver without assigning it a fixed increment. Those are legitimate analyst inferences. The proposed checks preserve them while making their provenance and assumptions visible. [Frozen judgement gold](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json), [Frozen judgement standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md:18)\n\n**Cheapest refuting test, $0.** Reconstruct the published calculations by hand from the archived requests above, using the same proposed rules on the frozen q02, q19, and q22 examples as controls. The decisive check is already visible: with Data Center at $164B through Q2 and about $100B in Q3, a company-wide Q4 of $127B caps that particular annual segment calculation at **about $391B**. To reach $410B, the analyst must revise a stated input or show a different scenario. Stop the design if it admits the incompatible pair, treats unknown overlap as an additive fact, or lets an unsupported adjustment cross a boundary simply by labelling it an assumption. Also stop if it rejects the gold’s valid share, base-year, or execution-risk reasoning. This test establishes whether the representation can express and check the right distinctions. It does not measure how often a future model would use it correctly.\n\n## Where I disagree with earlier findings\n\n- **The exact September 20 contradiction occurred in two draws.** Old-1 was **$126B versus $112.2B**, and old-2 was **$146B versus $127B**. Old-3’s roughly **$375B segment versus $446B total** implies an 84% annual share and different assumptions. That pair alone does not establish a part exceeding its whole. [Council Q](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/RESULT.md)\n- **Leaving the comparison band is one symptom.** The $53B commitments answer stayed in the band named by its questionable $70B comparison. The $146B Q4 answer returned to the band of its self-made $414B comparison. A rule that only checks moves away from the comparison would miss both. [Rejection census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/CENSUS.md)\n- **Linking helped the pair agree, while leaving the arithmetic problem open.** In the saved pilot, linked Data Center call **9024** used **92.5% × $419B ≈ $388B**, which reconciles with company revenue. Linked total-revenue call **8756** added about **$19.5B** of CPU sales to a **$387B** run rate without establishing what that base already included. The same judge treated the CPU estimate as included on July 20. D721 is right to describe the four additions as unsupported increments with unresolved overlap. A link passed along a conclusion. It did not pass along the assumptions needed to use it safely, and the interrupted control left the pilot inconclusive. [Pilot result](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md), [D721](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10859)\n\nThe investigation was read-only and used no model calls. The counts measure saved behaviour and explicit failure patterns. They cannot establish the overall rate of unsupported forecasts."}}
